// Cardnull OS 2 — the seed kernel: this file is its main
// body.
//
// The kernel is built as several files, never one big one
// — Tyler's standing rule, kept from the first Cardnull OS,
// whose kernel stood in twenty files. The libraries join
// this body as preludes at build (build.sh names them
// all); each holds pure functions — values in as arguments,
// results back — and every piece of state lives here, in
// the main body, because on bare metal a function that
// reads or assigns the program's shared variables is given
// a broken value (the README's notes for builders tell it
// in full). The files:
//
//   src/screenlib.nifla  the screen library: drawing on
//                        the text screen's own memory
//   src/clock.nifla      the clock's face, and the
//                        reckoning behind the time choice
//   src/bootmods.nifla   the hands that read what the
//                        loader brought — the modules
//                        that ride the disc
//   src/featherdoor.nifla  the feather door: the card's check,
//                        the doorstep, the call, the
//                        plain-words refusals
//   src/commands.nifla   deciding what a typed word is
//                        answered with
//   src/usbkey.nifla     ears on the USB road: a UHCI
//                        host and a HID boot keyboard,
//                        its estate in one desk of bytes
//   src/xhcikey.nifla    ears on the modern USB road: an
//                        xHCI host and a HID boot
//                        keyboard, its estate in one
//                        desk of bytes (Stone 32)
//   src/rtclock.nifla    the real clock: reading the
//                        machine's own CMOS clock, and
//                        reckoning dates from it
//   src/diskkeep.nifla   what the system remembers on a
//                        hard disk: the card, its reading,
//                        and the setup clearing
//   src/font.nifla       the letters of the face of
//                        light: the hand-drawn bitmap font
//   src/pixface.nifla    the face of light: the picture
//                        screen's desk, palette, and
//                        drawing
//   src/mouse.nifla      the pointer: the PS/2 mouse's
//                        waking and its packets, gathered
//                        into a desk this body hands it
//   src/speaker.nifla    the voice: the PC speaker's
//                        notes — the welcome chime and
//                        the refusal's low note — timed
//                        off the timer's count (Stone 18)
//   src/rest.nifla       the resting: the ACPI power
//                        doors, the reset doors, and the
//                        final halt (Stone 25)
//   src/verses.nifla     the kept set of verses the
//                        verse window shows, in the
//                        World English Bible's wording,
//                        and the day's reckoning (Stone 29)
//   src/helpwords.nifla  the plain words the help
//                        window shows, in five short
//                        pages (Stone 31)
//   src/bibletext.nifla  the book of Jonah, whole, in
//                        the World English Bible's
//                        wording, as the Bible reading
//                        window shows it (Stone 41)
//   src/cardnull.nifla   this main body: the boot
//                        sequence, the first-breath lift,
//                        the state, the face, the typing
//                        line, and the loop that calls
//                        the libraries
//
// (The welcome feather, src/welcome-feather.nifla, is a
// separate program that visits through the door; it is no
// part of the kernel.)
//
// Stone 1: first boot — the greeting and the memory figure.
// Stone 2: ears — the keyboard is heard; a typing line and
//          plain-words commands.
// Stone 3: a face — the desktop. A top bar carries the name
//          and a live clock; a welcome panel sits in the
//          middle; answers have their own region of the
//          screen and scroll there; the typing line still
//          works at the bottom. The face is drawn with the
//          system's own screen library (screenlib.nifla,
//          joined to this program as a prelude at build).
// Stone 4: the clock's time choice — the choice rides the
//          system disc itself, in a small settings module
//          the loader brings alongside the system, and the
//          words eastern and utc change it for the session.
//          A disc carrying no choice starts on Eastern
//          time, this system's home. (First proven kept on
//          a disk the same morning; Tyler's word that the
//          system ships as one disc and nothing else
//          re-pointed it here the same day — the README's
//          Stone 4 section tells it in full.)
// Stone 5: first feather — the word 'feather' receives the
//          welcome feather through the feather door: the feather
//          rides the disc as a module too, lifted into the
//          system's keeping at boot, checked against its
//          card (which rides as its own small module), run
//          to draw its own panel inside the desktop, and
//          returned from, with the system carrying on.
// Stone 6: ears on the USB road — a USB keyboard wakes on
//          the UHCI companion road and feeds the same
//          typing line as the PS/2 road (usbkey.nifla).
// Stone 7: a true clock — the machine's own clock is read
//          at boot (rtclock.nifla), and the bar shows the
//          real time and the date in plain words, the
//          disc's time choice applied on top.
// Stone 8: remembers for real — a fitted hard disk is
//          offered its keeping behind a typed gate
//          ('setup', then 'yes'), and thereafter holds the
//          time choice in preference to the disc's.
// Stone 9: a face with light — when the loader grants the
//          picture screen the kernel asks for, the desktop
//          is drawn in pixels (pixface.nifla, font.nifla),
//          the feather's visit composited from the cells it
//          paints in the host's keeping; when none is
//          granted, the text face below carries the
//          desktop and says so in plain words. The drawn
//          face is also what the EFI door needed: the disc
//          now boots under UEFI firmware too.
// Stone 12: real typing — the shift keys and Caps Lock
//          speak on both roads, so capitals, the figures,
//          and their marks can be typed; and a typed line
//          may hold more than one word: its first word is
//          its command word, and the rest of the line is
//          kept whole as handed text (linerest) for the
//          words that want it.
// Stone 13: a pointer — a PS/2 mouse is woken on the
//          face of light, an arrow follows it (kept
//          harmless by a save block), and a press of its
//          first button on the feather's panel receives the
//          feather, as the word 'feather' does.
// Stone 14: two windows — on the face of light the
//          welcome panel and the feather's panel stand as
//          framed windows, each with a plain title bar
//          and a close mark; the Tab key or a press of
//          the pointer brings one forward, the Escape
//          key or the close mark closes the forward one
//          back to the desktop, and the words 'welcome'
//          and 'feather' open them again. The windows'
//          standing is kept in the pixel face's desk
//          (see pixface.nifla's ledger), and the painting
//          is paintwindows below. The text face keeps
//          its one panel, as it always has.
// Stone 15: notes that stay — the word 'note' keeps the
//          rest of its line as a note on a page of eight,
//          and 'notes' shows the page. When a keeping
//          disk stands ready the page is written to it
//          (sectors 64 onward, beyond the settings card
//          and the setup clearing — diskkeep.nifla) and
//          read back at boot, so the notes stay; with no
//          keeping disk they live for the session only,
//          and the answers say so in plain words.
// Stone 19: the files window — a third window on the
//          face of light, opened by the word 'files'. It
//          lists exactly what the fitted disk's keeping
//          holds: the notes page (the CNN3 store, sectors
//          64 onward) is all the disk honestly holds, so
//          the window is a list of those notes and no
//          invented files. Picking an entry — a press of
//          the pointer on its row, or the up and down
//          arrow keys while the window stands forward —
//          shows that note's whole words in the window.
//          With no disk fitted it says so plainly and
//          that nothing is saved; Tab cycles through it
//          with the other windows, and Escape closes it.
// Stone 20: the settings window — a fourth window on the
//          face of light, opened by the word 'settings'.
//          Two choices are set there by clicking: the time
//          zone (Eastern or the world's standard time) and
//          whether the welcome chime sounds. The window
//          shows the choices as they truly stand — the
//          offset the bar's clock reckons by, and whether
//          the chime is on. When a keeping disk stands
//          ready, a click writes both choices through to
//          its settings card (sector 8, the chime in the
//          card's fourth byte — diskkeep.nifla), so they
//          survive a reboot; with no keeping disk the
//          window says plainly that the choices last only
//          for this sitting, exactly as the typed words
//          have always said. The typed words 'eastern',
//          'utc', and 'chime' keep working as before, and
//          the window's standing follows them. The chime
//          standing governs the welcome at the desktop's
//          rising and the word 'chime'; the refusal's low
//          note is not the chime and sounds as it always
//          has.
// Stone 21: the editor window — a fifth window on the
//          face of light, opened by the word 'editor'.
//          Here a note is written and changed with the
//          desktop's own typing (the shift keys and Caps
//          Lock speak as they do on the typing line) and
//          kept to the very same CNN3 note store the word
//          'note' keeps to — the same page in this body's
//          keeping, written through by the same notesave
//          — so a note kept here stands in the files
//          window and in the word 'notes' reckoning, and
//          theirs stand here. The window stands in two
//          ways: a picking way, listing the new-note entry
//          and every kept note (the arrow keys or a press
//          of the pointer pick, as in the files window),
//          and a writing way, entered by Enter on the
//          pick, where the words are typed into a draft
//          and Enter keeps them back to the same slot —
//          a changed note keeps its place on the page. A
//          note holds forty-three letters at most — the
//          typing line's own measure, the measure the
//          word 'note' has always kept by — and the
//          writing way says so in plain words rather than
//          letting the words run past it in silence.
//          With no keeping disk the note is kept only for
//          the sitting, and the window says so plainly,
//          as the answers have always said.
// Stone 22: window dragging — a window is moved by
//          dragging its title bar with the pointer: a
//          press on the bar (not on its close mark) takes
//          hold, the window follows the pointer while the
//          button stands held, and letting go sets it
//          down, clamped whole onto the desktop. Every
//          window's place is read through one set of
//          measures (winleft and its kin, below) standing
//          on the same reckonings the painters use, so
//          after a drag the painting, the close marks,
//          and every content press still reckon from the
//          window's one place. A dragged place lasts for
//          the sitting; nothing keeps it past it.
// Stone 23: the bar's menu — a press of the pointer on
//          the bar opens a small menu of plain words just
//          under the bar, at the desktop's left: the
//          five windows' names, one to a row — Welcome,
//          Feather, Files, Settings, Editor, the names
//          the desktop already gives them. A press on a
//          choice opens that window (or brings it
//          forward), exactly as the choice's typed word
//          does — the feather's choice asks for the
//          feather, as the word 'feather' does — and a
//          press anywhere else, or the Escape key, puts
//          the menu away without opening anything. The
//          menu's place is reckoned once (menuleft and
//          its kin, below) and read by its painter and
//          the pointer's pressing alike, the discipline
//          the windows have kept since Stone 14; its
//          standing lives in the pixel face's desk at
//          268. The typed words keep working exactly as
//          before, the bar's clock keeps telling time,
//          and a press that meets the menu is never a
//          title-bar drag.
// Stone 24: a paint feather — a second feather rides
//          the disc (paint.fea, its own module with its
//          own card), told apart from the welcome
//          feather by the name on its card and received
//          through the same door, checked first, exactly
//          as the welcome feather is. The word 'paint'
//          opens a sixth window, the paint window, and
//          asks for the feather's opening visit: the
//          feather paints its whole window — a row of
//          color swatches in plain reach, the chosen
//          color named in words, a Clear word, and an
//          empty canvas — into a second block of cells
//          the host holds and shows 1:1, as the feather
//          window shows its feather's cells. A feather
//          owns no machine: the compiler refuses it the
//          timer, the keyboard, and the mouse, so the
//          pointer cannot speak to it directly. What
//          the contract honestly carries is the desk:
//          one number in, the painted cells shared, one
//          number back. So drawing is a conversation of
//          visits: while the paint window stands open,
//          a press of the pointer inside its shown cells
//          is carried across the desk as another visit —
//          gift 1000 plus the press's row times 100 plus
//          its column, in the cells' own reckoning —
//          and the feather itself reckons what the press
//          means (a swatch chooses its color, the Clear
//          word empties the canvas, a canvas cell takes
//          the chosen color) and paints it, and returns.
//          The host is a courier of presses and a keeper
//          of cells; every mark in the canvas is the
//          feather's own. The window's standing lives in
//          the desk at 272 to 284, its place with the
//          other windows' (it drags by its title bar,
//          Tab reaches it, Escape and its close mark
//          close it), and the bar's menu keeps its five
//          choices — 'paint' is a typed word, as the
//          feather's own word has always been a word.
//          (Stone 25 grows the menu by a sixth choice,
//          Rest, below — the next paragraph tells it.)
// Stone 25: a kind shutdown, with restart — the word
//          'rest' offers the machine's resting, and so
//          does a sixth choice in the bar's menu, Rest
//          (the menu is six rows high now; Stone 23's
//          five window choices stand above it, unchanged).
//          Choosing Rest offers two plain-words choices:
//          on the face of light a small panel stands at
//          the desktop's middle — Shut down, Restart,
//          and Stay, which simply puts the panel away —
//          and on the text face the choices are offered
//          in words, for the next typed line to name.
//          The words 'shutdown' and 'restart' name the
//          two choices directly, too. Either choice first
//          flushes the disk, honestly: the system's own
//          writings are never cached — every keeping is
//          a completed writing when it is made — so the
//          flush writes the kept settings card and the
//          notes page through once more, whole, and then
//          sends the drive its own flush word, so what
//          the drive itself holds is committed too
//          (diskkeep.nifla's ataflush). Shut down then
//          says a short, kind goodbye and tries the ACPI
//          power doors (rest.nifla); if the machine stays
//          on, plain words say it is now safe to turn it
//          off, and the machine halts where it stands.
//          Restart flushes the same way and resets the
//          machine through the keyboard controller's
//          reset word, then the reset control door — the
//          machine boots again fresh, and what was kept
//          before the rest is there when it rises, read
//          back from the disk as at every boot.
// Stone 26: paint keeps its pictures — the paint
//          feather's drawing no longer lives only for
//          the sitting. The persistence is the HOST's,
//          honestly: a feather is refused the disk by
//          the compiler, and needs none of it — the host
//          already holds the painted cells between
//          visits (that holding is the canvas, Stone
//          24), so after every press visit that stands,
//          when a disk is keeping things, the host writes
//          the feather's painted region through to the
//          disk's picture store (sectors 80 onward,
//          beyond the notes page — diskkeep.nifla's
//          picsave), verbatim, with the feather's
//          chosen-color figure beside it. At boot a
//          keeping disk's picture is read back (picload)
//          into this body's keeping, and the paint
//          window's opening visit — the feather painting
//          its fresh window, checked at the door as
//          always — is followed by the host laying the
//          kept region back into the cells over it, so
//          the picture stands again with its colors as
//          drawn and its chosen color standing. The
//          feather itself is unchanged, and every visit
//          still runs the door's full reckoning first.
//          The files window lists the kept picture
//          beside the notes ("A picture you painted"),
//          and picking it — a press on its row, or Enter
//          while it stands picked — opens it again in
//          the paint window, the one opening doing above.
//          A picture stands kept while any canvas cell
//          stands painted; clearing the canvas clear
//          through lets the keeping go. With no keeping
//          disk, painting is exactly as Stone 24 left
//          it: for the sitting only.
// Stone 27: letting a note go — a kept note can be
//          removed now, in plain words, from the files
//          window and from the editor window. In the
//          files window, while a note stands picked,
//          "Let this note go" stands at the window's
//          foot beside the picked note's label; a press
//          on those words, or Enter while the note
//          stands picked (the window's own acting key,
//          as Enter on the picked picture opens it),
//          asks first, on the window itself: "Let this
//          note go for good?", with the note's words
//          shown, and two choices — "Yes, let it go"
//          and "Keep it". In the editor window the same
//          words stand while a note stands picked in
//          its picking way (at its foot, beside the
//          keeping word) and while a note stands open
//          in its writing way (below the keep word); a
//          press on them asks the same asking there.
//          The asking stands with "Keep it" picked, so
//          Enter alone never lets a note go: the yes
//          must be chosen — by the arrow keys and Enter,
//          or by a press on its row — and only the yes
//          removes. "Keep it", closing the window, and
//          Escape (which puts the asking away first)
//          all leave the note exactly as it was. The
//          removal itself goes through the very store
//          path the editor's keeping uses: the notes
//          after the let-go one move up a place in the
//          page in this body's keeping, the place it
//          leaves is wiped, and the page is written
//          through by the same notesave — once with the
//          old count, so the wiped place's sector is
//          wiped on the disk too, and once with the new
//          count, so the header and the standing notes
//          are the disk's truth — and a note kept only
//          for the sitting is let go the same way, in
//          the page alone. The files window now lists
//          a sitting's notes too, under their honest
//          heading, so they can be picked and let go
//          there as the editor has always shown them.
//          The windows' asking standings live in the
//          desk at 300 to 316 (pixface.nifla's ledger).
// Stone 28: large print — the settings window gains a
//          third choice, the size of the desktop's
//          words: "Usual size" or "Large", under the
//          plain label "Words:", set by clicking like
//          the other choices. Large print is a scale
//          reckoning in the face's own text path, not a
//          second font: the letter cell's size stands in
//          the desk at 320 (sixteen pixels at the usual
//          size, twenty-four at large), pixbegin reckons
//          the face's grid from it, pixglyph draws the
//          one hand-drawn font at twice or three times
//          size by it, and every painter and every
//          pointer reckoning reads that one measure, so
//          painter and pointer agree at either size.
//          The feather windows' carried cells are the
//          one exception, by their own standing rule:
//          a feather's cells are carried 1:1, never
//          scaled (Stone 14), so they stand at sixteen
//          pixels inside their windows at either size,
//          while the windows' frames and titles grow.
//          The choice is kept on the settings card
//          exactly as Stone 20's chime byte is kept: a
//          new byte at the card's end (sector 8, its
//          fifth byte): 1 usual size, 2 large, and 0
//          when no choice was ever kept there, so a card
//          laid before this stone keeps the usual size.
//          It is read at boot in preference order like
//          the other choices; with no keeping disk it
//          lasts for the sitting, and the window says so
//          the way it already does for the others.
//          Switching size re-lays the windows at their
//          first places on the new grid and repaints the
//          desktop whole at once. The text face stays as
//          it is: large print lives on the face of light
//          only, as the windows do.
// Stone 29: a verse window — a seventh window on the
//          face of light, opened by the word 'verse' and
//          by a new choice, Verse, in the bar's menu (the
//          menu's sixth window choice; Rest stands under
//          it now, seven rows in all). It shows one verse
//          from a kept set of twelve (verses.nifla), in
//          the World English Bible's wording exactly,
//          with its reference named plainly under it.
//          Which verse stands is reckoned when the
//          window is opened, from the date the desktop
//          itself shows — the true clock's day in the
//          person's chosen time (verseday in
//          verses.nifla) — taken around the kept set, so
//          a new day brings a new verse; when no true
//          clock reading could be had, the first verse
//          stands. Nothing is kept anywhere about which
//          verse was seen. The window is a window like
//          the others: it drags by its title bar, Tab
//          reaches it, Escape and its close mark close
//          it, and its words wrap at its own width, so
//          the whole verse stands at either word size.
//          Its standing lives in the desk at 324 to 336.
// Stone 30: a calculator feather — a third feather rides
//          the disc (calc.fea, its own module with its
//          own card), told apart from its sisters by the
//          name on its card (calcmark in featherdoor.nifla)
//          and received through the same door, checked
//          first on every visit, exactly as they are. The
//          word 'calculator' opens an eighth window, the
//          calculator window, and asks for the feather's
//          opening visit: the feather paints its whole
//          window — an invitation, the sum's line standing
//          at 0, and its keys, the figures and the four
//          workings named in plain words (Add, Take away,
//          Times, Share out, with Equals and Clear) — into
//          a third block of cells the host holds and shows
//          1:1, as the paint window shows its feather's.
//          The contract is Stone 24's, unchanged: a feather
//          owns no machine, so while the calculator window
//          stands open, a press of the pointer inside its
//          shown cells is carried across the desk as
//          another checked visit — gift 1000 plus the
//          press's row times 100 plus its column — and the
//          feather itself reckons what the press means and
//          keeps its reckoning in the cells between visits.
//          The host is a courier of presses and a keeper of
//          cells; every figure on the sum's line is the
//          feather's own. The window's standing lives in
//          the desk at 340 to 356, its place with the other
//          windows' (it drags by its title bar, Tab reaches
//          it, Escape and its close mark close it), and the
//          bar's menu grows by an eighth choice, Calculator
//          — Rest stands under it now, eight rows in all.
// Stone 31: a help window — a ninth window on the face
//          of light, opened by the word 'help' and by a
//          new choice, Help, in the bar's menu (the menu's
//          eighth window choice; Rest stands under it now,
//          nine rows in all). It tells, in plain words a
//          neighbor could follow, how Cardnull works:
//          what each window is, what each typed word does,
//          how to move windows, open the menu, and rest
//          the machine. The words stand in their own
//          library (helpwords.nifla) as five short pages
//          of eleven lines, each line at most thirty-six
//          letters — the window's inner width at the
//          large print size — so no line is cut at either
//          size. The pages are turned by a press on the
//          window's foot (its plain word More, or First
//          page on the last page) and by the up and down
//          arrow keys while the window stands forward.
//          The window is a window like the others: it
//          drags by its title bar, Tab reaches it, Escape
//          and its close mark close it. Its standing lives
//          in the desk at 360 to 372.
// Stone 32: ears on the modern USB road — a second USB
//          host driver (xhcikey.nifla) speaks to an xHCI
//          controller, the USB 3 arrangement, and wakes a
//          keyboard standing on it, feeding the same
//          typing line through the same key numbering and
//          the same shared shift standing as every road.
//          Both USB roads may stand ready; the loop asks
//          the UHCI road first, so when both carry a
//          keyboard the UHCI road is the one heard first
//          and the modern road is heard when it is quiet.
//          Neither road disables the other, and neither
//          disables the PS/2 road.
// Stone 33: the glass face — the desktop's look is
//          remade in the style of a glass desktop, drawn
//          entirely by Cardnull's own code with Cardnull's
//          own art (pixface.nifla's glass drawing): the
//          bar stands at the screen's FOOT, two rows tall,
//          glassy with a sheen across its upper part, and
//          a round start button at its left carries the
//          stained-glass cardinal, pixel-drawn in the
//          drawing library (birdrow there) from the
//          Cardnull logo's bird — crest, mask, gold wing,
//          orange beak, long tail — in stained-glass
//          colors with dark lead between. A press on the
//          button (the disc itself, tried as a disc) opens
//          the bar's menu, which rises from the button:
//          its foot on the bar's top edge, its nine choices
//          and their order unchanged; a press elsewhere on
//          the bar opens nothing. The windows wear the
//          glass too: rounded corners, a sheen across the
//          title bar with the blue-green tint at its foot,
//          and a soft shadow along the right edge and
//          under the foot — the shadow clipped at the
//          bar's top, and every wash (a window closed, a
//          drag's vacated ground, the rest panel) wiping
//          the shadow's reach with the frame's, so the
//          ground stands whole and the pointer's
//          save-and-restore discipline is untouched.
//          Windows stand above the bar now: their first
//          places and the drag's clamping reckon the
//          desktop as the grid less the bar's two rows,
//          and their tops may stand at the screen's very
//          top. The typing line stands just above the bar.
//          Everything underneath works as it did. The
//          text face keeps its own face, as it always has
//          — the glass lives on the face of light only.
// Stone 34: the region repaint — the first of the
//          four glitches Tyler witnessed in UTM (Nov
//          16, 2026) answered at its root: "when i move
//          windows it flickers the windows." The old
//          road repainted the WHOLE desktop on every
//          step of a drag — every closed window washed,
//          every open window redrawn whole, straight
//          into the picture screen — so the eye saw
//          windows that had nothing to do with the move
//          wash and redraw many times a second; that
//          shimmer was the flicker. A drag now repaints
//          only the road the window travelled — the
//          ground it left and the ground it took, as one
//          stretch of cells — and, within the road, only
//          the windows whose places touch it, painted
//          in the standing order (closed fellows washed
//          in window order, open fellows drawn in window
//          order, the forward window last), so the
//          road's final pixels are exactly what the
//          whole-desktop repaint would have laid there,
//          and a window outside the road is never washed
//          or redrawn at all. The reckoning stands in
//          paintregion, beside paintwindows; the road
//          and the window to paint travel through the
//          desk at 384 to 403 (the desk stands 404
//          bytes now), the machine's registers being
//          already full with a call's three blocks.
//          While the start menu or the rest panel
//          stands open — both paint over everything in
//          their own words — the whole-desktop repaint
//          keeps its say. The bench reckons the change
//          as well as sees it: a lone window's six-step
//          drag cost 3,685 of the runtime's loop
//          passes, against 11,765 measured for the old
//          road's drag.
// Stone 35: the region road carried to the feather
//          visits — the second glitch: "when i paint
//          with multiple windows, all the windows
//          flicker." A feather visit was bracketed by
//          two whole-desktop repaints, one as the
//          press landed and one as the visit was
//          received, so every bystander window was
//          washed and redrawn twice per press. The
//          press's repaint and the receiving's now
//          lay Stone 34's road over the visited
//          window alone — widened to the window it
//          took the forward place from, whose title
//          bar the coming-forward changes, and to the
//          files window when a disk is keeping the
//          picture, whose list the keeping can
//          change. On a full desktop the bench
//          reckons a calculator press at 5,201-5,413
//          loop passes against the old road's
//          5,961-6,966, and a canvas press at
//          12,809-12,873 against 13,707-14,706 — the
//          saving is the bystander repaints that no
//          longer happen; the visit itself remains.
// Stone 38: the desktop at the rising — the machine
//          wakes to the desktop itself, as Tyler
//          steered it (Nov 18, 2026): "make a real
//          desktop gui like windows 7 or Mac OS for
//          cardnull instead of being in shell at
//          start up." The console no longer stands
//          on the desktop's ground below the windows;
//          it lives in the terminal window, the tenth
//          window, which stands open at the rising
//          with its prompt at its foot, and typing
//          raises it again whenever it has been
//          closed, as the first keystroke always
//          reached the typing line. Down the
//          desktop's left edge stand eight icons —
//          Files, Editor, Paint, Calculator, Verse,
//          Help, Settings, Terminal, in the order
//          Tyler named them — a small picture and the
//          window's own plain name, and a press on
//          an icon opens its window, as the start
//          menu's choices and the typed words do.
//          The words themselves are unchanged: what
//          a person types in the terminal is answered
//          in the terminal, and the words that open
//          windows open them still.
// Stone 39: the bar shows the open windows — GUI
//          completeness II, in the ladder planned
//          November 18, 2026. The bottom bar carries
//          one plain-words presence chip to each of
//          the eight windows a person opens and
//          closes — Files, Editor, Paint, Calculator,
//          Verse, Help, Settings, Terminal, the names
//          the desktop already uses, in the icons'
//          order — the front window's chip white on
//          the bar's blue, a window behind white on
//          light gray, as their title bars stand. The
//          chips are laid along the bar's upper row
//          and, when it fills (at large print, whose
//          bar is narrower), on its lower row's
//          middle ground, clear of the bar's name and
//          its clock; they are painted at every whole
//          repaint and after the region repaints that
//          move the forward place, so they track
//          every opening, closing, and coming-
//          forward. A press on a chip raises its
//          window by Stones 34 and 35's region road.
//          No chip minimizes, and no chip opens: the
//          icons, the menu, and the words remain the
//          openers. The welcome and feather windows
//          keep no chip — they are the desktop's own
//          first furniture, standing open from the
//          rising under their title bars.
// Stone 40: names in the files window — a kept note
//          or the kept picture can be given a name
//          now, and a kept note can be copied under a
//          new name, in plain words on the files
//          window itself, beside Stone 27's letting
//          go. A note's name is kept in its own slot's
//          tail (diskkeep.nifla's heading tells the
//          shape: the length at the slot's forty-fifth
//          byte, nineteen letters at most), and the
//          picture's in its header's spare bytes
//          (forty-three at most); a thing with no name
//          stands under what it always stood under —
//          a note under its own words, the picture
//          under "A picture you painted" — and the
//          files window's list shows the name where
//          one stands. While a note stands picked,
//          "Rename" and "Copy" stand at the window's
//          foot beside "Let this note go"; while the
//          picture stands picked, "Rename" stands
//          there. A press on one opens the naming way
//          in the list's place: the thing named is
//          shown whole — a note with its words under
//          the naming, so what a rename leaves alone
//          stands in sight — the keeper types the new
//          name into the window (the desktop's own
//          typing, as the editor's draft is typed),
//          and Enter keeps it. A name that already
//          stands on the list is refused in plain
//          words in the window, and nothing is
//          changed; an empty name is refused the
//          same way; Escape puts the naming away and
//          leaves everything as it was. A copy is a
//          real second keeping: the note's whole slot
//          is laid into the page's next free place
//          under the new name and written through by
//          the same notesave, so it stands on its own
//          sector — letting the first go leaves the
//          copy whole, and changing one never touches
//          the other. A rename is written through the
//          same store paths (notesave for a note,
//          picsave for the picture), so a name stays
//          across a restart as the things themselves
//          do. The naming's standings live in the
//          desk at 376 and 380 (pixface.nifla's
//          ledger); the name being typed lives in
//          the notes block's tenth slot, and the
//          picture's name in its eleventh, beside the
//          editor's draft in the ninth. Every change
//          the naming makes repaints by Stones 34 and
//          35's region road over the files window
//          alone, as the feather visits widen it to
//          this window when a keeping changes its
//          list. The stone's growth carried the
//          kernel's staged segment end past the BIOS
//          doorman's 320 KiB claim, so the claim
//          stands at 352 KiB now and the module stages
//          stand above the whole of it in the last
//          32 KiB of low ground (boot/cardnull-boot
//          .asm's map tells it in full, in the line of
//          Stones 26, 29, and 32). One honest limit:
//          the picture cannot
//          be copied — the disk's picture store holds
//          one picture, and a copy would need a
//          second picture's place that does not stand
//          there; the window offers the picture no
//          "Copy", and says nothing it cannot do.
// Stone 41: a Bible reading window — an eleventh
//          window on the face of light, opened by the
//          word 'bible', by a ninth icon at the
//          desktop's left edge, and by a new choice,
//          Bible, in the bar's menu (the menu's ninth
//          window choice; Rest stands under it now,
//          ten rows in all). Where the verse window
//          shows one verse for the day, this window
//          reads a passage at length: the whole book
//          of Jonah — all four chapters, all forty-
//          eight verses — in the World English
//          Bible's wording exactly, the translation
//          the verse window already carries, kept in
//          bibletext.nifla a chapter at a time and
//          wrapped as they are painted, nine lines to
//          a page, thirty pages in all with its
//          contents page. It opens on that contents
//          page, which names the book and its
//          translation and lists its chapters; the
//          chapter pick moves with the arrow keys and
//          Enter reads the picked chapter, and a
//          press on the contents page's foot reads it
//          too.
//          Each reading page stands under its
//          passage's plain name at the window's head
//          ("Jonah 1"), each chapter beginning on a
//          fresh page; the pages turn forward and
//          back by the arrow keys and by the foot's
//          words — Back at its left, More at its
//          right, First page on the last page, which
//          returns to the contents page. The window
//          is a window like the others: it drags by
//          its title bar, Tab reaches it, Escape and
//          its close mark close it, its chip stands
//          on the bar with the others' (Stone 39's
//          laying grown to nine chips), and its
//          standing lives in the desk at 424 to 440.
//          The text stands in the kernel with the
//          kept verses and the help words: Stone 40
//          left 23,733 bytes of headroom under the
//          doorman's 352 KiB claim, and this book and
//          its window stand inside it — no claim
//          raised, no stage moved, no disc or disk
//          road needed (bibletext.nifla's heading and
//          the README's Stone 41 section record the
//          reckoning and the new segment end).
// Stone 42: the Shield shows itself — a twelfth
//          window on the face of light, opened by the
//          word 'shield', by a tenth icon at the
//          desktop's left edge, and by a new choice,
//          Shield, in the bar's menu (the menu's tenth
//          window choice; Rest stands under it now,
//          eleven rows in all). The guard itself is
//          not new: since Stone 5 the feather door has
//          read every feather's card and reckoned its
//          bytes before any visit, and turned away in
//          plain words any feather whose reckoning did
//          not match its card — the work was done in
//          the dark, and this stone brings it into the
//          light. The window tells, in plain words,
//          what the Shield is and what it has done
//          since the rising: how many feathers the
//          door has let in, and how many it has turned
//          away — the main body's reckoning, kept at
//          every receiving (the welcome feather's, the
//          paint feather's, and the calculator
//          feather's alike) and copied into the desk
//          for the painting, the discipline the files
//          window's facts have always kept. And it
//          tells the Shield's two standing truths:
//          it has no switch — nothing in the window,
//          and nothing anywhere, turns it off — and it
//          sends nothing anywhere, for the system has
//          no wire for sending and nothing about the
//          keeper leaves the machine. The window is
//          a window like the others in every road:
//          its chip stands on the bar (a tenth), Tab
//          reaches it, Escape and its close mark close
//          it, its title bar drags it. Its standing
//          lives in the desk at 444 to 463
//          (pixface.nifla's ledger), and the desk
//          stands 464 bytes now.
//
// Stone 43: the USB stick in the running system — a
//          mass-storage device standing on the xHCI
//          bus is raised by the running system itself
//          (usbstick.nifla), on the controller the
//          keyboard road set walking, and its sectors
//          are read and written through the bulk-only
//          transport and the SCSI words it carries.
//          The stick can carry the keeping — the card,
//          the notes page, the kept picture — in
//          diskkeep.nifla's very layout, kept by that
//          library's twin in the new one; and the
//          choice of which disk keeps is made once, at
//          the rising: a fitted hard disk keeps its
//          place, as it always has, and the stick
//          keeps when no hard disk stands fitted.
//          Every keeping call in this body goes
//          through the k— wrappers (they stand just
//          above the notes block's taking), which hand
//          the call to the keeping disk of the rising;
//          picsave's two call sites branch by hand,
//          its words being one too many for a wrapper.
//          The xHCI raising stands ahead of the
//          keeping's reading now, so the stick's
//          standing is known before the card is read.
//          The word 'usb' reports the stick beside the
//          keyboards, and the rising's offer names the
//          stick when the stick is the disk offered.
//
// Stone 44: a second screen — when the machine carries
//          a second display adapter on the PCI bus,
//          the running system raises a still companion
//          page on it (twoscreen.nifla): the system's
//          name, the day and the date, the clock's
//          face, and a line of Psalm 127, in the
//          desktop's own glass colors and the system's
//          own letters. It is not a second desktop and
//          not a mirror: no window stands on it, and
//          nothing moves there but the clock's minute
//          (the loop repaints it when the minute
//          turns, beside the bar's own drawing). The
//          rising's answers name it in plain words —
//          "A second screen is here." — and when no
//          second adapter stands, nothing anywhere
//          changes: the raising answers 0 and the
//          page is never painted.
//
// Stone 45: the repaint roads healed — the two faults
//          Tyler witnessed in UTM on the Stone 44
//          build (November 24, 2026): dragging the
//          welcome window left its body a flat gray
//          with garbled streaks where its words should
//          stand, and every window flickered while a
//          window moved. Both faults lived in the
//          repaint roads, not the drag's arithmetic.
//          The face paints straight onto the screen —
//          no back buffer, no clipping — and a window's
//          painter lays the frame's gray undercoat
//          first, then the body over it; a region
//          repaint pass could write farther than its
//          road (a re-laid icon's whole span, a
//          repainted window's whole place and shadow)
//          and leave that writing standing on a window
//          the pass never repainted, its undercoat
//          showing and its words streaked — the state
//          right, the glass wrong, healed only by some
//          later whole repaint. paintregion's set is
//          now closed before anything is washed: any
//          window a pass's writing can fall on joins
//          the set and is repainted in the same pass,
//          after the icons, so a window's body always
//          lands. And a drag step no longer repaints
//          bystanders whole: dragstep washes only the
//          ground the window leaves, lays its icons
//          again, and paints the window at its new
//          place; the windows a step uncovers stand
//          washed until the drop, when one region
//          repaint over the whole road the drag
//          travelled — gathered step by step — makes
//          the glass whole once. The drag's own proof,
//          prove-stone34, stands restored with it: its
//          loop-pass counter read a compiler cell the
//          compiler no longer lays in the kernel, so
//          the bystanders' ground is now hashed after
//          every step instead, and the dragged
//          window's body is censused and its lines
//          decoded after the drop. The desk stands
//          476 bytes now: the drag step's errand
//          travels at 464 to 475, the way the road
//          travels at 384.
//
// Stone 46: all the programs in the start menu — at
//          Tyler's word from the same witness ("i was
//          thinking all those programs would be in
//          the start menu"). The bar's menu carried
//          eleven choices; Paint and Terminal stood
//          on the desktop with none. The menu now
//          carries thirteen, in the desktop icons'
//          own order: Welcome and Feather first, as
//          they have always stood, then Files, Editor,
//          Paint, Calculator, Verse, Help, Settings,
//          Terminal, Bible, Shield, and Rest last —
//          Paint after Editor and Terminal after
//          Settings, where they stand among their
//          neighbors. The menu's reckoning grew with
//          it (thirteen rows, its top two rows higher,
//          its foot still on the bar's top edge), and
//          painter and pointer read the same reckoning
//          as they always have, so the press that
//          picks a choice lands on the choice the
//          painter drew. The two new choices open
//          their windows exactly as their typed words
//          do — Paint with its feather's opening visit
//          asked for, Terminal with its kept answers
//          standing in it.
// Stone 48: the picture window — a thirteenth window
//          on the face of light, where the kept picture
//          (the one Paint keeps to the disk, listed in
//          the files window) stands open big, so a
//          keeper can actually look at it: every cell
//          of the canvas stands sixteen pixels wide
//          and thirty-two tall here, twice the paint
//          window's height for each cell, the whole
//          canvas 768 by 224 pixels. It opens from
//          the files window — "Look at it" stands at
//          the foot while the picture stands picked,
//          beside "Rename" — from the bar's menu (a
//          fourteenth choice, Picture, standing after
//          Shield with Rest still last), and by the
//          word 'picture'. With no picture kept, the
//          window opens all the same and says so
//          plainly in its body. It is a true window of
//          the desktop: its standing and place live in
//          the desk at 476 to 488 (pixface.nifla's
//          ledger; the desk stands 492 bytes now), the
//          forward reckoning at 180 names it 13, its
//          title bar drags on Stone 47's healed roads,
//          and its rendering reads the kept picture's
//          own block through the address at 480 — the
//          real kept cells, never a placeholder. It
//          carries no desktop icon: the icons' column
//          is full — ten icons stand in it, and on the
//          short grid of large print the tenth already
//          reaches the bar's top row, so an eleventh
//          would stand on the bar itself. Its bar chip
//          stands eleventh when the bar has room, as
//          every chip does.
//
// Stone 49: the stick and disk exchange — the files
//          window gains the exchange with a plugged-in
//          USB stick. The window grew by one row again
//          (sixteen now, as it grew for Stone 26's
//          picture entry), and on its new last row,
//          while a thing stands picked, two words stand
//          in white: "To the stick" and "From the
//          stick". A press copies the picked thing — a
//          note as that note, its whole slot; the kept
//          picture as the picture, its header and its
//          three sectors — between the keeping disk and
//          the stick's own keeping ground (Stone 43's
//          twin of the disk's layout, at the same
//          sectors), through the stick library's own
//          roads, which stand as they were built. A
//          copy is a copy: the source stands untouched,
//          and the destination gains the thing under
//          the name it stands under. A note is known
//          across the two keepings by that name — its
//          given name, or its words — and the exchange
//          answers plainly in the window, on the same
//          row, whenever it cannot or should not copy:
//          no stick stands in this computer; the stick
//          is itself the keeping; that name already
//          stands on the stick (Stone 40's refusal —
//          nothing is silently overwritten); the
//          stick's notes page is full; a picture
//          already stands on the stick; the stick
//          holds no note of that name; no picture
//          stands on the stick. Bringing a note in
//          refreshes the picked note's own keeping
//          from its twin on the stick — the one keeping
//          of that name, never a second beside it —
//          and bringing the picture in takes the kept
//          picture's place the same way, name and all;
//          what is brought in is written through to
//          the keeping disk behind the disk's own
//          standing, as every keeping is. The word the
//          exchange last spoke lives in the desk at 492
//          (the desk stands 496 bytes now) and is
//          forgotten whenever the pick moves. No drag
//          arithmetic changed, none of Stone 47's six
//          parts, not Stone 48's window, and none of
//          the Shield's truths.
//
// Stone 50: make this computer a Cardinal — the system
//          installs itself from a Cardnull stick onto
//          the computer's own hard disk, so the machine
//          afterwards boots from that disk alone, no
//          stick in it and no disc in its drive. The
//          files window grew by one row again (seventeen
//          now), and on its new last row the words "Make
//          this computer a Cardinal" stand in white —
//          always, not only while a thing stands picked,
//          for the making concerns the whole machine. A
//          press opens an asking in the window, in the
//          letting-go asking's own shape (Stone 27): the
//          plain question, what will happen said plainly
//          — the system copied from the stick onto the
//          computer's disk, the keeping left as it
//          stands — and the two choices, "Yes, make it a
//          Cardinal" and "Not yet", with "Not yet"
//          standing picked, so no stray Enter installs
//          anything. Behind the yes, the making runs:
//          the stick must carry the system (usbstick's
//          ustickimage reads its record and its volume
//          mark and gives the image's length); the disk
//          must not already carry a Cardinal (diskkeep's
//          diskcardinal — a second system is never laid
//          over the first, Stone 40's discipline); the
//          disk must hold a fitted hard disk of room
//          enough; the image is then copied sector by
//          sector to the disk's sector 2048 — a megabyte
//          in, past the keeping's whole ground (the card
//          at 8, the notes at 64 and after, the picture
//          at 80 and after stand before it and are never
//          touched) — proved at three of its places
//          against the stick, and only then is the
//          disk's own master boot record laid at sector
//          0, from the stick's record with its partition
//          table's beginning set to 2048, and read back
//          whole. A disk that carried no card yet is
//          carded from the sitting's own standings —
//          the clock's choice, the chime, the words'
//          size, the sitting's notes and picture written
//          through by the disk's own store paths — so a
//          later boot from the disk alone rises with the
//          same keeping; a disk that already kept is not
//          written in its keeping at all. The refusals
//          stand in plain words on the window's last
//          row, where the making's word lives (the desk
//          at 504, with the asking's standing at 496 and
//          its pick at 500; the desk stands 508 bytes
//          now): no hard disk stands in this computer;
//          this disk already carries a Cardinal; no
//          stick stands in this computer; the stick does
//          not carry Cardnull; the disk is too small to
//          carry a Cardinal; the copying did not finish
//          — this computer is not a Cardinal yet. While
//          the copy runs the window says it is making,
//          and the desktop stands still for it: eighteen
//          thousand sector readings and writings are
//          slow, and the making does not pretend
//          otherwise. The boot doors learned the one new
//          thing the making asks of them (their own
//          headings tell it): the record's stub and the
//          doorman both take where the image begins from
//          the record's partition table — 0 on the stick
//          and every disc, the road unchanged there —
//          and the setup gate's clearing is guarded by
//          the same diskcardinal, for its promised wipe
//          of the first sixty-four sectors would unmake
//          an installed disk's record. The stick is only
//          read by the making, never written. No drag
//          arithmetic changed, none of Stone 47's six
//          parts, not the carried-press mend's gate, not
//          Stone 48's window, not the Shield, and not
//          the exchange.
//
// The arrangement of the screen (the text face's rows;
// on the face of light the same words stand on its grid,
// and since Stone 33 its bar stands at the foot):
//   row 0      the bar — "Cardnull" at the left, the date
//              and the clock at the right, white on blue
//   rows 3-9   the welcome panel, black on light gray
//   row 11     the invitation: what words the machine knows
//   rows 13-22 the answers, newest at the bottom
//   row 24     the typing line
//
// The clock tells the world's time (Stone 7): the reading
// taken at boot, carried forward by the machine's own
// tick. If no true reading could be had, it falls back to
// the honest count since waking, as it did before.


// ---- the keyboard's letters (Stone 2, grown at Stone 12) ----
//
// A key number (the make code of the first key set — both
// roads speak these, the USB road by translation) and the
// shift standing that was resolved for it when it was
// pressed: 1 when the letter stands capital, or the figure
// stands as its shifted mark. The caller's resolving folds
// Caps Lock in for the letters alone (see isletter and the
// loop). Stone 2's table knew only the small letters and
// the space; Stone 12 teaches the whole board a person
// types on — the figures and their marks, and the marks
// that stand between the letters.

make charFor(code, mod) {
    if code == 2 { if mod == 1 { give "!" } give "1" }
    if code == 3 { if mod == 1 { give "@" } give "2" }
    if code == 4 { if mod == 1 { give "#" } give "3" }
    if code == 5 { if mod == 1 { give "$" } give "4" }
    if code == 6 { if mod == 1 { give "%" } give "5" }
    if code == 7 { if mod == 1 { give "^" } give "6" }
    if code == 8 { if mod == 1 { give "&" } give "7" }
    if code == 9 { if mod == 1 { give "*" } give "8" }
    if code == 10 { if mod == 1 { give "(" } give "9" }
    if code == 11 { if mod == 1 { give ")" } give "0" }
    if code == 12 { if mod == 1 { give "_" } give "-" }
    if code == 13 { if mod == 1 { give "+" } give "=" }
    if code == 16 { if mod == 1 { give "Q" } give "q" }
    if code == 17 { if mod == 1 { give "W" } give "w" }
    if code == 18 { if mod == 1 { give "E" } give "e" }
    if code == 19 { if mod == 1 { give "R" } give "r" }
    if code == 20 { if mod == 1 { give "T" } give "t" }
    if code == 21 { if mod == 1 { give "Y" } give "y" }
    if code == 22 { if mod == 1 { give "U" } give "u" }
    if code == 23 { if mod == 1 { give "I" } give "i" }
    if code == 24 { if mod == 1 { give "O" } give "o" }
    if code == 25 { if mod == 1 { give "P" } give "p" }
    if code == 26 { if mod == 1 { give "{" } give "[" }
    if code == 27 { if mod == 1 { give "}" } give "]" }
    if code == 30 { if mod == 1 { give "A" } give "a" }
    if code == 31 { if mod == 1 { give "S" } give "s" }
    if code == 32 { if mod == 1 { give "D" } give "d" }
    if code == 33 { if mod == 1 { give "F" } give "f" }
    if code == 34 { if mod == 1 { give "G" } give "g" }
    if code == 35 { if mod == 1 { give "H" } give "h" }
    if code == 36 { if mod == 1 { give "J" } give "j" }
    if code == 37 { if mod == 1 { give "K" } give "k" }
    if code == 38 { if mod == 1 { give "L" } give "l" }
    if code == 39 { if mod == 1 { give ":" } give ";" }
    if code == 40 { if mod == 1 { give "\"" } give "'" }
    if code == 41 { if mod == 1 { give "~" } give "`" }
    if code == 43 { if mod == 1 { give "|" } give "\\" }
    if code == 44 { if mod == 1 { give "Z" } give "z" }
    if code == 45 { if mod == 1 { give "X" } give "x" }
    if code == 46 { if mod == 1 { give "C" } give "c" }
    if code == 47 { if mod == 1 { give "V" } give "v" }
    if code == 48 { if mod == 1 { give "B" } give "b" }
    if code == 49 { if mod == 1 { give "N" } give "n" }
    if code == 50 { if mod == 1 { give "M" } give "m" }
    if code == 51 { if mod == 1 { give "<" } give "," }
    if code == 52 { if mod == 1 { give ">" } give "." }
    if code == 53 { if mod == 1 { give "?" } give "/" }
    if code == 57 { give " " }
    give ""
}

// Whether a key number is one of the letters — the keys
// Caps Lock speaks to. The figures and marks answer to
// the shift keys alone.
make isletter(code) {
    if code >= 16 {
        if code <= 25 { give 1 }
    }
    if code >= 30 {
        if code <= 38 { give 1 }
    }
    if code >= 44 {
        if code <= 50 { give 1 }
    }
    give 0
}

// The typed line, rebuilt from 'count' key numbers in
// 'buf' beginning at 'start', each with the shift standing
// resolved for it in 'mods' at the same place (Stone 2's
// building, taught spans and standings at Stone 12 — a
// span is how the line's first word and its remainder are
// each built whole).
make buildline(buf, mods, start, count) {
    let s = ""
    let i = 0
    while i < count {
        s = s + charFor(buf[start + i], mods[start + i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    give s
}

// Draws the typing line — on the face of light, at
// the terminal window's foot (Stone 38): the row
// stands in the desk at 136, laid inside the window
// by layplaces, and the line is drawn across the
// window's own span, its prompt mark a cell in from
// the frame and the typed words two cells after it.
// While the terminal stands closed the line is not
// drawn on this face at all — the first key of a
// line raises the window before the letter lands
// (the key receiving in the main loop), so a line
// being typed always stands in an open window.
make drawtyping(text, pix, pixdesk) {
    if pix == 1 {
        if pget(pixdesk, 404) == 1 {
            let cs = pget(pixdesk, 320)
            let trow = pget(pixdesk, 136)
            let tl = teleft(pixdesk)
            pixink(pixdesk, 0)
            pixfill(pixdesk, tl * cs, trow * cs, tewide(pixdesk) * cs, cs)
            pixline(pixdesk, (trow) * 256 + (tl + 1), ">", 7)
            pixline(pixdesk, (trow) * 256 + (tl + 3), text, 7)
        }
    } else {
    floor(text) {
        mov rdx, 0xB8000
        add rdx, 3840
        mov byte ptr [rdx], 62
        mov byte ptr [rdx + 1], 0x07
        mov byte ptr [rdx + 2], 32
        mov byte ptr [rdx + 3], 0x07
        add rdx, 4
        mov rcx, 0
    dt_loop:
        cmp rcx, r12
        jae dt_blank
        mov al, [rbx + rcx]
        mov [rdx + rcx * 2], al
        mov byte ptr [rdx + rcx * 2 + 1], 0x07
        inc rcx
        jmp dt_loop
    dt_blank:
        cmp rcx, 48
        jae dt_done
        mov byte ptr [rdx + rcx * 2], 32
        mov byte ptr [rdx + rcx * 2 + 1], 0x07
        inc rcx
        jmp dt_blank
    dt_done:
    }
    }
}

// ---- the face ----

// Draws the bar's right-hand end: the date in plain words
// and the clock (Stone 7). 'utcnow' is the world's present
// count of seconds (see the boot sequence), with the
// person's time choice applied here; when it stands below
// zero — no true reading could be had at boot — the bar
// keeps the honest count since waking, as it did before
// this stone, and no date is shown. In the pixel face
// (Stone 9) the same words are drawn as light; 'pix' says
// which face is up, and 'pixdesk' is the pixel face's desk.
make drawclock(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk) {
    if pix == 1 {
        let cs = pget(pixdesk, 320)
        let cols = pget(pixdesk, 28)
        // The bar's row (Stone 33): the bar stands at the
        // screen's foot, and its words stand on its lower
        // row — the grid's last row.
        let brow = pget(pixdesk, 32) - 1
        if cols < 48 {
            // A narrow grid (large print, Stone 28): the
            // bar holds the clock alone — the date and
            // the title cannot both stand beside it at
            // this size — and the word 'time' still tells
            // the date in full, as it always has.
            pixink(pixdesk, 1)
            pixfill(pixdesk, (cols - 10) * cs, brow * cs, 10 * cs, cs)
            if utcnow < 0 {
                pixline(pixdesk, (brow) * 256 + (cols - 8), clocktext(ticks() / 100, offset), 31)
            } else {
                let local2 = utcnow + offset * 3600
                let days2 = local2 / 86400
                let daysec2 = local2 - days2 * 86400
                pixline(pixdesk, (brow) * 256 + (cols - 8), clocktext(daysec2, 0), 31)
            }
        } else {
        pixink(pixdesk, 1)
        pixfill(pixdesk, (cols - 40) * cs, brow * cs, 40 * cs, cs)
        if utcnow < 0 {
            pixline(pixdesk, (brow) * 256 + (cols - 8), clocktext(ticks() / 100, offset), 31)
        } else {
            let local = utcnow + offset * 3600
            let days = local / 86400
            let daysec = local - days * 86400
            let dt = datetext(days)
            pixline(pixdesk, (brow) * 256 + (cols - 10 - length(dt)), dt, 31)
            pixline(pixdesk, (brow) * 256 + (cols - 8), clocktext(daysec, 0), 31)
        }
        }
    } else {
        if utcnow < 0 {
            writeat(0, 72, clocktext(ticks() / 100, offset), 31)
        } else {
            let local = utcnow + offset * 3600
            let days = local / 86400
            let daysec = local - days * 86400
            let dt = datetext(days)
            writeat(0, 10, "                                                            ", 31)
            writeat(0, 70 - length(dt), dt, 31)
            writeat(0, 72, clocktext(daysec, 0), 31)
        }
    }
}

// Draws the whole desktop: the bar with its clock and the
// invitation, and — on the text face — the welcome panel.
// The answers region is left blank, ready. Two faces draw
// it (Stone 9): the pixel face when the loader granted a
// picture screen, and the text face — the system's first
// face — when it did not; the words are the same words
// either way. On the pixel face the welcome panel is one
// of the two windows (Stone 14), painted by paintwindows
// below, which the main body calls after this drawing.

// The second screen's painting (Stone 44): the
// companion page's standing lines from twoscreen's
// painter, and the two lines composed from the same
// words the bar speaks — the date in plain words at
// the page's 350 row, the clock's face at its 400.
// Without a true clock reading the date line stands
// empty and the clock shows the session's own count,
// as the bar's face does.
make paintsecond(twodesk: bytes, fontblock: bytes, utcnow: number, offset: number) {
    twopaint(twodesk, fontblock)
    if get32(twodesk, 0) == 1 {
        if utcnow >= 0 {
            put32(twodesk, 16, 350)
            twoline(twodesk, fontblock, datetext(utcnow / 86400))
            put32(twodesk, 16, 400)
            twoline(twodesk, fontblock, clocktext(utcnow, offset))
        } else {
            put32(twodesk, 16, 400)
            twoline(twodesk, fontblock, clocktext(ticks() / 100, offset))
        }
    }
}

// ---- the desktop's invitation (Stone 47) ----
//
// The two lines the face paints on its ground at the
// rising: "Type a word, then press Enter:" and the
// words the typing answers to. Until this stone the
// lines had no owner — drawface painted them at the
// rising and on a word-size switch, and no repaint
// path ever laid them again — so any wash that
// crossed them (a drag step's leaving ground, a
// region road's wash, a closed window's wash) rubbed
// them out in patches for the rest of the sitting,
// and only a restart laid them whole: half the
// "reset it in UTM" Tyler reported (Nov 24, 2026).
// Now the ground has an owner, as every window and
// icon has: groundline lays one line, paintground
// lays both, and the repaint paths call them the way
// they call the icons — the whole repaint after its
// closed windows' washes and before its icons (the
// Help icon's disc overdraws the first line's first
// letter, exactly as at the rising), the region
// repaint for the lines its road touches. drawface
// itself lays them through the same hands, so the
// rising and the re-laying can never disagree about
// a word or a place.

// groundrow: invitation line 'which' (0 the asking,
// 1 the words) stands at this row of the face's
// grid — the layout's rows 11 and 12, laid on the
// grid as drawface has always laid them.
make groundrow(desk, which) {
    give layrow(desk, 11 + which)
}

// groundwide: how many cells invitation line
// 'which' runs to, from its first cell at column 2 —
// the asking's thirty, and the words' sixty-one on
// a wide grid, thirty-nine on the narrow one, the
// same words groundline names at each size.
make groundwide(desk, which) {
    if which == 0 {
        give 30
    }
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 61
    }
    give 39
}

// groundline lays invitation line 'which' again,
// word for word as the rising lays it.
make groundline(desk, which) {
    if which == 0 {
        pixline(desk, (groundrow(desk, 0)) * 256 + (2), "Type a word, then press Enter:", 7)
    } else {
        if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
            pixline(desk, (groundrow(desk, 1)) * 256 + (2), "hello time memory eastern utc feather welcome usb setup clear", 7)
        } else {
            // A narrow grid (large print, Stone 28):
            // the invitation names the words that
            // fit, as it has always left words it has
            // no room for unnamed (Stone 15) — every
            // word still answers to its name all the
            // same.
            pixline(desk, (groundrow(desk, 1)) * 256 + (2), "hello time memory feather welcome clear", 7)
        }
    }
}

// paintground lays the invitation's both lines —
// the whole repaint's ground-keeping.
make paintground(desk) {
    groundline(desk, 0)
    groundline(desk, 1)
}

make drawface(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk) {
    if pix == 1 {
        let cs = pget(pixdesk, 320)
        pixink(pixdesk, 0)
        pixfill(pixdesk, 0, 0, pget(pixdesk, 8), pget(pixdesk, 12))
        // The glass bar at the screen's foot (Stone 33):
        // pixface draws it — its sheen, its tint, and the
        // round start button with the cardinal — and the
        // name stands on its lower row beside the button,
        // the clock at its right, as they always have.
        pixbar(pixdesk)
        pixline(pixdesk, (pget(pixdesk, 32) - 1) * 256 + (2), "Cardnull", 31)
        drawclock(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk)
        // The invitation on the ground, laid by its
        // owner (Stone 47): the same hands the
        // repaint paths use to lay it again after
        // any wash that crosses it.
        groundline(pixdesk, 0)
        groundline(pixdesk, 1)
    } else {
    clearscreen()
    paintrow(0, 31)
    writeat(0, 1, "Cardnull", 31)
    drawclock(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk)
    paintrect(3, 8, 64, 7, 112)
    writeat(4, 10, "Welcome to Cardnull", 112)
    writeat(5, 10, "This computer is public domain: free for everyone, forever.", 112)
    writeat(7, 10, "\"Unless Yahweh builds the house,", 112)
    writeat(8, 10, "they labor in vain who build it.\" - Psalm 127:1", 112)
    writeat(11, 2, "Type a word: hello, time, memory, eastern, utc, feather, usb, setup, clear", 7)
    }
}

// Writes one answer in the answer region at 'row', scrolling
// the region when it is full, and gives back the next row.
// In the pixel face the line is drawn by pixface's printer,
// which wraps words at the narrower grid and scrolls its own
// region; the rows it counts are the pixel face's layout
// rows, not the text face's.
make printline(row, text, pix, pixdesk) {
    if pix == 1 {
        give pixprintline(pixdesk, row, text)
    }
    let r = row
    if r > 22 {
        scrollanswers()
        r = 22
    }
    writeat(r, 2, text, 7)
    give r + 1
}

// Writes one kept note in the answer region at 'row',
// scrolling the region when it is full, and gives back
// the next row — printline's shape, for a note, which is
// kept as bytes in its slot of the notes block (the
// length at the slot's first byte, the letters after)
// and so is written from its bytes on both faces: drawn
// letter by letter on the face of light (pixnoteline),
// and laid into the text screen's own memory here. The
// slot and the face's standing travel packed in one word
// ('slotpix': the slot times two, plus 1 on the face of
// light), as pixline packs its cell — a call can carry
// only six words, and the block and the desk take four.
make printnote(row, block, slotpix, pixdesk) {
    let slot = slotpix / 2
    if slotpix - slot * 2 == 1 {
        give pixnoteline(pixdesk, block, row, slot * 64)
    }
    let r = row
    if r > 22 {
        scrollanswers()
        r = 22
    }
    floor(block, slot, r) {
        mov rax, [r10]
        shl rax, 6
        lea rsi, [rbx + rax]
        movzx ecx, byte ptr [rsi]
        mov rdx, 0xB8000
        mov rax, [r10 + 8]
        imul rax, 160
        add rdx, rax
        add rdx, 4
        xor r8, r8
    pn_loop:
        cmp r8, rcx
        jae pn_done
        mov al, [rsi + r8 + 1]
        mov [rdx + r8 * 2], al
        mov byte ptr [rdx + r8 * 2 + 1], 0x07
        inc r8
        jmp pn_loop
    pn_done:
    }
    give r + 1
}

// ---- the two windows (Stone 14) ----
//
// On the face of light the welcome panel and the feather's
// panel stand as windows: each is framed, each carries a
// plain title bar with its name and a close mark (a small
// x at the bar's right end), and one of them stands
// forward — its title bar blue, the other's quiet gray.
// The windows' standing lives in the pixel face's desk
// (offsets 172 to 184, pixface.nifla's ledger): which are
// open, which is forward, and whether a feather has visited.
// These painters read that standing from the desk they are
// handed, as all the face's drawing does. A window that
// stands closed is painted as the plain desktop it covers,
// so closing one leaves the desktop whole.

// Draws one window's frame and title bar: a gray edge
// around the whole window, the bar across its top — blue
// when the window stands forward, gray when it stands
// behind — and the close mark at the bar's right end. The
// window's place and size are in letter cells, packed as
// the face packs a cell: 'pos' is the top row times 256
// plus the left column, 'size' the height times 256 plus
// the width. The bar's paper is left set for the caller,
// which draws the window's name next.
make winframe(desk, pos, size, front) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let y = pos / 256
    let x = pos - y * 256
    let h = size / 256
    let w = size - h * 256
    // The glass treatment (Stone 33): the window's soft
    // shadow is laid first, along its right edge and
    // under its foot; the frame itself stands with
    // rounded corners — its cross of two fills leaves the
    // four corner squares to four small discs, so the
    // corner pixels are the disc's curve, and the ground
    // beyond the curve is never the frame's to paint.
    pixshadow(desk, x * cs, y * cs, w * cs, h * cs)
    pixink(desk, 8)
    pixfill(desk, x * cs + 4, y * cs, w * cs - 8, h * cs)
    pixfill(desk, x * cs, y * cs + 4, w * cs, h * cs - 8)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + 4, y * cs + 4, 4)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + w * cs - 5, y * cs + 4, 4)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + 4, y * cs + h * cs - 5, 4)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + w * cs - 5, y * cs + h * cs - 5, 4)
    if front == 1 {
        pixink(desk, 1)
    } else {
        pixink(desk, 8)
    }
    pixfill(desk, x * cs + 5, y * cs + 2, w * cs - 10, cs)
    pixfill(desk, x * cs + 2, y * cs + 5, w * cs - 4, cs - 3)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + 5, y * cs + 5, 3)
    pixdisc(desk, x * cs + w * cs - 6, y * cs + 5, 3)
    // The title bar's sheen: a pale band across its upper
    // part, and the calm blue-green tint where the sheen
    // meets the bar's own color at its foot.
    if front == 1 {
        pixinkrgb(desk, glasssheen())
    } else {
        pixinkrgb(desk, glasspale())
    }
    pixfill(desk, x * cs + 4, y * cs + 3, w * cs - 8, cs / 2 - 3)
    pixinkrgb(desk, glasstint())
    pixfill(desk, x * cs + 3, y * cs + cs, w * cs - 6, 2)
    if front == 1 {
        pixpaper(desk, 1)
    } else {
        pixpaper(desk, 8)
    }
    pixspan(desk, 0, 1, 15)
    pixtext(desk, y, x + w - 2, "x")
}

// Paints the welcome window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar and the welcome's own words on their light ground,
// exactly where they have always stood.
make onewelcome(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let pl = pget(desk, 140)
    let pt = pget(desk, 144)
    let pw = pget(desk, 148)
    let ph = pget(desk, 152)
    if pget(desk, 172) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, pl, pt, pw, ph)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 1 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, pt * 256 + pl, ph * 256 + pw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 7, 15)
        pixtext(desk, pt, pl + 1, "Welcome")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, pl * cs + 2, pt * cs + cs + 2, pw * cs - 4, ph * cs - cs - 4)
        pixline(desk, (pt + (ph * 1) / 7) * 256 + (pl + 2), "Welcome to Cardnull", 112)
        pixline(desk, (pt + (ph * 2) / 7) * 256 + (pl + 2), "This computer is public domain:", 112)
        pixline(desk, (pt + (ph * 3) / 7) * 256 + (pl + 2), "free for everyone, forever.", 112)
        pixline(desk, (pt + (ph * 4) / 7) * 256 + (pl + 2), "\"Unless Yahweh builds the house,", 112)
        pixline(desk, (pt + (ph * 5) / 7) * 256 + (pl + 2), "they labor in vain who build it.\" - Psalm 127:1", 112)
    }
}

// The feather window's place and size on the face of
// light, in the face's own letter cells. The window is
// fifty-four cells wide — room for the feather's whole
// panel (the text face's columns 14 to 65, fifty-two
// cells) with a cell to spare at each side — and nine
// cells high: its title row, a breathing row, the
// panel's five rows, and two rows below. Its top stands
// where the layout has always put it (the text face's
// row 14, laid on this grid), and it is centered across
// the face. These four reckonings are the window's one
// definition: the painter below and the pointer's
// pressing in the main loop both take the window's
// place from them, so the two can never disagree.
make fwwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 320) > 16 {
        // Large print (Stone 28): the window's frame
        // grows with the words, but the feather's cells
        // inside it are carried 1:1 at sixteen pixels, as
        // they always have been — so the window is as
        // many large cells as the carried panel (fifty-
        // two cells of sixteen pixels, with its margins)
        // needs: thirty-six, when the grid holds them.
        if pget(desk, 28) >= 38 {
            give 36
        }
        give pget(desk, 28) - 2
    }
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make fwleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 236)
}

make fwtop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 240)
}

make fwhigh(desk) {
    give 9
}

// Paints the feather window from the desk's standing: its
// frame stands around the panel the feather paints (the
// text face's rows 15 to 19, columns 14 to 65), with a
// title row above it. Before any visit its body says so
// in plain words; after one, the feather's own cells are
// shown as light, cell by cell in the colors the feather
// chose — the showing the visit has always had (Stone 9),
// now kept by the window, so the visit stands until the
// window is closed or the face is drawn again.
//
// The cells are shown by CARRYING, never by scaling:
// each of the feather's cells stands in the face's own
// grid exactly one cell from its neighbor — the panel's
// first cell at the window's second column and third
// row, and every other cell counted on from there, one
// for one. The window's first painting (Stone 9, kept
// through Stone 14) instead laid each cell at the place
// laycol and layrow reckon for its text-face row and
// column — and those reckonings squeeze the text face's
// eighty columns onto this face's sixty-four and stretch
// its twenty-five rows over this face's forty-eight, so
// neighboring cells landed on the SAME face cell (the
// later painting overwrote the earlier, and letters
// vanished — one in five, in a steady rhythm) and the
// panel's rows landed two face rows apart with an
// unpainted row between them (the panel showed as green
// bands). Tyler witnessed exactly that on his own
// machine (November 15, 2026: "A fetheris vsitig" for
// "A feather is visiting"), and the bench's pixel proof
// (prove-featherpaint.sh) now decodes the painted window
// letter by letter so it cannot ship again. The system's
// own words were never garbled: they are drawn in the
// face's own grid to begin with, and only this showing
// ever scaled.
make onefeather(desk, block) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = fwleft(desk)
    let gy = fwtop(desk)
    let gw = fwwide(desk)
    let gh = fwhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 176) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 2 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        // The title's span names all seven letters of
        // "Feather": a span of five (the Files window's
        // measure, copied here) drew only "Feath" — the
        // word losing its last two letters at the
        // desktop's rising, where both windows stand
        // open (the feather start fix).
        pixspan(desk, 0, 7, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Feather")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        if pget(desk, 184) == 1 {
            // The feather's cells are carried 1:1, never
            // scaled (the discipline above), at sixteen
            // pixels a cell at either word size (Stone
            // 28): their first cell stands one face cell
            // in from the window's left and two down —
            // the very place the cell reckoning has
            // always named, in pixels.
            let gr = 15
            while gr < 20 {
                let gc = 14
                while gc < 66 {
                    let ch = cellbyte(block, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2)
                    let at = cellbyte(block, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2 + 1)
                    let fg = at - (at / 16) * 16
                    let bgv = (at / 16) - (at / 128) * 8
                    let ly = gy * cs + 2 * cs + (gr - 15) * 16
                    let lx = gx * cs + cs + (gc - 14) * 16
                    pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bgv * 4))
                    pixfill(desk, lx, ly, 16, 16)
                    if ch != 32 {
                        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
                        pixglyph(desk, lx, ly, ch, 2)
                    }
                    gc = gc + 1
                }
                gr = gr + 1
            }
        } else {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No feather has visited yet.", 7)
        }
    }
}

// ---- the files window (Stone 19) ----
//
// A third window, standing at the desktop's foot. It
// lists what the fitted disk actually holds: the notes
// page of the CNN3 store (sectors 64 onward) is all that
// store honestly holds, so the list is those notes —
// their own words are their names here — and nothing is
// invented beyond them. Picking an entry shows that
// note's whole words at the window's foot. The window's
// place and size are reckoned here, like the feather
// window's, so the painter and the pointer's pressing
// can never disagree. Its standing lives in the desk at
// 188 to 204 (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, the picked
// note, and the main body's facts copied in — how many
// notes stand kept, whether a disk is keeping them, and
// whether a disk is fitted at all.
//
// Since Stone 26 the disk honestly holds one more thing
// — the kept picture (sectors 80 onward) — and the
// window lists it beside the notes, in plain words, on
// its last row: "A picture you painted". The pick runs
// over the notes and then the picture (the pick at the
// notes' count is the picture's, its standing copied
// into the desk at 296); picking it by the arrow keys
// shows at the window's foot how it opens, and a press
// on its row, or Enter while it stands picked, opens it
// again in the paint window — the main loop's doing,
// through the paint window's own opening below.

make fiwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make fileft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 244)
}

make fitop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 248)
}

make fihigh(desk) {
    // Fourteen rows through Stone 25; fifteen since
    // Stone 26 — the kept picture's entry stands on the
    // window's last row (gy + 13), below the picked
    // note's words, so the window grew by that one row
    // and nothing above it moved. Sixteen since Stone
    // 49 — the exchange with a USB stick stands on the
    // window's new last row (gy + 14), below the
    // picture's entry, and the window grew by that one
    // row the same way: nothing above the new row
    // moved, and the window's top stands one row
    // higher for it, layplaces' clamp reckoning the
    // window whole above the bar as it always has.
    // Seventeen since Stone 50 — the making of a
    // Cardinal stands on the window's new last row
    // (gy + 15), below the exchange's row, and the
    // window grew by that one row the same way again.
    give 17
}

// Draws one kept note's words at a letter cell, letter
// by letter from its bytes in the notes block — a note
// is kept as bytes, not as a text (pixnoteline's
// reason). The note and the place are named together
// by 'place' — the slot times 65536, plus the row times
// 256, plus the column — and the colors by 'attr', the
// text face's own reckoning (the ink's palette place
// plus sixteen times the paper's), as pixline packs
// them: a call can carry only six words, and the desk
// and the block take four. Each letter's cell is first
// filled with the paper, then its shape drawn with
// the ink.
make filenotetext(desk, block, place, attr) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let slot = place / 65536
    let cell = place - slot * 65536
    let row = cell / 256
    let col = cell - row * 256
    let fg = attr - (attr / 16) * 16
    let bg = (attr / 16) - (attr / 128) * 8
    let n = cellbyte(block, slot * 64)
    let i = 0
    while i < n {
        let code = cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 1 + i)
        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bg * 4))
        pixfill(desk, (col + i) * cs, row * cs, cs, cs)
        if code != 32 {
            pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
            pixglyph(desk, (col + i) * cs, row * cs, code, cs / 8)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// Draws one kept note's NAME at a letter cell, letter
// by letter from the name bytes in its slot's tail —
// the name's length at the slot's forty-fifth byte,
// its letters after (Stone 40). The place is packed
// as filenotetext packs it. A note whose tail stands
// empty has no name to draw; the callers show its
// words instead, by filenotetext.
make filenametext(desk, block, place, attr) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let slot = place / 65536
    let cell = place - slot * 65536
    let row = cell / 256
    let col = cell - row * 256
    let fg = attr - (attr / 16) * 16
    let bg = (attr / 16) - (attr / 128) * 8
    let n = cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 44)
    let i = 0
    while i < n {
        let code = cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 45 + i)
        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bg * 4))
        pixfill(desk, (col + i) * cs, row * cs, cs, cs)
        if code != 32 {
            pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
            pixglyph(desk, (col + i) * cs, row * cs, code, cs / 8)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// The name a kept note stands under (Stone 40): its
// given name's length when one stands in its slot's
// tail, else its own words' length — the words are
// the name of a note that was never given one.
make effnamelen(block, slot) -> number {
    if cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 44) > 0 {
        give cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 44)
    }
    give cellbyte(block, slot * 64)
}

// One letter of the name a kept note stands under,
// by the same reckoning as effnamelen.
make effnamebyte(block, slot, i) -> number {
    if cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 44) > 0 {
        give cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 45 + i)
    }
    give cellbyte(block, slot * 64 + 1 + i)
}

// Whether the name being typed (the notes block's
// tenth slot, at 576, in the block's own shape) is
// letter for letter the name the given kept note
// stands under (Stone 40) — the naming way's refusal
// reckoning: a name that already stands is not given
// twice.
make draftisname(block, slot) -> number {
    let dn = cellbyte(block, 576)
    if dn != effnamelen(block, slot) {
        give 0
    }
    let i = 0
    while i < dn {
        if cellbyte(block, 576 + 1 + i) != effnamebyte(block, slot, i) {
            give 0
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    give 1
}

// Whether the name being typed is letter for letter
// the name the kept picture stands under (Stone 40):
// its given name in the block's eleventh slot (at
// 640) when one stands there, else its plain words,
// "A picture you painted".
make draftispicname(block) -> number {
    let dn = cellbyte(block, 576)
    if cellbyte(block, 640) > 0 {
        if dn != cellbyte(block, 640) {
            give 0
        }
        let i = 0
        while i < dn {
            if cellbyte(block, 576 + 1 + i) != cellbyte(block, 640 + 1 + i) {
                give 0
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        give 1
    }
    let lit = "A picture you painted"
    if dn != length(lit) {
        give 0
    }
    let i = 0
    while i < dn {
        if cellbyte(block, 576 + 1 + i) != textbyte(lit, i) {
            give 0
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    give 1
}

// Whether the name one kept note stands under is
// letter for letter the name another kept note
// stands under, the two notes standing in two
// blocks (Stone 49) — the exchange's reckoning: a
// note is known on the other disk by the name it
// stands under here, its given name or its words,
// exactly as the naming way's refusal at Stone 40
// reckons a name that already stands.
make slotstandsmatch(ab, aslot, bb, bslot) -> number {
    let n = effnamelen(ab, aslot)
    if n != effnamelen(bb, bslot) {
        give 0
    }
    let i = 0
    while i < n {
        if effnamebyte(ab, aslot, i) != effnamebyte(bb, bslot, i) {
            give 0
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    give 1
}

// Paints the files window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar and the list of the kept notes, the picked one
// standing white on blue, with the picked note's whole
// words at the window's foot — and, since Stone 27,
// "Let this note go" beside the foot's label while a
// note stands picked, and the asking that those words
// open ("Let this note go for good?", with "Yes, let
// it go" and "Keep it" to choose from) in the list's
// place while it stands. Notes kept only for the
// sitting are listed too, under their honest heading,
// so they can be picked and let go here; with no notes
// at all, the window says in plain words where notes
// would live, as it always has, and no entry is ever
// shown that is not kept. Since Stone 40 the list
// shows a note's given name where one stands (its
// words still stand at the foot when it is picked),
// the foot carries "Rename" and "Copy" beside the
// letting-go words while a note stands picked and
// "Rename" while the picture stands picked, and the
// naming way those words open stands in the list's
// place while it stands, in onefiles' own telling
// below. Since Stone 49 the window's last row
// carries the exchange with a USB stick — "To the
// stick" and "From the stick" while a thing stands
// picked, and the exchange's own word in their
// place after a press — in the telling at the
// row's own drawing below. Since Stone 50 the
// window's new last row carries the making of a
// Cardinal — its words standing always, its own
// word in their place after a press — and the
// making's asking stands in the list's place while
// it stands, in the telling at its own drawing
// below.
make onefiles(desk, notes) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = fileft(desk)
    let gy = fitop(desk)
    let gw = fiwide(desk)
    let gh = fihigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 188) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 3 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 5, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Files")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        let keeping = pget(desk, 200)
        let count = pget(desk, 196)
        let haspic = pget(desk, 296)
        if pget(desk, 496) == 1 {
            // The Cardinal asking (Stone 50): stands in
            // the list's place while it stands, in the
            // letting-go asking's own shape — the plain
            // question, what will happen said in plain
            // words, and the two choices, the picked one
            // white on blue with the pick mark, as the
            // window's picks have always stood. The
            // asking opens with "Not yet" picked (the
            // desk's 500 stands at 1), so Enter alone
            // never installs anything.
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Make this computer a Cardinal?", 7)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Cardnull will be copied from the stick", 7)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 4) * 256 + (gx + 2), "onto this computer's own disk.", 7)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Your notes and your picture stay as they are.", 7)
            if pget(desk, 500) == 0 {
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 7) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, make it a Cardinal", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Not yet", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, make it a Cardinal", 7)
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 8) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Not yet", 31)
            }
        } else {
        if pget(desk, 376) > 0 {
            // The naming way (Stone 40): stands in the
            // list's place while it stands — the thing
            // being named shown whole (a note under the
            // name it stands under, with its words below,
            // so what a rename leaves alone stands in
            // sight; the picture under its name, with the
            // word that the picture itself will not
            // change), the name being typed standing as
            // it is typed (the notes block's tenth slot,
            // drawn by filenotetext as any slot is), the
            // measure said plainly, and the naming's own
            // word (the desk's 380) at the foot when it
            // has spoken: a refusal stands there in plain
            // words, and nothing has been changed.
            let nmway = pget(desk, 376)
            let nmsel = pget(desk, 192)
            if nmway == 3 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Give this picture a new name:", 7)
                if cellbyte(notes, 640) > 0 {
                    filenotetext(desk, notes, 10 * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
                } else {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), "A picture you painted", 15)
                }
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The picture itself will not change.", 7)
            } else {
                if nmway == 2 {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Make a copy of this note:", 7)
                } else {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Give this note a new name:", 7)
                }
                if cellbyte(notes, nmsel * 64 + 44) > 0 {
                    filenametext(desk, notes, nmsel * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
                } else {
                    filenotetext(desk, notes, nmsel * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
                }
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Its words:", 7)
                filenotetext(desk, notes, nmsel * 65536 + (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
            }
            if nmway == 2 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Type the copy's name, then press Enter:", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Type the new name, then press Enter:", 7)
            }
            filenotetext(desk, notes, 9 * 65536 + (gy + 9) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
            if nmway == 3 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 2), "A name can hold 43 letters at most.", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 2), "A name can hold 19 letters at most.", 7)
            }
            let nmword = pget(desk, 380)
            if nmword == 1 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "That name already stands. Pick another name.", 15)
            } else {
                if nmword == 2 {
                    if nmway == 2 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Write the copy's name first.", 15)
                    } else {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Write the new name first.", 15)
                    }
                } else {
                    if nmword == 3 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The notes page is full. Eight notes are kept there.", 15)
                    } else {
                        if nmway == 2 {
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Press Escape to make no copy.", 7)
                        } else {
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Press Escape to keep the name it has.", 7)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        } else {
        if pget(desk, 300) == 1 {
            // The asking (Stone 27): the window asks
            // before anything is let go — the plain
            // question, the picked note's own words, and
            // the two choices, the picked one white on
            // blue with the pick mark, as the window's
            // picks have always stood. The asking opens
            // with "Keep it" picked (the desk's 304
            // stands at 1), so Enter alone never lets a
            // note go.
            let asel = pget(desk, 192)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Let this note go for good?", 7)
            filenotetext(desk, notes, asel * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
            if pget(desk, 304) == 0 {
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 5) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, let it go", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Keep it", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, let it go", 7)
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 6) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Keep it", 31)
            }
        } else {
            let shown = 0
            if count == 0 {
                if haspic == 0 {
                    shown = 1
                    if keeping == 0 {
                        if pget(desk, 204) == 0 {
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "There is no disk fitted.", 7)
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Nothing is saved.", 7)
                        } else {
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This disk is not keeping your choices yet.", 7)
                            pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Nothing is saved on it.", 7)
                        }
                    } else {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No notes are kept on the disk yet.", 7)
                    }
                }
            }
            if shown == 0 {
                // The pick runs over the notes and then,
                // when a picture stands kept (Stone 26),
                // the picture itself: the pick at 'count'
                // is the picture's. The picture's entry
                // stands on the window's last row, below
                // the picked words, in plain words.
                let sel = pget(desk, 192)
                if sel > count {
                    sel = 0
                }
                if sel == count {
                    if haspic == 0 {
                        sel = 0
                    }
                }
                if keeping == 0 {
                    // A sitting's notes, listed under
                    // their honest heading (Stone 27):
                    // they are the page's notes all the
                    // same, and can be picked and let go
                    // here — they are never said to be
                    // saved.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Notes kept only for this sitting:", 7)
                } else {
                    if count > 0 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Notes kept on the disk:", 7)
                    } else {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "What is kept on the disk:", 7)
                    }
                }
                let i = 0
                while i < count {
                    let row = gy + 3 + i
                    // A note with a given name stands
                    // under its name (Stone 40); a note
                    // with none stands under its words,
                    // as it always has.
                    if i == sel {
                        pixink(desk, 1)
                        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, row * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                        pixline(desk, row * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                        if cellbyte(notes, i * 64 + 44) > 0 {
                            filenametext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 31)
                        } else {
                            filenotetext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 31)
                        }
                    } else {
                        pixline(desk, row * 256 + (gx + 2), " ", 7)
                        if cellbyte(notes, i * 64 + 44) > 0 {
                            filenametext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 7)
                        } else {
                            filenotetext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 7)
                        }
                    }
                    i = i + 1
                }
                if sel < count {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The note you picked:", 7)
                    // Naming the picked note (Stone 40):
                    // "Rename" and "Copy" stand at the
                    // foot, in white, left of the letting-
                    // go words; a press on one opens the
                    // naming way, above.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 24), "Rename", 15)
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 31), "Copy", 15)
                    // Letting the picked note go (Stone
                    // 27): the words stand at the foot's
                    // right, in white; a press on them —
                    // or Enter while the note stands
                    // picked — asks first, above.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 36), "Let this note go", 15)
                    filenotetext(desk, notes, sel * 65536 + (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
                } else {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The picture you picked:", 7)
                    // Looking at the kept picture (Stone
                    // 48): "Look at it" stands at the foot,
                    // in white, left of "Rename"; a press
                    // on it opens the picture window,
                    // where the picture stands big.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 26), "Look at it", 15)
                    // Naming the kept picture (Stone 40):
                    // "Rename" stands at the foot, in
                    // white, where the note's letting-go
                    // words stand; a press on it opens
                    // the naming way, above. The picture
                    // is offered no "Copy" — the disk
                    // keeps one picture, and a copy would
                    // need a second picture's place.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 36), "Rename", 15)
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Enter opens it in Paint. Look at it sees it big.", 15)
                }
                if haspic == 1 {
                    let prow = gy + 13
                    // The picture stands under its given
                    // name where one stands (Stone 40),
                    // in the block's eleventh slot; else
                    // under its plain words, as always.
                    if sel == count {
                        pixink(desk, 1)
                        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, prow * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                        pixline(desk, prow * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                        if cellbyte(notes, 640) > 0 {
                            filenotetext(desk, notes, 10 * 65536 + prow * 256 + (gx + 4), 31)
                        } else {
                            pixline(desk, prow * 256 + (gx + 4), "A picture you painted", 31)
                        }
                    } else {
                        pixline(desk, prow * 256 + (gx + 2), " ", 7)
                        if cellbyte(notes, 640) > 0 {
                            filenotetext(desk, notes, 10 * 65536 + prow * 256 + (gx + 4), 7)
                        } else {
                            pixline(desk, prow * 256 + (gx + 4), "A picture you painted", 7)
                        }
                    }
                }
                // The exchange with a USB stick (Stone
                // 49): on the window's last row, while a
                // thing stands picked — a note, or the
                // picture at the pick's end — the two
                // words "To the stick" and "From the
                // stick" stand in white; a press on one
                // copies the picked thing between the
                // keeping disk and a plugged-in stick,
                // and the exchange's own word (the
                // desk's 492) then stands on this row in
                // their place, in plain words, until the
                // pick moves. The row stands in the
                // list's view only: the naming way and
                // the letting-go asking own the window
                // while they stand, as they always have.
                let xpicked = 0
                if sel < count {
                    xpicked = 1
                }
                if haspic == 1 {
                    if sel == count {
                        xpicked = 1
                    }
                }
                if xpicked == 1 {
                    let xword = pget(desk, 492)
                    if xword == 0 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "To the stick", 15)
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 16), "From the stick", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 1 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Copied to the stick.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 2 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Brought in from the stick.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 3 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No stick stands in this computer.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 4 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "That name already stands on the stick.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 5 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The stick's notes page is full.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 6 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "A picture already stands on the stick.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 7 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The stick holds no note of that name.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 8 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No picture stands on the stick.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 9 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The stick is this computer's keeping.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 10 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The copy could not be made.", 15)
                    }
                    if xword == 11 {
                        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Brought in, but the disk would not keep it.", 15)
                    }
                }
            }
            // The making of a Cardinal (Stone 50): on
            // the window's new last row, below the
            // exchange's row, the words "Make this
            // computer a Cardinal" stand in white — or
            // the making's own word (the desk's 504) in
            // their place, once the making has spoken:
            // the done word, one of the plain refusals,
            // or the word that it is making, which
            // stands while the copy runs. The row stands
            // in the list's view only, like the
            // exchange's row, and stands whether or not
            // anything is kept or picked: the making
            // concerns the whole machine, not a kept
            // thing. The word is forgotten whenever the
            // pick moves, as the exchange's word is.
            let iword = pget(desk, 504)
            if iword == 0 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Make this computer a Cardinal", 15)
            }
            if iword == 1 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This computer is a Cardinal now.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 2 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No hard disk stands in this computer.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 3 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No stick stands in this computer.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 4 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This disk already carries a Cardinal.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 5 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This disk is too small to carry a Cardinal.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 6 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The copying did not finish.", 15)
            }
            if iword == 7 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Making this computer a Cardinal...", 15)
            }
            if iword == 8 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The stick does not carry Cardnull.", 15)
            }
        }
        }
        }
    }
}

// ---- the settings window (Stone 20) ----
//
// A fourth window, standing where the files window
// stands — the two are never forward at once, and the
// painting order below keeps each whole. Three choices
// are set here by clicking (Stone 28 grew it by one):
// the time zone, whether the welcome chime sounds, and
// the size of the desktop's words. Each choice stands
// as its own row; the one that truly stands is shown
// white on blue with the pick mark, exactly as the
// files window shows its pick, and a press of the
// pointer on a choice's row sets that choice (the
// pressing is the main loop's, reckoned from these same
// four measures). The window's standing lives in the
// desk at 208 to 216 (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, and
// the main body's facts copied in whenever they change
// — whether the chime stands on, and the clock's offset
// as the bar truly reckons it — with the keeping and the
// fitted standing shared from the files window's copy at
// 200 and 204, the same facts; the words' size is read
// from the desk's own cell measure at 320, which is the
// standing. When no disk is keeping the choices, the
// window says plainly that they last only for this
// sitting.

make sewide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make seleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 252)
}

make setop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 256)
}

make sehigh(desk) {
    // Thirteen rows through Stone 27; sixteen since
    // Stone 28 — the words' size choice (its label and
    // its two rows) stands below the chime's rows, and
    // the keeping word moved down by the same rows, so
    // nothing above it moved: the keeping word stands
    // at gy + 14 and its second line at gy + 15, the
    // window's last row, exactly filling the taller
    // window at its standing place.
    give 16
}

// Draws one of the settings window's choice rows: the
// row that truly stands is filled blue and its words
// stand white with the pick mark; the other stands in
// the window's quiet colors. The row and the words are
// named by 'place' — the row times 256 plus the column
// the words start at — as pixline packs its cell.
make sechoice(desk, place, text, picked) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let row = place / 256
    let col = place - row * 256
    if picked == 1 {
        pixink(desk, 1)
        pixfill(desk, (col - 4) * cs + 2, row * cs, sewide(desk) * cs - 4, cs)
        pixline(desk, row * 256 + (col - 2), ">", 31)
        pixline(desk, row * 256 + col, text, 31)
    } else {
        pixline(desk, row * 256 + col, text, 7)
    }
}

// Paints the settings window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar, the three choices with the standing one picked —
// the time zone from the offset the bar's clock truly
// reckons by, the chime from whether it truly sounds,
// and the words' size from the desk's cell measure —
// and at its foot the plain word of where the choices
// live: kept on the disk, or only for this sitting.
make onesettings(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = seleft(desk)
    let gy = setop(desk)
    let gw = sewide(desk)
    let gh = sehigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 208) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 4 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 8, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Settings")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Time zone:", 7)
        let eastern = 0
        if pget(desk, 216) != 0 {
            eastern = 1
        }
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Eastern time", eastern)
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 4) * 256 + (gx + 4), "World's standard time", 1 - eastern)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Welcome chime:", 7)
        let chon = pget(desk, 212)
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Chime on", chon)
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Chime off", 1 - chon)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 10) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Words:", 7)
        let large = 0
        if pget(desk, 320) > 16 {
            large = 1
        }
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Usual size", 1 - large)
        sechoice(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Large", large)
        if pget(desk, 200) == 1 {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Your choices are kept on the disk.", 7)
        } else {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Your choices last only for this sitting.", 7)
            if pget(desk, 204) == 0 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "There is no disk fitted.", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This disk is not keeping them yet.", 7)
            }
        }
    }
}

// ---- the editor window (Stone 21) ----
//
// A fifth window, standing where the files and settings
// windows stand — they are never forward at once, and
// the painting order below keeps each whole. Here a
// note is written and changed, not only by the word
// 'note': the window stands in two ways. In its picking
// way it lists the new-note entry first, then every
// kept note, the picked one white on blue with the pick
// mark, exactly as the files window shows its list; the
// arrow keys or a press of the pointer pick, and Enter
// takes the pick into the writing way. There the note's
// words stand as a draft — the note being changed is
// copied in whole, a new note starts empty — and the
// desktop's typing writes into the draft: the main loop
// hands the letters here (the notes block's ninth slot,
// a slot of the page's own shape), Backspace forgets the
// last one, and Enter keeps the draft back through the
// same store path the word 'note' uses. A note holds
// forty-three letters at most, and the writing way says
// so in plain words. The window's standing lives in the
// desk at 220 to 232 (pixface.nifla's ledger): open,
// the way it stands, the pick, and the note being
// changed; the count and the keeping it speaks of are
// the files window's copies at 196, 200 and 204, the
// same facts, so what it says of where notes live is
// what the files window says.

make edwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make edleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 260)
}

make edtop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 264)
}

make edhigh(desk) {
    give 14
}

// Paints the editor window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open in its picking way,
// its frame and title bar, the new-note entry and the
// kept notes with the picked one standing white on
// blue, and at its foot the plain word of where the
// notes live; open in its writing way, the draft's
// words as they stand, the measure of a note said
// plainly, and the word of where the kept note will
// live — on the disk, or only for this sitting.
make oneeditor(desk, notes) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = edleft(desk)
    let gy = edtop(desk)
    let gw = edwide(desk)
    let gh = edhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 220) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 5 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 8, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Editor")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        let count = pget(desk, 196)
        if pget(desk, 308) == 1 {
            // The asking (Stone 27): the same asking
            // the files window makes, in this window —
            // the plain question, the note's own words
            // (the note the desk's 316 names), and the
            // two choices, the picked one white on blue.
            // The asking opens with "Keep it" picked.
            let tgt = pget(desk, 316)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Let this note go for good?", 7)
            filenotetext(desk, notes, tgt * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
            if pget(desk, 312) == 0 {
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 5) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, let it go", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Keep it", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Yes, let it go", 7)
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 6) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Keep it", 31)
            }
        } else {
        if pget(desk, 224) == 1 {
            if pget(desk, 232) == 8 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Write your note:", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Change your note:", 7)
            }
            filenotetext(desk, notes, 8 * 65536 + (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), 15)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "A note can hold 43 letters at most.", 7)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Press Enter to keep the note.", 7)
            if pget(desk, 232) != 8 {
                // Letting the open note go (Stone 27):
                // the words stand under the keep word
                // while a kept note stands open; a press
                // on them asks first, above. A new note
                // has nothing to let go.
                pixline(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Let this note go", 15)
            }
            if pget(desk, 200) == 1 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Your note will be kept on the disk.", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Your note will be kept only for this sitting.", 7)
                if pget(desk, 204) == 0 {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 9) * 256 + (gx + 2), "There is no disk fitted.", 7)
                } else {
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 9) * 256 + (gx + 2), "This disk is not keeping notes yet.", 7)
                }
            }
        } else {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Pick a note, then press Enter to write it:", 7)
            let sel = pget(desk, 228)
            if sel > count {
                sel = 0
            }
            if sel == 0 {
                pixink(desk, 1)
                pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, (gy + 3) * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Write a new note", 31)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 3) * 256 + (gx + 4), "Write a new note", 7)
            }
            let i = 0
            while i < count {
                let row = gy + 4 + i
                if i + 1 == sel {
                    pixink(desk, 1)
                    pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, row * cs, gw * cs - 4, cs)
                    pixline(desk, row * 256 + (gx + 2), ">", 31)
                    filenotetext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 31)
                } else {
                    filenotetext(desk, notes, i * 65536 + row * 256 + (gx + 4), 7)
                }
                i = i + 1
            }
            if count == 0 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No notes are kept yet.", 7)
            }
            if pget(desk, 200) == 1 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Notes are kept on the disk.", 7)
            } else {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Notes are kept only for this sitting.", 7)
            }
            if sel >= 1 {
                if sel <= count {
                    // Letting the picked note go (Stone
                    // 27): the words stand at the foot's
                    // right while a kept note stands
                    // picked; a press on them asks first,
                    // above.
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 36), "Let this note go", 15)
                }
            }
        }
        }
    }
}

// ---- the paint window (Stone 24) ----
//
// A sixth window, standing where the files, settings,
// and editor windows stand. Its body is not the system's
// drawing at all: it is the paint feather's own cells,
// shown 1:1 exactly as the feather window shows the
// welcome feather's (the feather-paint fix's discipline:
// carried, never scaled). The feather paints the cells
// of its window's region — the text reckoning's rows 10
// to 20, columns 14 to 65 — and this painter lays the
// region's first cell at the window's second column and
// third row and counts on from there, one for one.
// Before the feather's first visit the body says so in
// plain words, as the feather window's does. The
// window's measures are reckoned here, like every
// window's, so its painter and the pointer's pressing
// can never disagree: fifty-four cells wide (the
// region's fifty-two, with a cell to spare at each
// side) and fourteen high (its title row, a breathing
// row, the region's eleven rows, and one row below).
// Its standing lives in the desk at 272 to 284
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, whether the paint
// feather has visited, and its place.

make pawide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 320) > 16 {
        // Large print (Stone 28): the feather window's
        // own reckoning (fwwide) — the frame grows with
        // the words, the carried cells inside it do not.
        if pget(desk, 28) >= 38 {
            give 36
        }
        give pget(desk, 28) - 2
    }
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make paleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 280)
}

make patop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 284)
}

make pahigh(desk) {
    give 14
}

// One byte of the paint feather's cell block, read by
// its address: the block's address stands in the desk
// at 288 (pixface.nifla's ledger), laid there at the
// face's rising — the shape the face's other blocks
// take (the letter shapes at 36, the arrow at 44),
// because a call can carry only six words and
// paintwindows' hands are already full with the desk,
// the feather's cells, and the notes.
make pcellbyte(desk: bytes, off: number) -> number {
    give floor(desk, off) {
        mov rax, [r10]
        mov rcx, qword ptr [rbx + 288]
        movzx eax, byte ptr [rcx + rax]
    }
}

// Paints the paint window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar and — after the paint feather has visited — the
// feather's own cells, carried 1:1: its invitation, its
// swatches, its named color, its Clear word, and its
// canvas with every mark the feather has laid in it.
make onepaint(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = paleft(desk)
    let gy = patop(desk)
    let gw = pawide(desk)
    let gh = pahigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 272) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 6 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 5, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Paint")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        if pget(desk, 276) == 1 {
            // The feather's cells, carried 1:1 at sixteen
            // pixels at either word size, as the feather
            // window's are (see onefeather, Stone 28).
            let gr = 10
            while gr < 21 {
                let gc = 14
                while gc < 66 {
                    let ch = pcellbyte(desk, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2)
                    let at = pcellbyte(desk, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2 + 1)
                    let fg = at - (at / 16) * 16
                    let bgv = (at / 16) - (at / 128) * 8
                    let ly = gy * cs + 2 * cs + (gr - 10) * 16
                    let lx = gx * cs + cs + (gc - 14) * 16
                    pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bgv * 4))
                    pixfill(desk, lx, ly, 16, 16)
                    if ch != 32 {
                        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
                        pixglyph(desk, lx, ly, ch, 2)
                    }
                    gc = gc + 1
                }
                gr = gr + 1
            }
        } else {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No paint feather has visited yet.", 7)
        }
    }
}

// ---- the verse window (Stone 29) ----
//
// A seventh window, standing where the files, settings,
// and editor windows stand. It shows one verse from the
// kept set in verses.nifla — the World English Bible's
// wording exactly, wrapped at the window's own width so
// the whole verse stands at either word size (Stone
// 28's measure, read like every painter reads it) —
// with its reference named plainly under it. Which
// verse stands is the desk's 336, laid by the main body
// when the window is opened, reckoned from the day
// (verseday in verses.nifla); the painter simply shows
// the verse that stands there. Its standing lives in
// the desk at 324 to 336 (pixface.nifla's ledger):
// open, its place, and the verse's place in the set.
// The window is fifty-four cells wide, like the files
// window's, and twelve high: its title row, a breathing
// row, up to seven wrapped rows of the verse, a
// breathing row, the reference's row, and one row below.

make vwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make vleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 328)
}

make vtop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 332)
}

make vhigh(desk) {
    give 12
}

// Paints the verse window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar, the day's verse wrapped word by word at the
// window's width — the answers printer's own wrapping
// reckoning (pixprintline in pixface.nifla), laid in
// the window's light ground — and the verse's
// reference under it, named plainly.
make oneverse(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = vleft(desk)
    let gy = vtop(desk)
    let gw = vwide(desk)
    let gh = vhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 324) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 7 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 5, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Verse")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        let idx = pget(desk, 336)
        let text = versetext(idx)
        let n = length(text)
        let width = gw - 4
        let r = gy + 2
        let start = 0
        let going = 1
        if n == 0 {
            going = 0
        }
        while going == 1 {
            if r > gy + 8 {
                going = 0
            } else {
                let end = start + width
                if end >= n {
                    end = n
                } else {
                    let j = end
                    let hunting = 1
                    while hunting == 1 {
                        if j <= start {
                            hunting = 0
                        } else {
                            if textbyte(text, j) == 32 {
                                hunting = 0
                            } else {
                                j = j - 1
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    if j > start {
                        end = j
                    }
                }
                pixpaper(desk, 7)
                pixspan(desk, start, end - start, 0)
                pixtext(desk, r, gx + 2, text)
                r = r + 1
                start = end
                if start < n {
                    if textbyte(text, start) == 32 {
                        start = start + 1
                    }
                }
                if start >= n {
                    going = 0
                }
            }
        }
        pixline(desk, (gy + 10) * 256 + (gx + 2), verseref(idx), 112)
    }
}

// ---- the calculator window (Stone 30) ----
//
// An eighth window, standing where the files, settings,
// and editor windows stand. Its body is not the system's
// drawing at all: it is the calculator feather's own
// cells, shown 1:1 exactly as the paint window shows the
// paint feather's (the feather-paint fix's discipline:
// carried, never scaled). The feather paints the cells
// of its window's region — the text reckoning's rows 10
// to 20, columns 14 to 65 — and this painter lays the
// region's first cell at the window's second column and
// third row and counts on from there, one for one.
// Before the feather's first visit the body says so in
// plain words, as the paint window's does. The window's
// measures are reckoned here, like every window's, so
// its painter and the pointer's pressing can never
// disagree: fifty-four cells wide (the region's
// fifty-two, with a cell to spare at each side) and
// fourteen high (its title row, a breathing row, the
// region's eleven rows, and one row below). Its
// standing lives in the desk at 340 to 356
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, whether the
// calculator feather has visited, its place, and the
// address of the feather's cell block at 356.

make cawide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 320) > 16 {
        // Large print (Stone 28): the paint window's own
        // reckoning (pawide) — the frame grows with the
        // words, the carried cells inside it do not.
        if pget(desk, 28) >= 38 {
            give 36
        }
        give pget(desk, 28) - 2
    }
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make caleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 348)
}

make catop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 352)
}

make cahigh(desk) {
    give 14
}

// The terminal window (Stone 38): the tenth window,
// and the desktop's own console — the typing line and
// its answers, which stood on the desktop's ground
// below the windows until now, live inside it. Its
// measures are reckoned here, like every window's,
// so its painter and the pointer's pressing can never
// disagree — and in pixface.nifla's termwide and
// termhigh from the same rule, where the answers'
// keeping is drawn: fifty-four cells wide and
// fourteen high (its title row, eleven rows of kept
// answers, the typing line's row, and its foot). Its
// standing lives in the desk at 404 to 416
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, its place, and the
// address of the answers' keeping block at 416.

make tewide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make tehigh(desk) {
    give 14
}

make teleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 408)
}

make tetop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 412)
}

// One byte of the calculator feather's cell block, read
// by its address: the block's address stands in the
// desk at 356 (pixface.nifla's ledger), laid there at
// the face's rising — the shape the paint cells' block
// takes at 288, for the same reason (paintwindows'
// hands are full).
make ccellbyte(desk: bytes, off: number) -> number {
    give floor(desk, off) {
        mov rax, [r10]
        mov rcx, qword ptr [rbx + 356]
        movzx eax, byte ptr [rcx + rax]
    }
}

// Paints the calculator window from the desk's
// standing: closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame
// and title bar and — after the calculator feather has
// visited — the feather's own cells, carried 1:1: its
// invitation, its sum's line, and its keys with every
// figure the feather has reckoned into them.
make onecalc(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = caleft(desk)
    let gy = catop(desk)
    let gw = cawide(desk)
    let gh = cahigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 340) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 8 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 10, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Calculator")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        if pget(desk, 344) == 1 {
            // The feather's cells, carried 1:1 at sixteen
            // pixels at either word size, as the paint
            // window's are (see onepaint, Stone 28).
            let gr = 10
            while gr < 21 {
                let gc = 14
                while gc < 66 {
                    let ch = ccellbyte(desk, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2)
                    let at = ccellbyte(desk, (gr * 80 + gc) * 2 + 1)
                    let fg = at - (at / 16) * 16
                    let bgv = (at / 16) - (at / 128) * 8
                    let ly = gy * cs + 2 * cs + (gr - 10) * 16
                    let lx = gx * cs + cs + (gc - 14) * 16
                    pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bgv * 4))
                    pixfill(desk, lx, ly, 16, 16)
                    if ch != 32 {
                        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
                        pixglyph(desk, lx, ly, ch, 2)
                    }
                    gc = gc + 1
                }
                gr = gr + 1
            }
        } else {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No calculator has visited yet.", 7)
        }
    }
}

// ---- the help window (Stone 31) ----
//
// A ninth window, standing where the files, settings,
// and editor windows stand. It tells how Cardnull works
// in plain words — what each window is, what each typed
// word does, how to move windows, open the menu, and
// rest the machine — in the kept words of
// helpwords.nifla: five short pages of eleven lines,
// each line at most thirty-six letters, the window's
// inner width at the large print size, so every word
// stands whole at both print sizes (Stone 28's measure,
// read like every painter reads it) and no line is ever
// cut at the window's edge. Which page stands is the
// desk's 364, laid by the main body when the window is
// opened and turned by a press on the window's foot
// and by the arrow keys (the main loop's doing); the
// painter simply shows the page that stands there. Its
// standing lives in the desk at 360 to 372
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, the page, and its
// place. The window is fifty-four cells wide, like the
// files window's, and sixteen high: its title row, a
// breathing row, the page's eleven lines, a breathing
// row, its foot — the page's own standing ("Page 1 of
// 5") at its left and the turning word at its right,
// More, or First page on the last page, standing white
// on blue as a choice stands — and one row below.

make hwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make hleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 368)
}

make htop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 372)
}

make hhigh(desk) {
    give 16
}

// Paints the help window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and title
// bar, the standing page's eleven lines in the window's
// light ground, and its foot — which page stands, and
// the word that turns it.
make onehelp(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = hleft(desk)
    let gy = htop(desk)
    let gw = hwide(desk)
    let gh = hhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 360) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 9 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 4, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Help")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        let page = pget(desk, 364)
        let li = 0
        while li < helplines() {
            let line = helptext(page * helplines() + li)
            if length(line) > 0 {
                pixline(desk, (gy + 2 + li) * 256 + (gx + 2), line, 112)
            }
            li = li + 1
        }
        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Page " + (page + 1) + " of " + helppages(), 112)
        let nav = "More"
        if page + 1 >= helppages() {
            nav = "First page"
        }
        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + gw - 2 - length(nav)), nav, 31)
    }
}

// ---- the Bible window (Stone 41) ----
//
// An eleventh window, standing where the files,
// settings, and editor windows stand. It is a READING
// window: where the verse window (Stone 29) shows one
// verse for the day, this window reads a passage at
// length — the whole book of Jonah, all four chapters
// and forty-eight verses, in the same World English
// Bible wording the verse window carries, from the
// kept text in bibletext.nifla. It opens on its
// contents page, which names the book and its
// translation and lists the four chapters as the
// plain-words choice of a passage; the book's pages
// follow, nine lines to a page, each chapter
// beginning on a fresh page, so every page stands
// under one passage's name — and the passage is
// named plainly at the window's head, in the words a
// person names it with ("Jonah 1"). The pages turn
// forward and back — by the arrow keys, by presses
// on the window's foot, and, on the contents page,
// by the chapter pick (the main loop's doings). Its standing lives
// in the desk at 424 to 440 (pixface.nifla's
// ledger): open, the page that stands, its place,
// and the contents page's chapter pick. The window
// is fifty-four cells wide, like the help window's,
// and sixteen high: its title row, the passage's
// row, a breathing row, the page's nine lines, two
// breathing rows, its foot — the turning word Back
// at its left (on a reading page), the page's
// standing ("Page 1 of 30") after it, and the
// forward word at its right (More, First page on
// the last page, Read on the contents page) — and
// one row below.

make bwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make bleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 432)
}

make btop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 436)
}

make bhigh(desk) {
    give 16
}

// The reading's wrap measure for the standing face:
// the window's inner width, and never wider than
// thirty-six letters.
make bibwidth(desk) {
    let w = bwide(desk) - 4
    if w > 36 {
        give 36
    }
    give w
}

// The end of the wrapped line of 'text' that begins
// at 'start' and must end by 'limit', wrapped at
// thirty-six letters — the reading's one wrap
// measure: the reading window's inner width —
// thirty-six letters at the usual print size, and
// the window's own narrower inner width at the large
// size, as the verse window wraps its verse by its
// window's width (Stone 29), so no line ever runs
// past the window's edge at either size, and the
// pagination is reckoned from the same measure the
// painting uses. The hunting is the answers
// printer's, as the verse window wraps: the line
// runs to the measure, or back to the last space
// within it; a word longer than the measure — no
// word of this book is — would be cut at the measure.
make bibspanend(text, start, limit, w) {
    let end = start + w
    if end >= limit {
        give limit
    }
    let j = end
    let hunting = 1
    while hunting == 1 {
        if j <= start {
            hunting = 0
        } else {
            if textbyte(text, j) == 32 {
                hunting = 0
            } else {
                j = j - 1
            }
        }
    }
    if j > start {
        give j
    }
    give end
}

// Where a verse begins in its chapter's kept text:
// the verses stand parted by a mark the book's words
// never use (the upright bar, 124), and a verse's
// place is found by counting the marks before it.
make bibverseoff(ch, vi) {
    let text = biblechaptertext(ch)
    let n = length(text)
    let pos = 0
    let count = 0
    while count < vi {
        let hunting = 1
        while hunting == 1 {
            if pos >= n {
                hunting = 0
            } else {
                if textbyte(text, pos) == 124 {
                    hunting = 0
                } else {
                    pos = pos + 1
                }
            }
        }
        pos = pos + 1
        count = count + 1
    }
    give pos
}

// How long a verse runs in its chapter's kept text:
// from its beginning to the next mark, or to the
// chapter's end.
make bibverselen(ch, vi) {
    let text = biblechaptertext(ch)
    let n = length(text)
    let pos = bibverseoff(ch, vi)
    let len = 0
    let hunting = 1
    while hunting == 1 {
        if pos + len >= n {
            hunting = 0
        } else {
            if textbyte(text, pos + len) == 124 {
                hunting = 0
            } else {
                len = len + 1
            }
        }
    }
    give len
}

// How many wrapped lines one verse takes, at the
// reading's measure.
make bibverselines(ch, vi, w) {
    let text = biblechaptertext(ch)
    let start = bibverseoff(ch, vi)
    let limit = start + bibverselen(ch, vi)
    let lines = 0
    let going = 1
    if start >= limit {
        going = 0
    }
    while going == 1 {
        let end = bibspanend(text, start, limit, w)
        lines = lines + 1
        start = end
        if start < limit {
            if textbyte(text, start) == 32 {
                start = start + 1
            }
        }
        if start >= limit {
            going = 0
        }
    }
    give lines
}

// How many lines a whole chapter takes: its verses'
// wrapped lines, and a blank line between verses.
make bibchapterlines(ch, w) {
    let vcount = biblechapterverses(ch)
    let lines = 0
    let vi = 0
    while vi < vcount {
        lines = lines + bibverselines(ch, vi, w)
        if vi < vcount - 1 {
            lines = lines + 1
        }
        vi = vi + 1
    }
    give lines
}

// How many pages a whole chapter takes, at nine
// lines to a page.
make bibchapterpages(ch, w) {
    give (bibchapterlines(ch, w) + 8) / 9
}

// How many pages stand in the reading window in
// all: the contents page, and the chapters' pages
// after it.
make bibpages(w) {
    let total = 1
    let ch = 1
    while ch <= 4 {
        total = total + bibchapterpages(ch, w)
        ch = ch + 1
    }
    give total
}

// The first page of a chapter (1 to 4): the contents
// page stands first, then the pages of the chapters
// before it.
make bibchapterpage(ch, w) {
    let page = 1
    let before = 1
    while before < ch {
        page = page + bibchapterpages(before, w)
        before = before + 1
    }
    give page
}

// Which chapter a page stands in (1 to 4; the
// contents page gives 0).
make bibpagechapter(page, w) {
    if page == 0 {
        give 0
    }
    let ch = 1
    let at = 1
    while ch <= 4 {
        if page < at + bibchapterpages(ch, w) {
            give ch
        }
        at = at + bibchapterpages(ch, w)
        ch = ch + 1
    }
    give 4
}

// Paints the Bible window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and
// title bar, the standing page's passage named at
// its head, and its nine lines in the window's light
// ground — on the contents page, its kept lines with
// the picked chapter's line standing white on blue,
// as a choice stands; on a reading page, the
// chapter's verses wrapped as they are painted (the
// verse window's own way, Stone 29), from the page's
// first line, a blank line between verses — and its
// foot.
make onebible(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = bleft(desk)
    let gy = btop(desk)
    let gw = bwide(desk)
    let gh = bhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 424) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 11 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 5, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Bible")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        let page = pget(desk, 428)
        let w = bibwidth(desk)
        if page == 0 {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 1) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Jonah", 112)
            let pick = pget(desk, 440)
            let li = 0
            while li < 9 {
                let line = biblecontents(li)
                if length(line) > 0 {
                    let attr = 112
                    if li == pick + 3 {
                        attr = 31
                    }
                    pixline(desk, (gy + 3 + li) * 256 + (gx + 2), line, attr)
                }
                li = li + 1
            }
        } else {
            let ch = bibpagechapter(page, w)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 1) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Jonah " + ch, 112)
            let firstline = (page - bibchapterpage(ch, w)) * 9
            let text = biblechaptertext(ch)
            let vcount = biblechapterverses(ch)
            let line = 0
            let vi = 0
            while vi < vcount {
                let start = bibverseoff(ch, vi)
                let limit = start + bibverselen(ch, vi)
                let going = 1
                if start >= limit {
                    going = 0
                }
                while going == 1 {
                    let end = bibspanend(text, start, limit, w)
                    if line >= firstline {
                        if line < firstline + 9 {
                            pixpaper(desk, 7)
                            pixspan(desk, start, end - start, 0)
                            pixtext(desk, gy + 3 + (line - firstline), gx + 2, text)
                        }
                    }
                    line = line + 1
                    start = end
                    if start < limit {
                        if textbyte(text, start) == 32 {
                            start = start + 1
                        }
                    }
                    if start >= limit {
                        going = 0
                    }
                }
                if vi < vcount - 1 {
                    line = line + 1
                }
                vi = vi + 1
            }
        }
        if page > 0 {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Back", 31)
        }
        let standing = "Page " + (page + 1) + " of " + bibpages(w)
        if gw < 40 {
            standing = "Page " + (page + 1)
        }
        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 8), standing, 112)
        let nav = "More"
        if page == 0 {
            nav = "Read"
        }
        if page + 1 >= bibpages(w) {
            nav = "First page"
        }
        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + gw - 2 - length(nav)), nav, 31)
    }
}

// The terminal window's painting (Stone 38): the
// desktop's console in a window of its own — its
// frame, its name, and the kept answers laid from
// their block by pixface's drawtermbody, in the
// answers' own colors. The typing line at its foot
// is the main loop's to draw (drawtyping), as it
// always has been; it is laid again at the next key
// after any repaint. Closed, the window's ground is
// washed like any window's.
make oneterminal(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = teleft(desk)
    let gy = tetop(desk)
    let gw = tewide(desk)
    let gh = tehigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 404) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 10 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 8, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Terminal")
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        drawtermbody(desk)
    }
}

// ---- the desktop's icons (Stone 38) ----
//
// The desktop carries its windows' icons down its
// left edge — a small picture and the window's own
// plain name — and a press on an icon opens its
// window, as the menu's choices and the typed words
// do. Eight icons, in the order Tyler named them:
// Files, Editor, Paint, Calculator, Verse, Help,
// Settings, Terminal. Each stands in a box three
// rows high beginning at row 1 — four rows apart on
// the tall grid, three on the short one, so the
// column ends above the bar at either size — its
// picture at the box's left (two cells wide) and its
// name beside the picture; the press reckoning in
// the main loop tries the same boxes (iconat shares
// this reckoning: the box's left column 1, its width
// thirteen cells — the longest name, Calculator,
// with its picture and a breathing cell).

// One icon's box top row: the icons stand four rows
// apart on the tall grid, three on the short one
// (large print) — eight boxes of three rows must end
// above the bar at either size, and on the short
// grid four rows apart would run the last icon into
// the bar's own rows.
make iconat(desk, ii) {
    if pget(desk, 32) >= 40 {
        give 1 + ii * 4
    }
    give 1 + ii * 3
}

// ---- the Shield window (Stone 42) ----
//
// A twelfth window, standing where the help and
// Bible windows stand. The guard it shows is not
// new: since Stone 5 the feather door
// (featherdoor.nifla) has read every feather's card
// and reckoned its bytes before any visit, and
// turned away in plain words any feather whose
// reckoning did not match its card — work done in
// the dark, in the door's own keeping. This window
// is that guard showing itself: it tells, in plain
// words, what the Shield does, and it tells the
// door's reckoning since the rising — how many
// feathers the door has let in, and how many it has
// turned away — from the desk's copies at 456 and
// 460, which the main body keeps at every receiving
// (the welcome feather's, the paint feather's, and
// the calculator feather's alike; the door reckons
// the card on every visit, so every visit is
// counted, an opening visit and a carried press
// alike). And it tells the Shield's two standing
// truths, which are the design's own: the Shield
// has no switch — nothing in this window, and
// nothing anywhere in the system, turns it off —
// and it sends nothing anywhere, for the system
// holds no network and no wire for sending, and
// nothing about the keeper leaves the machine.
// The window has no page to turn and no key of its
// own: it stands to be read. Its standing lives in
// the desk at 444 to 463 (pixface.nifla's ledger):
// open, and its place. The window is fifty-four
// cells wide, like the help window's, and eighteen
// high: its title row, a breathing row, its fifteen
// lines, and one row below.

make shwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 54
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make shleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 448)
}

make shtop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 452)
}

make shhigh(desk) {
    give 18
}

// Paints the Shield window from the desk's standing:
// closed, the plain desktop; open, its frame and
// title bar, and its plain words in the window's
// light ground — what the Shield does, the door's
// reckoning since the rising, and its two standing
// truths.
make oneshield(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = shleft(desk)
    let gy = shtop(desk)
    let gw = shwide(desk)
    let gh = shhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 444) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 12 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 6, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Shield")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The Shield stands on guard.", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 4) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Every feather is checked at the door before", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "it may visit: its card is read, its bytes are", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 6) * 256 + (gx + 2), "reckoned, and only one whose reckoning matches", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 7) * 256 + (gx + 2), "its card is let in. One that does not reckon", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 8) * 256 + (gx + 2), "true is turned away in plain words, and", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 9) * 256 + (gx + 2), "never runs.", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 11) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Feathers let in since this rising: " + pget(desk, 456), 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 12) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Feathers turned away: " + pget(desk, 460), 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 14) * 256 + (gx + 2), "The Shield has no switch. It cannot be turned", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 15) * 256 + (gx + 2), "off, and it sends nothing anywhere: nothing", 112)
        pixline(desk, (gy + 16) * 256 + (gx + 2), "about you leaves this machine.", 112)
    }
}

// ---- the picture window (Stone 48) ----
//
// A thirteenth window: the kept picture stands open
// big in it, so a keeper can actually look at it.
// The window's place and size are reckoned here,
// like every window's, so the painter and the
// pointer's pressing can never disagree. Its
// standing lives in the desk at 476 to 488
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): open, the kept picture
// block's address, and its place. The window is
// fifty cells wide on the wide grid — the canvas
// stands one cell in from its left edge, below —
// and sixteen rows high: its title row, the
// picture's fourteen rows of pixels, and one row
// below. On the narrow grid it stands as wide as
// the grid honestly holds, like the files window's
// measure, and the picture stands the same size in
// it: the drawing is counted in pixels from the
// window's corner, not in letter cells, so it
// stands whole at either word size.

make puwide(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 56 {
        give 50
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 2
}

make puleft(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 484)
}

make putop(desk) {
    give pget(desk, 488)
}

make puhigh(desk) {
    give 16
}

// Reads one byte of the kept picture's block, whose
// address the main body laid in the desk at 480 at
// the rising — the same road pcellbyte walks for
// the paint window's cells at 288, walked for the
// same reason: paintwindows' hands are already
// full, and the picture's painter needs the block
// without another word in the call.
make piccellbyte(desk: bytes, off: number) -> number {
    give floor(desk, off) {
        mov rax, [r10]
        mov ecx, dword ptr [rbx + 480]
        movzx eax, byte ptr [rcx + rax]
    }
}

// Paints the picture window from the desk's
// standing: closed, the plain desktop; open, its
// frame and title bar and the kept picture itself,
// cell by cell from its own keeping — the canvas
// the paint feather keeps (its cells at rows 14 to
// 20 and columns 16 to 63 of the feather's region,
// kept in the picture block at rows 4 to 10 and
// columns 2 to 49, a letter byte and a color byte
// to a cell, as diskkeep.nifla's telling and the
// main body's copies reckon). Every cell stands
// sixteen pixels wide and thirty-two tall here —
// twice the paint window's height for each cell —
// so the whole canvas stands 768 by 224 pixels,
// big enough to look at, one cell in from the
// window's left edge and directly under its title
// bar. A cell's letter stands in its cell at the
// paint window's own scale, centered in the
// cell's taller measure. With no picture kept,
// the window says so plainly in its body, and how
// one comes to stand here.
make onepicture(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let gx = puleft(desk)
    let gy = putop(desk)
    let gw = puwide(desk)
    let gh = puhigh(desk)
    if pget(desk, 476) == 0 {
        pixwashwin(desk, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    } else {
        let front = 0
        if pget(desk, 180) == 13 {
            front = 1
        }
        winframe(desk, gy * 256 + gx, gh * 256 + gw, front)
        pixspan(desk, 0, 7, 15)
        pixtext(desk, gy, gx + 1, "Picture")
        pixink(desk, 7)
        pixfill(desk, gx * cs + 2, gy * cs + cs + 2, gw * cs - 4, gh * cs - cs - 4)
        if pget(desk, 296) == 0 {
            pixline(desk, (gy + 2) * 256 + (gx + 2), "No picture stands kept yet.", 112)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 4) * 256 + (gx + 2), "Paint one in the paint window and keep it,", 112)
            pixline(desk, (gy + 5) * 256 + (gx + 2), "and it will stand here, big, to look at.", 112)
        } else {
            let rr = 0
            while rr < 7 {
                let cc = 0
                while cc < 48 {
                    let off = ((rr + 4) * 52 + (cc + 2)) * 2
                    let ch = piccellbyte(desk, off)
                    let at = piccellbyte(desk, off + 1)
                    let fg = at - (at / 16) * 16
                    let bgv = (at / 16) - (at / 128) * 8
                    let lx = gx * cs + cs + cc * 16
                    let ly = gy * cs + cs + rr * 32
                    pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + bgv * 4))
                    pixfill(desk, lx, ly, 16, 32)
                    if ch != 32 {
                        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + fg * 4))
                        pixglyph(desk, lx, ly + 8, ch, 2)
                    }
                    cc = cc + 1
                }
                rr = rr + 1
            }
        }
    }
}

// One icon's painting: its picture, then its name in
// white beside it. The pictures are drawn plainly, in
// the palette's own colors — a folder, a page, a
// painter's palette, a reckoning box, a book, a
// helping disc, a settings disc, and the terminal's
// own black box with its prompt mark.
make oneicon(desk, ii) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    let top = iconat(desk, ii)
    let x = 1 * cs
    let y = top * cs
    if ii == 0 {
        // Files: a gold folder.
        pixink(desk, 14)
        pixfill(desk, x + 2, y + 5, 2 * cs - 4, 2 * cs - 7)
        pixfill(desk, x + 2, y + 2, cs - 2, 4)
    }
    if ii == 1 {
        // Editor: a white page with two written lines.
        pixink(desk, 15)
        pixfill(desk, x + 4, y + 2, 2 * cs - 8, 2 * cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 8)
        pixfill(desk, x + 7, y + 8, 2 * cs - 14, 2)
        pixfill(desk, x + 7, y + 13, 2 * cs - 14, 2)
    }
    if ii == 2 {
        // Paint: a red palette disc with a blue pool.
        pixink(desk, 12)
        pixdisc(desk, x + cs, y + cs, cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 9)
        pixdisc(desk, x + cs + 3, y + cs - 2, 3)
    }
    if ii == 3 {
        // Calculator: a gray box with a pale reckoning line.
        pixink(desk, 8)
        pixfill(desk, x + 3, y + 2, 2 * cs - 6, 2 * cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 15)
        pixfill(desk, x + 6, y + 5, 2 * cs - 12, 5)
    }
    if ii == 4 {
        // Verse: a brown book with a pale page edge.
        pixink(desk, 6)
        pixfill(desk, x + 3, y + 2, 2 * cs - 6, 2 * cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 15)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 1, y + 2, 2, 2 * cs - 4)
    }
    if ii == 5 {
        // Help: a blue disc with its question mark.
        pixink(desk, 9)
        pixdisc(desk, x + cs, y + cs, cs - 4)
        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + 15 * 4))
        pixglyph(desk, x + cs - 8, y + cs - 8, 63, 2)
    }
    if ii == 6 {
        // Settings: a gray disc with a dark hub.
        pixink(desk, 8)
        pixdisc(desk, x + cs, y + cs, cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixdisc(desk, x + cs, y + cs, 4)
    }
    if ii == 7 {
        // Terminal: a black box with its prompt mark.
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, x + 2, y + 2, 2 * cs - 4, 2 * cs - 4)
        pset(desk, 56, pget(desk, 64 + 10 * 4))
        pixglyph(desk, x + 5, y + 5, 62, 2)
    }
    if ii == 8 {
        // Bible (Stone 41): a blue book with a gold
        // cross on its cover.
        pixink(desk, 9)
        pixfill(desk, x + 3, y + 2, 2 * cs - 6, 2 * cs - 4)
        pixink(desk, 14)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 1, y + 5, 2, 8)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 4, y + 8, 8, 2)
    }
    if ii == 9 {
        // Shield (Stone 42): a blue shield with a
        // gold cross on its face — its top standing
        // wide, its sides drawing in to the point
        // below.
        pixink(desk, 9)
        pixfill(desk, x + 3, y + 2, 2 * cs - 6, cs - 4)
        pixfill(desk, x + 5, y + cs - 2, 2 * cs - 10, cs - 6)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 3, y + 2 * cs - 8, 6, 6)
        pixink(desk, 14)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 1, y + 5, 2, 9)
        pixfill(desk, x + cs - 4, y + 8, 8, 2)
    }
    let name = ""
    if ii == 0 { name = "Files" }
    if ii == 1 { name = "Editor" }
    if ii == 2 { name = "Paint" }
    if ii == 3 { name = "Calculator" }
    if ii == 4 { name = "Verse" }
    if ii == 5 { name = "Help" }
    if ii == 6 { name = "Settings" }
    if ii == 7 { name = "Terminal" }
    if ii == 8 { name = "Bible" }
    if ii == 9 { name = "Shield" }
    pixspan(desk, 0, length(name), 15)
    // Stone 47: the name's paper is the icon's own —
    // the ground's black — set here, not inherited.
    // pixtext fills each letter's cell with the paper
    // standing in the desk, and until now oneicon
    // never set it: the name stood on whatever the
    // last painter before it had left standing — the
    // front chip's blue, most often — and the labels
    // changed color from pass to pass with no icon
    // pressed at all. (Every other pixtext caller
    // already sets its own: the window titles take
    // winframe's, the verse and Bible bodies set
    // theirs, and pixline sets its own within.)
    pixpaper(desk, 0)
    pixtext(desk, top + 1, 4, name)
}

// Every icon's painting — the desktop's ground
// furniture, laid before the windows in every whole
// repaint, so a window stands over the icons it
// covers and the icons stand whole where no window
// stands. (Ten icons since Stone 42: the Shield
// window's stands tenth, under the Bible's. On the
// short grid the tenth icon's picture and name stand
// clear of the bar as the others do; its press box's
// lowest row reaches the bar's own top row there,
// where the bar's presses are reckoned first, as
// the pointer's reckoning below tells.)
make oneicons(desk) {
    let ii = 0
    while ii < 10 {
        oneicon(desk, ii)
        ii = ii + 1
    }
}

// ---- the bar's window chips (Stone 39) ----
//
// The bottom bar shows the open windows: one
// plain-words presence chip to a window, carrying
// the window's own name — the names the desktop
// already uses, in the icons' order (Stone 38):
// Files, Editor, Paint, Calculator, Verse, Help,
// Settings, Terminal. (The welcome and feather
// windows keep no chip: they are the desktop's own
// first furniture, standing open from the rising
// under their title bars at the desktop's heart,
// and the bar's chips are for the eight windows a
// person opens and closes at the icons, the menu,
// and the typed words.) The front window's chip
// stands white on the bar's blue, as its title bar
// stands blue; a window standing behind stands
// white on light gray, as its title bar stands
// gray — the chip tells at the bar what the title
// bars tell on the desktop. A press on a chip
// raises its window to the front (the pointer's
// reckoning in the main loop), exactly as a press
// on the window itself does. No chip minimizes,
// and no chip opens a closed window: a window with
// no chip is not open, and the icons, the menu, and
// the words remain its openers.
//
// The chips are laid from the bar's upper row,
// beginning at its second cell (the start button
// stands in the first two), one blank cell between
// chips; when the upper row fills, the laying goes
// on at the lower row's middle ground, beginning
// at its tenth cell (the bar's name stands in the
// eight before it) and ending where the clock's
// ground begins, so no chip ever stands under the
// name, the button, or the clock, and the clock's
// own redrawing at every tick can never rub one
// out. At either word size the eight names, laid
// so, stand whole on the bar together — reckoned
// on the bench: fifty-one letters and seven blanks
// at the widest, against the upper row's sixty-two
// cells at the usual size and the two rows' room
// together at the large.
//
// The laying is reckoned once, here — chipplace —
// and the painter below and the pointer's pressing
// in the main loop both read it, the discipline
// the icons and the windows have kept: painter and
// pointer can never disagree about where a chip
// stands. The chips are painted at the end of
// every whole repaint (paintwindows), so they
// track every opening, closing, and coming-forward
// the standing records, and again after the few
// region repaints that move the forward place (the
// feather visits and the feather windows' own
// presses, Stones 34 and 35's road) — the bar is
// never inside a region road, for no window stands
// on it, so those repaints leave the chips to this
// painting alone.

// The window the chip at 'slot' speaks for, in the
// icons' order: the forward reckoning's own numbers.
make chipwin(slot) {
    if slot == 0 { give 3 }
    if slot == 1 { give 5 }
    if slot == 2 { give 6 }
    if slot == 3 { give 8 }
    if slot == 4 { give 7 }
    if slot == 5 { give 9 }
    if slot == 6 { give 4 }
    if slot == 7 { give 10 }
    if slot == 8 { give 11 }
    if slot == 9 { give 12 }
    give 13
}

// The name the chip at 'slot' carries: the window's
// own plain name, as its title bar and its icon
// speak it.
make chipname(slot) {
    if slot == 0 { give "Files" }
    if slot == 1 { give "Editor" }
    if slot == 2 { give "Paint" }
    if slot == 3 { give "Calculator" }
    if slot == 4 { give "Verse" }
    if slot == 5 { give "Help" }
    if slot == 6 { give "Settings" }
    if slot == 7 { give "Terminal" }
    if slot == 8 { give "Bible" }
    if slot == 9 { give "Shield" }
    give "Picture"
}

// The last column a chip on 'row' may reach (the
// first column past its ground): the grid's own
// edge on the bar's upper row (row 0); on the lower
// row (row 1), the clock's ground's first column —
// the fortieth from the right on a wide grid, the
// tenth on a narrow one, the ground drawclock
// fills.
make chiplimit(desk, row) {
    if row == 0 {
        give pget(desk, 28)
    }
    if pget(desk, 28) >= 48 {
        give pget(desk, 28) - 40
    }
    give pget(desk, 28) - 10
}

// Where the chip at 'slot' stands: its row on the
// bar (0 the upper, 1 the lower) times 256 plus its
// first column — or nothing (0 - 1) when its window
// stands closed, or when the bar has no room for it
// (a laying the eight names never meet, at either
// size, as the heading reckons). The laying packs
// the open windows' chips in the icons' order from
// the upper row's second cell, one blank cell
// between chips, going on at the lower row's tenth
// cell when the upper row fills.
make chipplace(desk, slot) {
    let row = 0
    let col = 2
    let s = 0
    while s <= slot {
        let win = chipwin(s)
        if winopen(desk, win) == 1 {
            let w = length(chipname(s))
            if col + w > chiplimit(desk, row) {
                row = 1
                col = 10
            }
            if s == slot {
                if col + w > chiplimit(desk, row) {
                    give 0 - 1
                }
                give row * 256 + col
            }
            col = col + w + 1
        } else {
            if s == slot {
                give 0 - 1
            }
        }
        s = s + 1
    }
    give 0 - 1
}

// Paints the bar's chips from the desk's standing:
// the chips' ground is first laid as the bar itself
// stands (pixface's pixchipground), so a window that
// closed leaves no plate behind and the chips that
// remain pack from the row's beginning again; then
// each open window's chip is laid at its reckoned
// place — the front window's white on blue, the
// others white on light gray, in the text face's
// own attribute reckoning the menu's words use.
make paintchips(desk) {
    pixchipground(desk)
    let s = 0
    while s < 11 {
        let place = chipplace(desk, s)
        if place >= 0 {
            let row = place / 256
            let col = place - row * 256
            let attr = 127
            if pget(desk, 180) == chipwin(s) {
                attr = 31
            }
            pixline(desk, (pget(desk, 32) - 2 + row) * 256 + col, chipname(s), attr)
        }
        s = s + 1
    }
}

// ---- the bar's menu (Stone 23) ----
//
// A press of the pointer on the top bar — the Cardnull
// bar — opens a small menu of plain words just under
// the bar, at the desktop's left: the five windows'
// names, one to a row — Welcome, Feather, Files,
// Settings, Editor, the names the desktop already
// gives them — and, since Stone 25, a sixth choice
// under them: Rest, which offers the machine's
// resting, exactly as the word 'rest' does. Since
// Stone 29 a sixth window choice, Verse, stands
// between Editor and Rest, and Rest stands last. Since
// Stone 30 a seventh window choice, Calculator, stands
// between Verse and Rest, and Rest stands last still.
// Since Stone 31 an eighth window choice, Help, stands
// between Calculator and Rest, and Rest stands last
// still. A press
// on a window choice opens that window (or brings it
// forward), exactly as the choice's typed word does —
// the feather's choice asks for the feather, as the
// word 'feather' does — and a press anywhere else, or
// the Escape key, puts the menu away without opening
// anything. The menu is desktop chrome: plain words
// in the pixel face, and no codes.
//
// The menu's place is reckoned once, here — its left,
// top, width, and height in letter cells — and the
// painter below and the pointer's pressing in the main
// loop both read those reckonings, the discipline the
// windows have kept since Stone 14: painter and
// pointer can never disagree about where the menu
// stands. Its standing lives in the pixel face's desk
// at 268 (pixface.nifla's ledger): 1 while it stands
// open. The menu stands above the bar at the left: its
// foot is the bar's top edge (Stone 33 — it rises from
// the start button), it is twelve
// cells wide — room for the longest name, Settings,
// with the frame's breathing room the windows keep —
// and fourteen cells high, one row to a choice
// (thirteen window choices, and Rest under them
// since Stone 25, standing last since Stone 29, one
// row lower again since Stone 30's Calculator choice
// took the seventh row, and one row lower again
// since Stone 31's Help choice took the eighth row,
// since Stone 41's Bible choice took the ninth,
// since Stone 42's Shield choice took the tenth,
// and since Stone 48's Picture choice took the
// eleventh). Since Stone 46 the choices stand in
// the desktop icons' own order — Welcome and
// Feather first, as they have always stood, then
// Files, Editor, Paint, Calculator, Verse, Help,
// Settings, Terminal, Bible, Shield, and Rest last —
// for Tyler's word at his Stone 44 witness: all the
// programs belong in the start menu. Paint and
// Terminal stood on the desktop from Stones 24 and 38
// with no row here; they have theirs now, Paint after
// Editor and Terminal after Settings, where they
// stand among their neighbors on the desktop, and
// Settings and the choices under it moved down to
// make their room — the menu's choices and the
// desktop's icons now name the same programs in the
// same order. The Picture choice (Stone 48) stands
// after Shield, where the newest window's choice
// has always stood — the append-before-Rest place
// Bible and Shield took before it — for the picture
// window carries no desktop icon whose order it
// could follow: the icons' column is full.

make menuleft(desk) {
    give 0
}

make menutop(desk) {
    // Stone 33: the menu rises from the start button at
    // the screen's foot — its foot stands on the bar's
    // top edge, so its top is the bar's two rows and its
    // own fourteen rows up from the grid's foot
    // (fourteen since Stone 48's Picture choice took
    // its row; thirteen since Stone 46's Paint and
    // Terminal choices took theirs; eleven since Stone
    // 42's Shield choice took the tenth row; ten since
    // Stone 41's Bible choice took the ninth).
    give pget(desk, 32) - 16
}

make menuwide(desk) {
    give 12
}

make menuhigh(desk) {
    give 14
}

// Wipes the menu's place back to the plain desktop:
// the ground the menu covered is filled black, and the
// caller paints the windows again over it, so whatever
// stood under the menu stands whole once more.
make menuwash(desk) {
    let cs = pget(desk, 320)
    pixink(desk, 0)
    pixfill(desk, menuleft(desk) * cs, menutop(desk) * cs, menuwide(desk) * cs, menuhigh(desk) * cs)
}

// Paints the bar's menu from the desk's standing: open,
// its gray edge and black body and the fourteen choices
// in white, one to a row — the desktop icons' own order
// since Stone 46, Welcome and Feather first and Rest
// last; closed, nothing — putting the menu away is
// menuwash's and the windows' painting's work.
make onemenu(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 268) == 1 {
        let ml = menuleft(desk)
        let mt = menutop(desk)
        let mw = menuwide(desk)
        let mh = menuhigh(desk)
        let cs = pget(desk, 320)
        pixink(desk, 8)
        pixfill(desk, ml * cs, mt * cs, mw * cs, mh * cs)
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, ml * cs + 2, mt * cs + 2, mw * cs - 4, mh * cs - 4)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 0) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Welcome", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 1) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Feather", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 2) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Files", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 3) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Editor", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 4) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Paint", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 5) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Calculator", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 6) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Verse", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 7) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Help", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 8) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Settings", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 9) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Terminal", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 10) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Bible", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 11) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Shield", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 12) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Picture", 15)
        pixline(desk, (mt + 13) * 256 + (ml + 1), "Rest", 15)
    }
}

// ---- the rest panel (Stone 25) ----
//
// Choosing Rest — by the word 'rest', or by the bar
// menu's Rest choice — opens a small panel of plain
// words at the desktop's middle, offering the resting's
// two choices, one to a row under its plain heading:
// Shut down, and Restart, and a third row, Stay, which
// simply puts the panel away and rests nothing. A press
// on a choice's row does that choice (through the one
// resting doing in the main loop, where the disk is
// flushed first); a press anywhere else, or the Escape
// key, puts the panel away, as the menu is put away.
// The panel is desktop chrome: plain words in the pixel
// face, and no codes.
//
// The panel's place is reckoned once, here — its left,
// top, width, and height in letter cells, centered on
// the desktop the face's grid names — and the painter
// below and the pointer's pressing in the main loop
// both read those reckonings, the discipline the menu
// and the windows have kept: painter and pointer can
// never disagree about where the panel stands. Its
// standing lives in the pixel face's desk at 292
// (pixface.nifla's ledger): 1 while it stands open.
// The panel is sixteen cells wide — room for the
// longest choice, Shut down, with the frame's breathing
// room — and four cells high: its heading, its two
// choices, and Stay.

make restleft(desk) {
    give (pget(desk, 28) - restwide(desk)) / 2
}

make resttop(desk) {
    give (pget(desk, 32) - resthigh(desk)) / 2
}

make restwide(desk) {
    give 16
}

make resthigh(desk) {
    give 4
}

// Wipes the panel's place back to the plain desktop:
// the ground it covered is filled black, and the caller
// paints the windows again over it, so whatever stood
// under the panel stands whole once more.
make restwash(desk) {
    // The panel's place and its shadow's, wiped whole —
    // the windows' own wash (Stone 33).
    pixwashwin(desk, restleft(desk), resttop(desk), restwide(desk), resthigh(desk))
}

// Paints the rest panel from the desk's standing: open,
// its gray edge and black body and its plain words in
// white — the heading Rest, then Shut down, Restart,
// and Stay, one to a row; closed, nothing.
make onerest(desk) {
    if pget(desk, 292) == 1 {
        let rl = restleft(desk)
        let rt = resttop(desk)
        let rw = restwide(desk)
        let rh = resthigh(desk)
        let cs = pget(desk, 320)
        pixshadow(desk, rl * cs, rt * cs, rw * cs, rh * cs)
        pixink(desk, 8)
        pixfill(desk, rl * cs, rt * cs, rw * cs, rh * cs)
        pixink(desk, 0)
        pixfill(desk, rl * cs + 2, rt * cs + 2, rw * cs - 4, rh * cs - 4)
        pixline(desk, (rt + 0) * 256 + (rl + 1), "Rest", 15)
        pixline(desk, (rt + 1) * 256 + (rl + 1), "Shut down", 15)
        pixline(desk, (rt + 2) * 256 + (rl + 1), "Restart", 15)
        pixline(desk, (rt + 3) * 256 + (rl + 1), "Stay", 15)
    }
}

// Paints the windows: the closed ones first (each is
// painted as the plain desktop it covers), then the open
// ones behind, and the forward one last, so the forward
// window always stands whole. Called whenever the
// windows' standing changes: at the desktop's rising, on
// a clearing of the face, and on every opening, closing,
// and bringing-forward.
make paintwindows(desk, block, notes) {
    if pget(desk, 172) == 0 {
        onewelcome(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 176) == 0 {
        onefeather(desk, block)
    }
    if pget(desk, 188) == 0 {
        onefiles(desk, notes)
    }
    if pget(desk, 208) == 0 {
        onesettings(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 220) == 0 {
        oneeditor(desk, notes)
    }
    if pget(desk, 272) == 0 {
        onepaint(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 324) == 0 {
        oneverse(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 340) == 0 {
        onecalc(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 360) == 0 {
        onehelp(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 404) == 0 {
        oneterminal(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 424) == 0 {
        onebible(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 444) == 0 {
        oneshield(desk)
    }
    if pget(desk, 476) == 0 {
        onepicture(desk)
    }
    // The desktop's invitation (Stone 47): the
    // ground's own lines are laid again here, after
    // the closed windows' washes — a closed window
    // standing over the lines has just washed its
    // place, lines and all — and before the icons,
    // so the Help icon's disc overdraws the first
    // line's first letter as at the rising. The open
    // windows painted below stand over the lines as
    // they always have.
    paintground(desk)
    // The desktop's icons (Stone 38) are laid after
    // the closed windows' washes and before the open
    // windows: a closed window has no ground presence
    // — its wash must not rub the icons out — and an
    // open window stands over the icons it covers, as
    // a window stands over the ground.
    oneicons(desk)
    let f = pget(desk, 180)
    if f != 1 {
        if pget(desk, 172) == 1 {
            onewelcome(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 2 {
        if pget(desk, 176) == 1 {
            onefeather(desk, block)
        }
    }
    if f != 3 {
        if pget(desk, 188) == 1 {
            onefiles(desk, notes)
        }
    }
    if f != 4 {
        if pget(desk, 208) == 1 {
            onesettings(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 5 {
        if pget(desk, 220) == 1 {
            oneeditor(desk, notes)
        }
    }
    if f != 6 {
        if pget(desk, 272) == 1 {
            onepaint(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 7 {
        if pget(desk, 324) == 1 {
            oneverse(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 8 {
        if pget(desk, 340) == 1 {
            onecalc(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 9 {
        if pget(desk, 360) == 1 {
            onehelp(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 10 {
        if pget(desk, 404) == 1 {
            oneterminal(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 11 {
        if pget(desk, 424) == 1 {
            onebible(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 12 {
        if pget(desk, 444) == 1 {
            oneshield(desk)
        }
    }
    if f != 13 {
        if pget(desk, 476) == 1 {
            onepicture(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 1 {
        if pget(desk, 172) == 1 {
            onewelcome(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 2 {
        if pget(desk, 176) == 1 {
            onefeather(desk, block)
        }
    }
    if f == 3 {
        if pget(desk, 188) == 1 {
            onefiles(desk, notes)
        }
    }
    if f == 4 {
        if pget(desk, 208) == 1 {
            onesettings(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 5 {
        if pget(desk, 220) == 1 {
            oneeditor(desk, notes)
        }
    }
    if f == 6 {
        if pget(desk, 272) == 1 {
            onepaint(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 7 {
        if pget(desk, 324) == 1 {
            oneverse(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 8 {
        if pget(desk, 340) == 1 {
            onecalc(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 9 {
        if pget(desk, 360) == 1 {
            onehelp(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 10 {
        if pget(desk, 404) == 1 {
            oneterminal(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 11 {
        if pget(desk, 424) == 1 {
            onebible(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 12 {
        if pget(desk, 444) == 1 {
            oneshield(desk)
        }
    }
    if f == 13 {
        if pget(desk, 476) == 1 {
            onepicture(desk)
        }
    }
    // The bar's menu stands over everything (Stone 23):
    // while it stands open it is painted last, so no
    // window's painting can cover it.
    if pget(desk, 268) == 1 {
        onemenu(desk)
    }
    // The rest panel stands over everything too (Stone
    // 25), painted after the menu: the two never stand
    // open together — the menu's Rest choice closes the
    // menu as it opens the panel — and the panel is the
    // newer asking, so it stands on top.
    if pget(desk, 292) == 1 {
        onerest(desk)
    }
    // The bar's window chips (Stone 39) are painted
    // last of all: they stand on the bar, which no
    // window, menu, or panel covers, and painting them
    // here — at every whole repaint, which is every
    // opening, closing, and coming-forward — keeps
    // them always telling the standing as it is.
    paintchips(desk)
}

// ---- the region repaint (Stone 34) ----
//
// The first fix of the four glitches Tyler witnessed in
// UTM (Nov 16, 2026): "when i move windows it flickers
// the windows." The mechanism stood plain in the old
// road: dragging a window one cell called the
// whole-desktop repaint — every closed window washed,
// every open window redrawn whole, straight into the
// picture screen with no back buffer — once per pointer
// packet, many times a second. The eye saw the wash and
// the redraw of windows that had nothing to do with the
// move; that wash-redraw shimmer is the flicker. (The
// bench reckoned it too: one six-step drag of a window
// burned 11,765 of the runtime's million loop passes,
// nearly all of it repainting untouched windows.)
//
// The fix is scope, not speed: a move repaints only the
// road the window travelled — the ground it left and
// the ground it took, taken as one stretch of cells —
// and, within that road, only the windows whose places
// touch it, painted in the standing order (closed
// fellows washed in window order, open fellows drawn in
// window order, the forward window last), so the road's
// final pixels are exactly what the whole-desktop
// repaint would have laid there. A window outside the
// road is never washed and never redrawn, so it cannot
// flicker. Two honest edges: an open window standing
// where a closed fellow's wash will fall joins the set
// (the wash would rub a hole in it that nothing
// repaints), and while the start menu or the rest panel
// stands open — both paint over everything in their own
// words — the whole-desktop repaint keeps its say.
//
// The window callpainter is to paint stands in the
// desk at 400, laid by paintregion before each call —
// the machine's registers carry six words at most, and
// callpainter's three blocks already fill them.

// callpainter paints the window the desk's 400 names,
// its standing way: the wash for a closed one, the
// whole face for an open one.
make callpainter(desk, block, notes) {
    let win = pget(desk, 400)
    if win == 1 {
        onewelcome(desk)
    }
    if win == 2 {
        onefeather(desk, block)
    }
    if win == 3 {
        onefiles(desk, notes)
    }
    if win == 4 {
        onesettings(desk)
    }
    if win == 5 {
        oneeditor(desk, notes)
    }
    if win == 6 {
        onepaint(desk)
    }
    if win == 7 {
        oneverse(desk)
    }
    if win == 8 {
        onecalc(desk)
    }
    if win == 9 {
        onehelp(desk)
    }
    if win == 10 {
        oneterminal(desk)
    }
    if win == 11 {
        onebible(desk)
    }
    if win == 12 {
        oneshield(desk)
    }
    if win == 13 {
        onepicture(desk)
    }
}

// rectstouchx and rectstouchy answer whether two
// stretches of cells reach across each other, one
// measure at a time — the machine's registers carry
// six words at most, so the two measures are asked
// apart and the caller believes both.
make rectstouchx(al, aw, bl, bw) {
    let hit = 0
    if al + aw > bl {
        if al < bl + bw {
            hit = 1
        }
    }
    give hit
}

make rectstouchy(at, ah, bt, bh) {
    let hit = 0
    if at + ah > bt {
        if at < bt + bh {
            hit = 1
        }
    }
    give hit
}

// winbit answers window win's bit in a set reckoned as
// one number: window 1 is 1, window 2 is 2, and so on,
// doubling — nine windows, nine bits, no block needed.
make winbit(win) {
    let bit = 1
    let step = 1
    while step < win {
        bit = bit * 2
        step = step + 1
    }
    give bit
}

// inset answers whether window win's bit stands in the
// set number: its share of the set, halved away, leaves
// 1 standing exactly when the bit is set.
make inset(set, win) {
    let share = set / winbit(win)
    let half = share / 2
    give share - half * 2
}

// The road the region repaint serves stands in the
// desk at 384 to 396, laid by layroad before the call
// — the machine's registers carry six words at most,
// and paintregion's three blocks already fill them, so
// the road travels the way the paint cells' block
// does at 288: through the desk.

// layroad lays the road (in cells) the next region
// repaint will serve, into the desk at 384 to 396.
make layroad(desk, rx, ry, rw, rh) {
    pset(desk, 384, rx)
    pset(desk, 388, ry)
    pset(desk, 392, rw)
    pset(desk, 396, rh)
}

// layroadwins lays the region repaint's road over a
// window's own place — and, when a second window is
// named and stands open, over the stretch of cells
// holding both windows' places together (Stone 35).
// A feather visit changes only its own window's
// cells, but the visit's coming forward changes the
// window it took the forward place from as well: that
// window's title bar must be repainted in its behind
// colors, and it stands inside the road when the two
// places are taken together. A second window of 0
// names none.
make layroadwins(desk, wina, winb) {
    let lx = winleft(desk, wina)
    let ty = wintop(desk, wina)
    let rx = lx + winwide(desk, wina)
    let by = ty + winhigh(desk, wina)
    if winb >= 1 {
        if winb != wina {
            if winopen(desk, winb) == 1 {
                let bx = winleft(desk, winb)
                let byy = wintop(desk, winb)
                if bx < lx {
                    lx = bx
                }
                if byy < ty {
                    ty = byy
                }
                let brx = bx + winwide(desk, winb)
                let bby = byy + winhigh(desk, winb)
                if brx > rx {
                    rx = brx
                }
                if bby > by {
                    by = bby
                }
            }
        }
    }
    layroad(desk, lx, ty, rx - lx, by - ty)
}

// paintregion repaints the stretch of the desktop the
// laid road names: the road is washed bare, then every
// window whose place — with one cell of grace at its
// right and bottom, for its shadow's reach — touches
// the road is painted its standing way, in the standing
// order. Windows outside the road are not touched at
// all — unless the pass's own writing reaches them
// (Stone 45's closure, below): a pass writes farther
// than the road, and every window its writing falls
// on is repainted in the same pass, so no window is
// ever left with a writing standing on it and no body
// over it. Stone 47 brings the ground's own writing
// under the same rule: the desktop's invitation lines
// the road touches are the pass's writing too — they
// are laid again after the wash, and a window
// standing over a touched line joins the set, so its
// body lands over the line in the same pass.
make paintregion(desk, block, notes) {
    let rx = pget(desk, 384)
    let ry = pget(desk, 388)
    let rw = pget(desk, 392)
    let rh = pget(desk, 396)
    let full = 0
    if pget(desk, 268) == 1 {
        full = 1
    }
    if pget(desk, 292) == 1 {
        full = 1
    }
    if full == 1 {
        paintwindows(desk, block, notes)
    } else {
        let set = 0
        let win = 1
        while win <= 13 {
            let wl = winleft(desk, win)
            let wt = wintop(desk, win)
            let ww = winwide(desk, win)
            let wh = winhigh(desk, win)
            if rectstouchx(wl, ww + 1, rx, rw) == 1 {
                if rectstouchy(wt, wh + 1, ry, rh) == 1 {
                    if inset(set, win) == 0 {
                        set = set + winbit(win)
                    }
                }
            }
            win = win + 1
        }
        // An open window standing where a closed
        // fellow's wash will fall joins the set, or the
        // wash would rub a hole in it.
        win = 1
        while win <= 13 {
            if inset(set, win) == 1 {
                if winopen(desk, win) == 0 {
                    let cl = winleft(desk, win)
                    let ct = wintop(desk, win)
                    let cw = winwide(desk, win)
                    let ch = winhigh(desk, win)
                    let other = 1
                    while other <= 13 {
                        if winopen(desk, other) == 1 {
                            if inset(set, other) == 0 {
                                let ql = winleft(desk, other)
                                let qt = wintop(desk, other)
                                let qw = winwide(desk, other)
                                let qh = winhigh(desk, other)
                                if rectstouchx(ql, qw, cl, cw + 1) == 1 {
                                    if rectstouchy(qt, qh, ct, ch + 1) == 1 {
                                        set = set + winbit(other)
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        other = other + 1
                    }
                }
            }
            win = win + 1
        }
        // Stone 47: the ground's invitation joins the
        // pass's reckoning, before anything is washed.
        // A line the road touches is laid again after
        // the wash (below): the wash rubs out the part
        // of it the road crosses, and until now no
        // repaint ever laid it again — the lines stood
        // rubbed out in patches for the rest of the
        // sitting. Which lines are touched is reckoned
        // here, from the road and the lines' own
        // places (groundrow and groundwide), and the
        // closure below takes the reckoning up.
        let g0 = 0
        let g1 = 0
        if rectstouchx(2, groundwide(desk, 0), rx, rw) == 1 {
            if rectstouchy(groundrow(desk, 0), 1, ry, rh) == 1 {
                g0 = 1
            }
        }
        if rectstouchx(2, groundwide(desk, 1), rx, rw) == 1 {
            if rectstouchy(groundrow(desk, 1), 1, ry, rh) == 1 {
                g1 = 1
            }
        }
        // Stone 45: the body-always-lands closure. A
        // pass writes farther than the road it serves:
        // the road is washed bare below; every icon the
        // road touches is laid again whole, all three
        // of its rows and its name beside its picture,
        // wherever that span stands; and every window
        // the pass repaints is painted whole — frame,
        // body, and the shadow's reach past its right
        // and foot — with no clipping anywhere in the
        // face. So a window standing where any of those
        // writings fall, but not itself in the set,
        // would keep the writing standing on it with no
        // body over it: the frame's gray undercoat
        // showing where its words should stand, an
        // icon's name stamped across its body — the
        // broken glass Tyler witnessed in UTM (Nov 24,
        // 2026), its state right and its picture wrong,
        // healed only when some later whole repaint
        // happened by. The set is therefore closed
        // before anything is washed: a window whose
        // place meets a re-laid icon's whole span, or
        // the place-and-shadow of a window already in
        // the set, joins the set — and the closing
        // repeats until no window is added, since a
        // joined window's own painting writes just as
        // far. Every window a pass writes on is then
        // repainted in that same pass, after the icons,
        // in the standing order — so its body always
        // lands.
        let changed = 1
        while changed == 1 {
            changed = 0
            let cicon = 0
            while cicon < 10 {
                let ctop = iconat(desk, cicon)
                if rectstouchx(1, 13, rx, rw) == 1 {
                    if rectstouchy(ctop, 3, ry, rh) == 1 {
                        win = 1
                        while win <= 13 {
                            if inset(set, win) == 0 {
                                if rectstouchx(winleft(desk, win), winwide(desk, win), 1, 13) == 1 {
                                    if rectstouchy(wintop(desk, win), winhigh(desk, win), ctop, 3) == 1 {
                                        set = set + winbit(win)
                                        changed = 1
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                            win = win + 1
                        }
                    }
                }
                cicon = cicon + 1
            }
            // Stone 47: a window standing over a
            // touched invitation line joins the set,
            // the icon pass's own shape — the line is
            // laid whole after the wash, across the
            // window's place too, and the window's
            // body must land over it in the same pass.
            let gline = 0
            while gline < 2 {
                let gtouch = g0
                if gline == 1 {
                    gtouch = g1
                }
                if gtouch == 1 {
                    win = 1
                    while win <= 13 {
                        if inset(set, win) == 0 {
                            if rectstouchx(winleft(desk, win), winwide(desk, win), 2, groundwide(desk, gline)) == 1 {
                                if rectstouchy(wintop(desk, win), winhigh(desk, win), groundrow(desk, gline), 1) == 1 {
                                    set = set + winbit(win)
                                    changed = 1
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        win = win + 1
                    }
                }
                gline = gline + 1
            }
            let member = 1
            while member <= 13 {
                if inset(set, member) == 1 {
                    let el = winleft(desk, member)
                    let et = wintop(desk, member)
                    let ew = winwide(desk, member)
                    let eh = winhigh(desk, member)
                    win = 1
                    while win <= 13 {
                        if win != member {
                            if inset(set, win) == 0 {
                                if rectstouchx(winleft(desk, win), winwide(desk, win), el, ew + 1) == 1 {
                                    if rectstouchy(wintop(desk, win), winhigh(desk, win), et, eh + 1) == 1 {
                                        set = set + winbit(win)
                                        changed = 1
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        win = win + 1
                    }
                }
                member = member + 1
            }
        }
        // The road itself washed bare, the way a closed
        // window's wash leaves its own place.
        pixwashwin(desk, rx, ry, rw, rh)
        // The invitation's touched lines are laid
        // again on the washed ground (Stone 47),
        // before the road's icons — the ground's
        // furniture in the rising's own order.
        if g0 == 1 {
            groundline(desk, 0)
        }
        if g1 == 1 {
            groundline(desk, 1)
        }
        // The desktop's icons standing in the road are
        // laid again on the washed ground (Stone 38),
        // before the road's windows — the icons are the
        // ground's furniture, and a window stands over
        // the icons it covers.
        let ii = 0
        while ii < 10 {
            let itop = iconat(desk, ii)
            if rectstouchx(1, 13, rx, rw) == 1 {
                if rectstouchy(itop, 3, ry, rh) == 1 {
                    oneicon(desk, ii)
                }
            }
            ii = ii + 1
        }
        win = 1
        while win <= 13 {
            if inset(set, win) == 1 {
                if winopen(desk, win) == 0 {
                    pset(desk, 400, win)
                    callpainter(desk, block, notes)
                }
            }
            win = win + 1
        }
        win = 1
        while win <= 13 {
            if inset(set, win) == 1 {
                if winopen(desk, win) == 1 {
                    if win != pget(desk, 180) {
                        pset(desk, 400, win)
                        callpainter(desk, block, notes)
                    }
                }
            }
            win = win + 1
        }
        let fwd = pget(desk, 180)
        if fwd >= 1 {
            if fwd <= 13 {
                if inset(set, fwd) == 1 {
                    if winopen(desk, fwd) == 1 {
                        pset(desk, 400, fwd)
                        callpainter(desk, block, notes)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// dragstep paints one step of a window's drag
// (Stone 45): the ground the window is leaving is
// washed bare — its frame's place and its shadow's,
// the wash pixwashwin has always made — and the
// icons standing in that ground are laid again; the
// window is set at its new place and painted there,
// standing forward as it has since the hold was
// taken, so its painting over whatever it now covers
// is the true picture. No other window is painted.
// Until this stone a drag step repainted, whole,
// every window touching the road of the ground left
// and taken together — so bystanders were washed and
// redrawn again and again as the window passed, the
// flicker Tyler saw in UTM. Now the windows a step
// uncovers stand washed until the button is let go,
// when the drop's one whole repaint (Stone 47 —
// Stones 34 and 45 paid a region repaint over the
// drag's gathered road here) makes the glass whole
// again. A step's whole work is the leaving
// ground and the dragged window itself. The step's
// errand — which window, and the place it is going
// to — travels in the desk at 464 to 475, the way
// the region repaint's road travels at 384: the
// call's three blocks already fill the machine's
// registers. The Drag Mend (November 27, 2026):
// the ground washed is the place the window's
// painting stands (the desk at 508 to 515), not
// the window's standing place — during a packet
// flood the standing place runs ahead of the
// painting (the packets move it silently, and one
// step is paid at the pass's end), so the wash
// must reckon from where the painting truly is,
// and the step lays the painted place forward to
// the place it has just painted.
make dragstep(desk, block, notes) {
    let win = pget(desk, 464)
    let newleft = pget(desk, 468)
    let newtop = pget(desk, 472)
    let ol = pget(desk, 508)
    let ot = pget(desk, 512)
    let dw = winwide(desk, win)
    let dh = winhigh(desk, win)
    pixwashwin(desk, ol, ot, dw, dh)
    let ii = 0
    while ii < 10 {
        let itop = iconat(desk, ii)
        if rectstouchx(1, 13, ol, dw) == 1 {
            if rectstouchy(itop, 3, ot, dh) == 1 {
                oneicon(desk, ii)
            }
        }
        ii = ii + 1
    }
    winmove(desk, win, newleft, newtop)
    pset(desk, 400, win)
    callpainter(desk, block, notes)
    pset(desk, 508, newleft)
    pset(desk, 512, newtop)
}

// ---- window dragging (Stone 22) ----
//
// A window is moved by dragging its title bar with the
// pointer: a press on the bar (not on its close mark)
// takes hold of the window, the window follows the
// pointer while the button stands held, and letting
// the button go sets it down. A window's place stays
// whole on the desktop: its left stands between the
// desktop's left edge and the place where its right
// edge meets the desktop's right edge, and its top
// between the desktop's top edge and the place where
// its bottom meets the bar's top edge (Stone 33: the
// bar stands at the foot, so the desktop a window may
// stand on is the grid less the bar's two rows) —
// simply clamped at the edges, in letter cells, the
// measure every window's place is reckoned in. The new
// place lasts for the sitting; nothing writes it anywhere.
//
// The reckoning stays single-sourced, the discipline
// every window has kept: a window's place is read
// through the one set of measures below, which stand
// on the same reckonings the painters use — each
// window's place in the desk (the welcome's at 140 to
// 152, the others' left and top at 236 to 264, laid at
// the face's rising from the places their measures
// first reckon), and their own wide/high reckonings.
// The pointer's
// pressing in the main loop — the drag's taking hold,
// its following, and every content press after a drag
// — reads a window's place only through these, so
// painter and pointer can never disagree about where
// a dragged window stands.

// Whether window 'win' stands open (1 the welcome, 2
// the feather, 3 the files, 4 the settings, 5 the
// editor, 6 the paint window, 7 the verse window, 8
// the calculator window, 9 the help window — the
// forward reckoning's own numbers).
make winopen(desk, win) {
    if win == 1 { give pget(desk, 172) }
    if win == 2 { give pget(desk, 176) }
    if win == 3 { give pget(desk, 188) }
    if win == 4 { give pget(desk, 208) }
    if win == 5 { give pget(desk, 220) }
    if win == 6 { give pget(desk, 272) }
    if win == 7 { give pget(desk, 324) }
    if win == 8 { give pget(desk, 340) }
    if win == 9 { give pget(desk, 360) }
    if win == 10 { give pget(desk, 404) }
    if win == 11 { give pget(desk, 424) }
    if win == 12 { give pget(desk, 444) }
    if win == 13 { give pget(desk, 476) }
    give 0
}

// Window 'win''s left column, top row, width, and
// height, in letter cells — each from the window's own
// reckoning, dragged move and all.
make winleft(desk, win) {
    if win == 1 { give pget(desk, 140) }
    if win == 2 { give fwleft(desk) }
    if win == 3 { give fileft(desk) }
    if win == 4 { give seleft(desk) }
    if win == 5 { give edleft(desk) }
    if win == 6 { give paleft(desk) }
    if win == 7 { give vleft(desk) }
    if win == 8 { give caleft(desk) }
    if win == 9 { give hleft(desk) }
    if win == 10 { give teleft(desk) }
    if win == 11 { give bleft(desk) }
    if win == 12 { give shleft(desk) }
    if win == 13 { give puleft(desk) }
    give 0
}

make wintop(desk, win) {
    if win == 1 { give pget(desk, 144) }
    if win == 2 { give fwtop(desk) }
    if win == 3 { give fitop(desk) }
    if win == 4 { give setop(desk) }
    if win == 5 { give edtop(desk) }
    if win == 6 { give patop(desk) }
    if win == 7 { give vtop(desk) }
    if win == 8 { give catop(desk) }
    if win == 9 { give htop(desk) }
    if win == 10 { give tetop(desk) }
    if win == 11 { give btop(desk) }
    if win == 12 { give shtop(desk) }
    if win == 13 { give putop(desk) }
    give 0
}

make winwide(desk, win) {
    if win == 1 { give pget(desk, 148) }
    if win == 2 { give fwwide(desk) }
    if win == 3 { give fiwide(desk) }
    if win == 4 { give sewide(desk) }
    if win == 5 { give edwide(desk) }
    if win == 6 { give pawide(desk) }
    if win == 7 { give vwide(desk) }
    if win == 8 { give cawide(desk) }
    if win == 9 { give hwide(desk) }
    if win == 10 { give tewide(desk) }
    if win == 11 { give bwide(desk) }
    if win == 12 { give shwide(desk) }
    if win == 13 { give puwide(desk) }
    give 0
}

make winhigh(desk, win) {
    if win == 1 { give pget(desk, 152) }
    if win == 2 { give fwhigh(desk) }
    if win == 3 { give fihigh(desk) }
    if win == 4 { give sehigh(desk) }
    if win == 5 { give edhigh(desk) }
    if win == 6 { give pahigh(desk) }
    if win == 7 { give vhigh(desk) }
    if win == 8 { give cahigh(desk) }
    if win == 9 { give hhigh(desk) }
    if win == 10 { give tehigh(desk) }
    if win == 11 { give bhigh(desk) }
    if win == 12 { give shhigh(desk) }
    if win == 13 { give puhigh(desk) }
    give 0
}

// Sets window 'win' down at a new place (its left
// column and top row, in letter cells): the window's
// place in the desk is written — the welcome's at 140
// and 144, where its painting has always read it, and
// each other window's at its own pair (236 to 264),
// where its left/top reckonings read it — so the one
// place stands for painter and pointer alike.
make winmove(desk, win, newleft, newtop) {
    if win == 1 {
        pset(desk, 140, newleft)
        pset(desk, 144, newtop)
    }
    if win == 2 {
        pset(desk, 236, newleft)
        pset(desk, 240, newtop)
    }
    if win == 3 {
        pset(desk, 244, newleft)
        pset(desk, 248, newtop)
    }
    if win == 4 {
        pset(desk, 252, newleft)
        pset(desk, 256, newtop)
    }
    if win == 5 {
        pset(desk, 260, newleft)
        pset(desk, 264, newtop)
    }
    if win == 6 {
        pset(desk, 280, newleft)
        pset(desk, 284, newtop)
    }
    if win == 7 {
        pset(desk, 328, newleft)
        pset(desk, 332, newtop)
    }
    if win == 8 {
        pset(desk, 348, newleft)
        pset(desk, 352, newtop)
    }
    if win == 9 {
        pset(desk, 368, newleft)
        pset(desk, 372, newtop)
    }
    if win == 10 {
        pset(desk, 408, newleft)
        pset(desk, 412, newtop)
        // Stone 47: the console's rows follow their
        // window — the answers' first row (128) and
        // last row (132) and the typing line's row
        // (136), reckoned from the new top exactly as
        // layplaces reckons them from the first place.
        // Until now a move left them standing where
        // layplaces first laid them, and the terminal's
        // painter, which lays its kept answers at
        // those rows at the window's live left, stamped
        // them below the dragged window's foot forever
        // — over ground, icons, and other windows —
        // where no wash could ever reach them, every
        // wash in the system being reckoned from the
        // window's place. The terminal was the only
        // window whose painter read a place its move
        // did not write.
        pset(desk, 128, newtop + 1)
        pset(desk, 132, newtop + tehigh(desk) - 3)
        pset(desk, 136, newtop + tehigh(desk) - 2)
    }
    if win == 11 {
        pset(desk, 432, newleft)
        pset(desk, 436, newtop)
    }
    if win == 12 {
        pset(desk, 448, newleft)
        pset(desk, 452, newtop)
    }
    if win == 13 {
        pset(desk, 484, newleft)
        pset(desk, 488, newtop)
    }
}

// Lays every window's first place on the face's grid, as
// the face's rising lays them: each window centered, the
// feather at the layout's row 14 and the files, settings,
// editor, and paint windows at its row 17 — each top
// clamped so the window stands whole on the grid, which
// a narrower grid (large print, Stone 28) can require of
// the taller windows. The welcome's place is pixbegin's
// own, laid there from the layout at every beginning.
// The main body calls this at the face's rising, and
// again whenever the words' size is switched in the
// settings window, when the whole desktop is re-laid on
// the new grid and the windows return to their first
// places.
make layplaces(desk) {
    pset(desk, 236, (pget(desk, 28) - fwwide(desk)) / 2)
    let ft = layrow(desk, 14)
    if ft + fwhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        ft = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - fwhigh(desk)
    }
    if ft < 0 {
        ft = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 240, ft)
    pset(desk, 244, (pget(desk, 28) - fiwide(desk)) / 2)
    let it = layrow(desk, 17)
    if it + fihigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        it = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - fihigh(desk)
    }
    if it < 0 {
        it = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 248, it)
    pset(desk, 252, (pget(desk, 28) - sewide(desk)) / 2)
    let st = layrow(desk, 17)
    if st + sehigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        st = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - sehigh(desk)
    }
    if st < 0 {
        st = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 256, st)
    pset(desk, 260, (pget(desk, 28) - edwide(desk)) / 2)
    let et = layrow(desk, 17)
    if et + edhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        et = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - edhigh(desk)
    }
    if et < 0 {
        et = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 264, et)
    pset(desk, 280, (pget(desk, 28) - pawide(desk)) / 2)
    let pt2 = layrow(desk, 17)
    if pt2 + pahigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        pt2 = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - pahigh(desk)
    }
    if pt2 < 0 {
        pt2 = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 284, pt2)
    pset(desk, 328, (pget(desk, 28) - vwide(desk)) / 2)
    let vt = layrow(desk, 17)
    if vt + vhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        vt = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - vhigh(desk)
    }
    if vt < 0 {
        vt = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 332, vt)
    pset(desk, 348, (pget(desk, 28) - cawide(desk)) / 2)
    let ct = layrow(desk, 17)
    if ct + cahigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        ct = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - cahigh(desk)
    }
    if ct < 0 {
        ct = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 352, ct)
    pset(desk, 368, (pget(desk, 28) - hwide(desk)) / 2)
    let ht = layrow(desk, 17)
    if ht + hhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        ht = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - hhigh(desk)
    }
    if ht < 0 {
        ht = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 372, ht)
    // The Bible window's place (Stone 41), laid like
    // the help window's — centered, at the layout's
    // row 17, clamped whole onto the grid.
    pset(desk, 432, (pget(desk, 28) - bwide(desk)) / 2)
    let bt2 = layrow(desk, 17)
    if bt2 + bhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        bt2 = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - bhigh(desk)
    }
    if bt2 < 0 {
        bt2 = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 436, bt2)
    // The Shield window's place (Stone 42), laid like
    // the Bible window's — centered, at the layout's
    // row 17, clamped whole onto the grid.
    pset(desk, 448, (pget(desk, 28) - shwide(desk)) / 2)
    let zt = layrow(desk, 17)
    if zt + shhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        zt = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - shhigh(desk)
    }
    if zt < 0 {
        zt = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 452, zt)
    // The picture window's place (Stone 48), laid like
    // the Shield window's — centered, at the layout's
    // row 17, clamped whole onto the grid.
    pset(desk, 484, (pget(desk, 28) - puwide(desk)) / 2)
    let ut2 = layrow(desk, 17)
    if ut2 + puhigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        ut2 = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - puhigh(desk)
    }
    if ut2 < 0 {
        ut2 = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 488, ut2)
    // The terminal window's place (Stone 38), and the
    // console's rows laid inside it: the answers' first
    // row (128) just under its title, their last row
    // (132) one above the typing line's row (136),
    // which stands at the window's foot. Until now
    // those rows named the answers strip on the
    // desktop's ground; the strip is the window now,
    // and every reader of 128, 132, and 136 — the
    // answers' keeping, the scroll, the typing line's
    // drawing — finds the window's rows there.
    pset(desk, 408, (pget(desk, 28) - tewide(desk)) / 2)
    let tt = layrow(desk, 17)
    if tt + tehigh(desk) > pget(desk, 32) - 2 {
        tt = pget(desk, 32) - 2 - tehigh(desk)
    }
    if tt < 0 {
        tt = 0
    }
    pset(desk, 412, tt)
    pset(desk, 128, tt + 1)
    pset(desk, 132, tt + tehigh(desk) - 3)
    pset(desk, 136, tt + tehigh(desk) - 2)
}

// ---- the machine ----

// The first breath: lift what the loader brought into the
// system's own keeping, before anything else is allocated
// (the discipline bootmods.nifla's note tells in full).
// First the module
// table's addresses, into plain numbers — the disc carries
// four modules; up to four are read.
let nmods = modcount()
let s0 = 0
let l0 = 0
let s1 = 0
let l1 = 0
let s2 = 0
let l2 = 0
let s3 = 0
let l3 = 0
if nmods > 0 {
    s0 = modstart(0)
    l0 = modend(0) - s0
}
if nmods > 1 {
    s1 = modstart(1)
    l1 = modend(1) - s1
}
if nmods > 2 {
    s2 = modstart(2)
    l2 = modend(2) - s2
}
if nmods > 3 {
    s3 = modstart(3)
    l3 = modend(3) - s3
}

// In the same first breath, the loader's answer to the
// kernel's video request (Stone 9) is read into plain
// numbers, for the same reason: the information stands in
// low memory the heap grows into. fbflags' twelfth bit
// (4096) says a picture screen was granted; the rest is
// its shape, read by bootmods' mbwordat and mbbyteat.
let fbflags = mbwordat(0)
let fbaddrlo = mbwordat(88)
let fbaddrhi = mbwordat(92)
let fbpitch = mbwordat(96)
let fbwidth = mbwordat(100)
let fbheight = mbwordat(104)
let fbbpp = mbbyteat(108)
let fbtype = mbbyteat(109)
let fbrpos = mbbyteat(112)
let fbgpos = mbbyteat(114)
let fbbpos = mbbyteat(116)

// What the lift gathers: the clock's offset (Eastern, -5,
// unless the disc's settings carry a choice), and the
// feather itself — the whole module as it rode the disc,
// its card at its head and its program after, once found.
let offset = 0 - 5
let featherimg = makebytes(0)
let feathergot = 0

let mi = 0
while mi < nmods {
    let s = 0
    let l = 0
    if mi == 0 { s = s0  l = l0 }
    if mi == 1 { s = s1  l = l1 }
    if mi == 2 { s = s2  l = l2 }
    if mi == 3 { s = s3  l = l3 }
    if l == 512 {
        let blk = makebytes(512)
        let c = modcopyfrom(s, l, blk)
        if blk[0] == 67 {
            if blk[1] == 78 {
                if blk[2] != 0 {
                    offset = blk[2] - 64
                }
            }
        }
    }
    mi = mi + 1
}

// The feather itself, found by the card at its own head: a
// module whose first bytes spell the card's first line is
// the feather, card and program together (Nifla Stone 20:
// feathers come from the compiler with their cards on
// them). The whole module is lifted; the door reads the
// card from its head when the feather is asked for. (A
// module longer than the doorstep plus a card's room
// cannot be a feather for this door, and is stepped over:
// a feather's card is a few hundred bytes of plain text.)
let paintimg = makebytes(0)
let paintgot = 0
let calcimg = makebytes(0)
let calcgot = 0
let mi2 = 0
while mi2 < nmods {
    let s = 0
    let l = 0
    if mi2 == 0 { s = s0  l = l0 }
    if mi2 == 1 { s = s1  l = l1 }
    if mi2 == 2 { s = s2  l = l2 }
    if mi2 == 3 { s = s3  l = l3 }
    if l > 19 {
        if l <= 525312 {
            let blk = makebytes(l)
            let c = modcopyfrom(s, l, blk)
            if feathermark(blk, c) {
                // Three feathers ride the disc since
                // Stone 30, told apart by the name on
                // the card: the paint feather (its card
                // names it — paintmark) and the
                // calculator feather (calcmark) are kept
                // apart, and every other carded feather
                // is the welcome feather the word
                // 'feather' receives, as it always has
                // been. The name only routes the module;
                // each feather is still let in by its
                // own card's reckoning at the door, or
                // not at all.
                if paintmark(blk, c) {
                    paintimg = blk
                    paintgot = c
                } else {
                    if calcmark(blk, c) {
                        calcimg = blk
                        calcgot = c
                    } else {
                        featherimg = blk
                        feathergot = c
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    mi2 = mi2 + 1
}

// Remembers for real (Stone 8): if a hard disk is fitted,
// it is asked for its card before anything is drawn — a
// disk that has been given over to the system's keeping
// (see the 'setup' word in the loop) holds the time choice
// in preference to the disc's settings, which have just
// been read above. A disk that is fitted but not yet the
// system's is remembered here too, so the desktop can
// offer it plainly; a machine with no disk simply carries
// on, and the disc's choice rules as it always has.
//
// The notes page (Stone 15): eight kept lines of words,
// each in its own sixty-four bytes of one block — the
// length at the slot's first byte, the letters after —
// and how many stand kept. The page is this body's to
// keep; diskkeep.nifla only carries it to the disk and
// back. The block carries a ninth slot besides (Stone
// 21), at its end: the editor window's draft, in the
// same shape, holding the words being written or changed
// in the window until Enter keeps them into the page
// itself through the same store path. The ninth slot is
// never the disk's: notesave and notesload carry the
// eight kept slots and no more, by the count they are
// handed. Since Stone 40 the block carries two more
// slots in the same shape, neither of them a note: the
// tenth, at 576, holds the name being typed in the
// files window's naming way, and the eleventh, at 640,
// holds the kept picture's name as the main body keeps
// it — read back at boot by picnameload and handed to
// every picsave, so the picture's name stands in this
// body's keeping as the picture itself does. A kept
// note's own name needs no slot of theirs: it lives in
// the note's own slot's tail (diskkeep.nifla's
// heading), and rides the page through notesave and
// notesload with the note.

// The keeping's two hands (Stone 43): every keeping
// call in this body goes through one of these, and
// each hands the call to the disk that keeps — the
// hard disk's library (diskkeep.nifla) when 'keepusb'
// stands 0, the stick's twin of it (usbstick.nifla)
// when it stands 1. The two libraries keep one layout
// in two carriages, and the callers below neither know
// nor need to know which carriage carried a call; the
// choice is made once, at the rising, where the desks
// are taken. (picsave alone has no wrapper: its own
// words already fill a call's six, and the desk and
// the choice would overflow it, so its two call
// sites branch by hand, saying the same choice —
// and on the stick's side the saving stands in two
// makings, upichead and upicbody, for the same
// bound, as usbstick.nifla tells.)
make ksavedoffset(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give usavedoffset(xdesk)
    }
    give savedoffset()
}

make ksavedchime(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give usavedchime(xdesk)
    }
    give savedchime()
}

make ksavedsize(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give usavedsize(xdesk)
    }
    give savedsize()
}

make krememberall(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, offset: number, chime: number, size: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give urememberall(xdesk, offset, chime, size)
    }
    give rememberall(offset, chime, size)
}

make kremember(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, offset: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give uremember(xdesk, offset)
    }
    give remember(offset)
}

make knotesload(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, block: bytes) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give unotesload(xdesk, block)
    }
    give notesload(block)
}

make knotesave(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, block: bytes, count: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give unotesave(xdesk, block, count)
    }
    give notesave(block, count)
}

make kpicload(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, block: bytes) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give upicload(xdesk, block)
    }
    give picload(block)
}

make kpicnameload(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, block: bytes, off: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give upicnameload(xdesk, block, off)
    }
    give picnameload(block, off)
}

make kdisksetup(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number, offset: number, chime: number, size: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give udisksetup(xdesk, offset, chime, size)
    }
    give disksetup(offset, chime, size)
}

make kflush(xdesk: bytes, keepusb: number) -> number {
    if keepusb == 1 {
        give uflush(xdesk)
    }
    give ataflush()
}

let notesblock = makebytes(704)
let notecount = 0

// Ears on the modern USB road (Stone 32): a second USB
// driver's whole estate, in the same pattern — one desk
// of bytes taken here and handed to its library
// (xhcikey.nifla) at every asking. The raising walks the
// bus for an xHCI controller, and wakes a keyboard if one
// stands on a port. When no such controller stands in
// the machine, or no keyboard is plugged into it, the
// road is simply quiet and the other roads carry the
// typing, exactly as before; neither USB road disables
// the other, and neither disables the PS/2 road. (The
// raising stands here, ahead of the keeping's reading
// below, because Stone 43's stick is raised on the same
// controller and the keeping must know its standing
// before it reads; and the desk is taken at 98304
// bytes, larger than Stone 32's taking, so the stick's
// estate stands clear of this road's fields at every
// alignment, as usbstick.nifla's heading tells.)
let xdesk = makebytes(98304)
let xup = xhinit(xdesk)
let twodesk = makebytes(64)
let fontblock = makebytes(0)

// The USB stick in the running system (Stone 43): the
// mass-storage device the same bus may hold, raised by
// usbstick.nifla on the controller the raising above
// set walking — its estate laid in the same desk, on
// ground that road never touches (the desk is taken
// larger for it, above). It comes home with the
// stick's sector count, or 0 when the bus holds no
// stick the running system can carry.
let ustick = ustart(xdesk)

// The keeping's disk (Stone 8, and Stone 43): a hard
// disk fitted in the machine keeps its place, as it
// always has; when none stands fitted and a stick
// stands raised, the stick is the keeping's disk, and
// 'keepusb' says so to every keeping call below (the
// k— wrappers just above the notes block's taking hand
// each call to the disk that keeps). 'diskup' keeps
// its meaning through both: a disk stands fitted for
// the keeping, of whichever kind stands.
let diskup = diskhere()
let keepusb = 0
if diskup == 0 {
    if ustick > 0 {
        keepusb = 1
        diskup = 1
    }
}
let diskset = 0
if diskup == 1 {
    let stored = ksavedoffset(xdesk, keepusb)
    if stored != 0 {
        offset = stored - 64
        diskset = 1
    }
}
// The chime's standing (Stone 20): it sounds, unless a
// keeping disk's card says it stands off — the card's
// chime byte (diskkeep.nifla) is read with the offset
// above, from the same card. A card laid before the
// chime had a byte of its own carries 0 there, and the
// chime stands on, as it always has.
let chimeon = 1
if diskset == 1 {
    if ksavedchime(xdesk, keepusb) == 2 {
        chimeon = 0
    }
}
// The words' size standing (Stone 28): usual size,
// unless a keeping disk's card says the words stand
// large — the card's size byte (diskkeep.nifla) is read
// with the offset and the chime above, from the same
// card, in the same preference order. A card laid
// before the size had a byte of its own carries 0
// there, and the words stand at their usual size, as
// they always have. 'wordsize' is 0 for usual size and
// 1 for large; the face's cell measure is laid from it
// when the face rises below (sixteen pixels, or
// twenty-four), and the settings window's clicks move
// it from there.
let wordsize = 0
if diskset == 1 {
    if ksavedsize(xdesk, keepusb) == 2 {
        wordsize = 1
    }
}
if diskset == 1 {
    // A keeping disk's notes are read back at boot, so
    // they stay (Stone 15).
    notecount = knotesload(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock)
}

// The kept picture (Stone 26): the paint feather's
// painted region as the disk keeps it — eleven rows of
// fifty-two cells, a letter byte and a color byte to a
// cell, 1144 bytes in a block of 1536 — read back at
// boot from a keeping disk (diskkeep.nifla's picload),
// and laid back into the paint window's cells when the
// paint feather's opening visit stands, so the picture
// stands again. 'pichas' says whether a picture stands
// kept; 'picsel' is the chosen-color figure the feather
// had standing when the picture was kept ('0' to '5',
// the letter at its cells' first cell). The picture is
// this body's keeping, as the notes page is; the
// feather never touches the disk.
let picblock = makebytes(1536)
// The exchange's scratch (Stone 49): the stick's
// notes page and kept picture are read into blocks
// of their own while a copy is made between the
// keeping disk and the stick, so the body's own
// keeping above is never the copy's scratch — a
// copy that fails midway leaves the keeping as it
// stood, and a copy that lands is laid into the
// keeping whole, by the exchange's own presses in
// the main loop below.
let sticknotes = makebytes(704)
let stickpic = makebytes(1536)
// The making's scratch (Stone 50): a bare sector's
// block for the installing in the main loop below —
// the sector in flight between the stick and the
// disk — so a copying never flies through the body's
// own keeping blocks above, as the exchange's
// scratch stands apart for the same reason. (The
// proof and the record-laying carry their second
// block inside themselves; a call can carry only so
// many words.)
let copyblock = makebytes(512)
let pichas = 0
let picsel = 48
if diskset == 1 {
    let picret = kpicload(xdesk, keepusb, picblock)
    if picret != 0 {
        pichas = 1
        picsel = picret
        // The picture's name (Stone 40), read back with
        // the picture into the notes block's eleventh
        // slot, where this body keeps it.
        let pnm = kpicnameload(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, 640)
    }
}

// The pixel face (Stone 9): when the loader granted a
// picture screen of the shape this face can draw on — a
// direct-color screen, thirty-two bits a pixel, standing
// at an address that is really an address (a grant whose
// framebuffer address is nothing at all is a hollow
// grant, refused here as the doormen now refuse it
// there — the witness fix), its whole address under
// four billion, and room enough for the desktop's
// words — the face of light is prepared: its desk, the
// font's letters lifted into a block, and a scratch
// page for reaching a screen that stands above the
// waking map. When nothing was granted, or the grant
// is not of that shape, pix stays 0 and the text face —
// the system's first face — carries the desktop, with a
// plain line at boot saying why.
let pix = 0
// The feather door's reckoning (Stone 42): how many
// feathers the door has let in since the rising, and
// how many it has turned away for a card that did not
// reckon true — kept here in the main body at every
// receiving, and copied into the desk (at 456 and
// 460) whenever they change, so the Shield window's
// painting can tell the reckoning plainly, the
// discipline the files window's facts have always
// kept. A rising with no feather on the disc (the
// door's 'no feather' answer) is neither a letting
// in nor a turning away, and is counted as neither.
let shieldin = 0
let shieldout = 0
let pixdesk = makebytes(0)
let cellblock = makebytes(0)
let paintblock = makebytes(0)
let calcblock = makebytes(0)
let termblock = makebytes(0)
if fbflags - (fbflags / 8192) * 8192 >= 4096 {
    if fbtype == 1 {
        if fbbpp == 32 {
            if fbaddrhi == 0 {
                if fbaddrlo != 0 {
                    if fbwidth >= 768 {
                        if fbheight >= 256 {
                            pix = 1
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
if pix == 1 {
    // The desk stands 508 bytes since Stone 50 (the
    // region repaint's road and window at 384 to 403,
    // the terminal window's standing, place, and
    // answers-keeping address at 404 to 423, the
    // Bible window's standing, page, place, and
    // chapter pick at 424 to 443, the Shield
    // window's standing, place, and the door's
    // reckoning at 444 to 463, the drag step's
    // errand at 464 to 475, the picture window's
    // standing, the kept picture block's address,
    // and its place at 476 to 491, the files
    // window's exchange word at 492 to 495, and the
    // Cardinal asking's standing, its pick, and the
    // making's word at 496 to 507, and the drag's
    // painted place at 508 to 515, since the Drag
    // Mend).
    pixdesk = makebytes(516)
    fontblock = makebytes(1024)
    fontload(fontblock)
    // The pointer's shapes and its save block (Stone 13):
    // the arrow's rows laid into their block, and a block
    // to keep the patch of screen the arrow stands on —
    // sixteen pixels wide and seventeen tall, four bytes
    // a pixel: 16 * 17 * 4 = 1088 bytes (the pointer
    // trail fix: the patch is seventeen tall because the
    // arrow's white edge paints one row below its shape,
    // at y + 16 — see pixface.nifla's pointer heading).
    let arrowblock = makebytes(64)
    arrowload(arrowblock)
    let saveblock = makebytes(1088)
    let pdblock = makebytes(8192)
    pset(pixdesk, 0, fbaddrlo)
    pset(pixdesk, 4, fbpitch)
    pset(pixdesk, 8, fbwidth)
    pset(pixdesk, 12, fbheight)
    pset(pixdesk, 16, fbrpos)
    pset(pixdesk, 20, fbgpos)
    pset(pixdesk, 24, fbbpos)
    pset(pixdesk, 36, blockaddr(fontblock))
    pset(pixdesk, 40, blockaddr(pdblock))
    pset(pixdesk, 44, blockaddr(arrowblock))
    pset(pixdesk, 48, blockaddr(saveblock))
    // The two windows' standing (Stone 14), kept in the
    // desk at 172 to 184: both windows stand open, the
    // welcome forward, and no feather has visited yet.
    pset(pixdesk, 172, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 176, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 180, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 184, 0)
    // The files window's standing (Stone 19), at 188 to
    // 204: it starts closed — the word 'files' opens it —
    // the first note stands picked, and the main body's
    // facts (how many notes stand kept, whether a disk
    // is keeping them, whether one is fitted at all) are
    // copied in here and whenever they change below.
    pset(pixdesk, 188, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 192, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
    pset(pixdesk, 200, diskset)
    pset(pixdesk, 204, diskup)
    // The settings window's standing (Stone 20), at 208
    // to 216: it starts closed — the word 'settings'
    // opens it — and the facts it shows (whether the
    // chime stands on, the clock's offset) are copied in
    // here and whenever they change below.
    pset(pixdesk, 208, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 212, chimeon)
    pset(pixdesk, 216, offset)
    // (The editor window's standing at 220 to 232 starts
    // at nothing, as the desk is taken cleared: closed,
    // in its picking way.)
    // The bar menu's standing (Stone 23), at 268: it
    // starts closed — a press on the bar opens it.
    pset(pixdesk, 268, 0)
    // The rest panel's standing (Stone 25), at 292: it
    // starts closed — the word 'rest' or the menu's
    // Rest choice opens it.
    pset(pixdesk, 292, 0)
    // The kept picture's standing (Stone 26), at 296:
    // whether a picture stands kept on the disk, copied
    // in here and whenever it changes below, for the
    // files window's painting.
    pset(pixdesk, 296, pichas)
    // The letting-go standings (Stone 27), at 300 to
    // 316: both windows' askings start put away, each
    // with "Keep it" standing picked for when it opens,
    // and no note named as an asking's subject.
    pset(pixdesk, 300, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 304, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 308, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 312, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 316, 0)
    // The naming standings (Stone 40), at 376 and 380:
    // no naming stands open in the files window, and it
    // has spoken no word.
    pset(pixdesk, 376, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 380, 0)
    // The exchange's word (Stone 49), at 492: the
    // exchange with a USB stick has spoken no word in
    // the files window yet.
    pset(pixdesk, 492, 0)
    // The Cardinal asking and the making's word
    // (Stone 50), at 496 to 504: no asking stands in
    // the files window, its pick stands at "Not yet"
    // for when it opens, and the making has spoken no
    // word in the window yet.
    pset(pixdesk, 496, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 500, 1)
    pset(pixdesk, 504, 0)
    // The verse window's standing (Stone 29), at 324 to
    // 336: it starts closed — the word 'verse' opens it —
    // and the first verse stands in it until an opening
    // reckons the day's. Its place is laid with the other
    // windows' places by layplaces below.
    pset(pixdesk, 324, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 336, 0)
    // The letter cell's size (Stone 28), at 320: sixteen
    // pixels at the words' usual size, twenty-four when
    // large print stands — laid from the size standing
    // read from the card above, before pixbegin reckons
    // the grid from it.
    pset(pixdesk, 320, 16 + wordsize * 8)
    pixbegin(pixdesk)
    // The windows' places (Stone 22), at 236 to 264 and
    // the paint window's at 280 and 284: laid here, at
    // the face's rising, by layplaces — each window
    // centered at the place its measures first reckon —
    // and dragging writes the pairs from here on (see
    // winmove above). The welcome's place was laid by
    // pixbegin itself, at 140 to 152, as it always has
    // been.
    layplaces(pixdesk)
    // The feather's canvas on this face: a text screen's
    // worth of cells, laid out as the text screen's own
    // are — a letter byte and a color byte to a cell —
    // and filled with the resting cell (a space, light
    // gray on black) the text screen itself starts with.
    cellblock = makebytes(4000)
    let ci = 0
    while ci < 1000 {
        pset(cellblock, ci * 4, 119539488)
        ci = ci + 1
    }
    // The paint window's standing (Stone 24), at 272 to
    // 284: it starts closed — the word 'paint' opens it —
    // and no paint feather has visited yet. Its place is
    // laid with the other windows' places below.
    pset(pixdesk, 272, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 276, 0)
    // The paint feather's canvas on this face (Stone
    // 24): a second text screen's worth of cells, the
    // paint window's own, filled with the same resting
    // cell. The paint feather paints here — its panel,
    // its swatches, its canvas — and the window shows
    // the cells 1:1, as the feather window shows its
    // feather's. What stands in these cells between
    // visits IS the painting, the feather's keeping
    // held by the host, as the feather's cells have
    // always been held.
    paintblock = makebytes(4000)
    let pi = 0
    while pi < 1000 {
        pset(paintblock, pi * 4, 119539488)
        pi = pi + 1
    }
    // The paint cells' address stands in the desk at
    // 288, where the paint window's painter reads it
    // (pcellbyte above) — paintwindows' hands are full,
    // as its heading tells.
    pset(pixdesk, 288, blockaddr(paintblock))
    // The calculator window's standing (Stone 30), at
    // 340 to 356: it starts closed — the word
    // 'calculator' opens it — and no calculator feather
    // has visited yet. Its place is laid with the other
    // windows' places by layplaces above.
    pset(pixdesk, 340, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 344, 0)
    // The calculator feather's canvas on this face
    // (Stone 30): a third text screen's worth of cells,
    // the calculator window's own, filled with the same
    // resting cell. The calculator feather paints here
    // — its invitation, its sum's line, and its keys —
    // and the window shows the cells 1:1, as the paint
    // window shows its feather's. What stands in these
    // cells between visits IS the reckoning, the
    // feather's keeping held by the host, as the paint
    // feather's cells have always been held.
    calcblock = makebytes(4000)
    let ci2 = 0
    while ci2 < 1000 {
        pset(calcblock, ci2 * 4, 119539488)
        ci2 = ci2 + 1
    }
    // The calculator cells' address stands in the desk
    // at 356, where the calculator window's painter
    // reads it (ccellbyte above).
    pset(pixdesk, 356, blockaddr(calcblock))
    // The help window's standing (Stone 31), at 360 to
    // 372: it starts closed — the word 'help' opens it —
    // with its first page standing in it. Its place is
    // laid with the other windows' places by layplaces
    // above.
    pset(pixdesk, 360, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 364, 0)
    // The Bible window's standing (Stone 41), at 424
    // to 443: it starts closed — the word 'bible', its
    // icon, and the menu's Bible choice open it — with
    // its contents page standing in it and the first
    // chapter picked there. Its place is laid with the
    // other windows' places by layplaces above.
    pset(pixdesk, 424, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 428, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 440, 1)
    // The Shield window's standing (Stone 42), at
    // 444 to 463: it starts closed — the word
    // 'shield', its icon, and the menu's Shield
    // choice open it — and the door's reckoning
    // starts at none let in and none turned away,
    // the main body's counts copied in here and at
    // every change. Its place is laid with the
    // other windows' places by layplaces above.
    pset(pixdesk, 444, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 456, shieldin)
    pset(pixdesk, 460, shieldout)
    // The picture window's standing (Stone 48), at
    // 476 to 488: it starts closed — the word
    // 'picture', the files window's "Look at it",
    // and the menu's Picture choice open it — and
    // the kept picture block's address stands at
    // 480, where the picture window's painter reads
    // the kept picture's cells through it
    // (piccellbyte above). Its place is laid with
    // the other windows' places by layplaces above.
    pset(pixdesk, 476, 0)
    pset(pixdesk, 480, blockaddr(picblock))
    // The terminal window's standing (Stone 38), at 404
    // to 416: it stands OPEN as the face rises — the
    // desktop arrives with its terminal on it, the
    // console a window among windows from the first
    // moment — and the word 'terminal', its desktop
    // icon, and the first key of any typed line raise
    // it again whenever it has been closed. Its place
    // was laid with the other windows' places by
    // layplaces above, and the console's rows with it.
    pset(pixdesk, 404, 1)
    // The answers' keeping (Stone 38): eleven rows of
    // forty-eight cells, a letter byte and a color byte
    // to a cell as the feather canvases are kept,
    // filled with the resting cell (a space, light gray
    // on black) the answers have always stood in. What
    // stands in these cells between printings IS the
    // console's memory: the terminal's painter lays
    // them again after any repaint, and the scroll
    // shifts them, as pixface's keeping tells. Its
    // address stands in the desk at 416, where
    // pixface's floors read it.
    termblock = makebytes(1056)
    let ti = 0
    while ti < 264 {
        pset(termblock, ti * 4, 119539488)
        ti = ti + 1
    }
    pset(pixdesk, 416, blockaddr(termblock))
}

// Ears on the USB road (Stone 6): the driver's whole
// estate lives in one desk of bytes, taken here and handed
// to its library (usbkey.nifla) at every asking — the
// raising walks the bus, wakes a keyboard if one stands on
// a port, and arms its report door. When no controller of
// the kind it speaks stands in the machine, or no keyboard
// is plugged in, the road is simply quiet and the PS/2
// road carries the typing alone, as before.
let usbdesk = makebytes(16384)
let usbup = usbinit(usbdesk)

// The pointer (Stone 13): a PS/2 mouse, woken only on
// the face of light — the text face has no pointer, as
// it never has. Its packet desk, its standing, the
// arrow's place and the button standing live here; the
// driver itself is mouse.nifla, and the arrow's drawing
// is pixface.nifla's. A feather may be asked for by the
// word 'feather' or by a press of the mouse's first button
// on the feather's panel; 'featherask' carries either asking
// to the one receiving, in the loop below.
let mousedesk = makebytes(64)
let mouseup = 0
let mx = 0
let my = 0
let mbtn = 0
let mshown = 0
let featherask = 0
// The paint feather's asking (Stone 24): 0 when no visit
// is owed; otherwise the gift the next visit carries
// across the desk — 1 for the opening visit the word
// 'paint' asks for, or 1000 plus a press's row times 100
// plus its column for a press the pointer's asking below
// carries in. The one receiving below serves every
// asking, as the feather's has since Stone 13.
let paintask = 0
// The calculator feather's asking (Stone 30): 0 when no
// visit is owed; otherwise the gift the next visit
// carries across the desk — 1 for the opening visit the
// word 'calculator' asks for, or 1000 plus a press's row
// times 100 plus its column for a press the pointer's
// asking below carries in. The one receiving below
// serves every asking, as the paint feather's does.
let calcask = 0
// The files window's owing (Stone 47): 1 when a paint
// stroke's press visits have changed the kept
// picture's standing — the files window's list's own
// fact, at the desk's 296 — and the list has not been
// repainted since. The owing is paid once, where the
// pointer's button is let go: the list is repainted
// at the stroke's end, not per packet, the way it was
// when every press visit ran the region road over
// the files window with the paint window's.
let filesowed = 0
// The drag's standing (Stone 22): which window the
// pointer holds by its title bar (0 none, else the
// window's own number, as winopen and its kin reckon),
// and the hold's place within that window — the
// pointer's cell less the window's corner cell, taken
// when the drag begins, so the window follows the
// pointer by the same hold all the way.
let dragwin = 0
let draggox = 0
let draggoy = 0
// The Drag Mend (November 27, 2026): whether the
// held window's standing place has run ahead of
// its painting during this pass's packet gathering
// — the packets move the window's place silently,
// and the pass's end pays the one repaint (see the
// drag's following in the pointer's asking below).
let dragdirty = 0
// (Stones 34 and 45 kept the drag's whole road here —
// every place the held window had stood, gathered
// step by step for the drop's region repaint. Stone
// 47's drop pays one whole repaint instead, which
// needs no road, and the gathering is gone.)
if pix == 1 {
    mouseup = mouseinit()
    mx = pget(pixdesk, 8) / 2 - 8
    my = pget(pixdesk, 12) / 2 - 8
}

// A true clock (Stone 7): the machine's own clock is read
// once, here, and kept as a count of seconds — the world's
// present moment, on the standing assumption that the
// machine's clock holds the world's standard time (see
// rtclock.nifla) — and 'bootsec' remembers the machine's
// own tick at the reading, so the loop can carry the
// moment forward by the tick's advance. When no true
// reading can be had, rtcok stays 0 and the bar keeps the
// honest count since waking, as it did before this stone.
let rtcblock = makebytes(32)
let rtcok = 0
let rtcbase = 0
let bootsec = ticks() / 100
if rtcread(rtcblock) == 1 {
    let base = rtcbaseof(rtcblock)
    if base > 0 {
        rtcbase = base
        rtcok = 1
    }
}
let utcstart = 0 - 1
if rtcok == 1 {
    utcstart = rtcbase
}

drawface(offset, utcstart, pix, pixdesk)
if pix == 1 {
    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
}

// The welcome is sounded as the desktop stands (Stone
// 18): three gentle notes from the machine's small
// voice. The waits inside it are bounded twice over —
// a machine with no speaker, or a timer that stands
// still, is not held by it, and the typing line below
// is reached either way. Since Stone 20 it sounds only
// when the chime stands on — a keeping disk's card may
// have said off, above.
if chimeon == 1 {
    welcomechime()
}

let keys = makebytes(48)
// The shift standing resolved for each key in 'keys', at
// the same place (Stone 12): 1 when that key stands as its
// capital or its shifted mark. The standing is resolved
// when the key is pressed — a shift taken later must not
// rewrite letters already typed.
let keymod = makebytes(48)
let klen = 0
let ansrow = 13
if pix == 1 {
    ansrow = pget(pixdesk, 128)
}
let lastsec = 0 - 1
let twolastmin = 0 - 1
drawtyping("", pix, pixdesk)
if pix == 0 {
    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "No picture screen was granted, so this face stays in words.", pix, pixdesk)
}
if usbup == 1 {
    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB keyboard is ready.", pix, pixdesk)
}
if xup == 1 {
    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB keyboard is ready on the modern USB road.", pix, pixdesk)
}

// The second screen (Stone 44): a second display
// adapter, when the machine carries one, is raised
// here and its companion page painted; the answer
// names it in plain words. With none, nothing
// anywhere changes.
let twoup = 0
if pix == 1 {
    twoup = twostart(twodesk, fbaddrlo)
    if twoup == 1 {
        let utcfirst = 0 - 1
        if rtcok == 1 {
            utcfirst = rtcbase + (ticks() / 100 - bootsec)
        }
        paintsecond(twodesk, fontblock, utcfirst, offset)
    }
}
if twoup == 1 {
    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A second screen is here.", pix, pixdesk)
}

if diskup == 1 {
    if diskset == 0 {
        if keepusb == 1 {
            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB stick is here. Type setup to let it remember your choices.", pix, pixdesk)
        } else {
            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A hard disk is here. Type setup to let it remember your choices.", pix, pixdesk)
        }
    }
}

// The arrow is laid at the screen's middle before the
// loop begins (Stone 13); every pass of the loop takes
// it off before anything is drawn and lays it again at
// the pass's end, wherever it then stands.
if pix == 1 {
    if mouseup == 1 {
        pixsave(pixdesk, mx, my)
        pixarrow(pixdesk, mx, my)
        mshown = 1
    }
}

// The setup gate's standing (Stone 8): 1 while the system
// is waiting for the gate's answer — the next finished
// word is that answer, and only the exact word 'yes' lets
// the clearing begin.
let awaiting = 0

// The resting's standing (Stone 25): 'restdo' carries a
// chosen resting to the one doing at the loop's end, as
// featherask carries the feather's asking — 0 no resting
// is owed, 1 shut down, 2 restart — however it was chosen:
// the words 'shutdown' and 'restart' themselves, the
// rest panel's rows on the face of light, or the text
// face's offered answer below. 'awaitingrest' is the
// text face's gate: 1 while the system waits for the
// line that answers the word 'rest' there — the exact
// words 'shutdown' and 'restart' name the choices, and
// anything else rests nothing.
let restdo = 0
let awaitingrest = 0

// The shift standing of the two roads together (Stone
// 12): both roads speak the same key numbers, the shifts
// among them, so one standing serves both — 1 while the
// left or right shift key is held, and Caps Lock's own
// standing beside it, turned over by its key. The rest of
// the last finished line, after its first word, is kept
// whole in 'linerest' for the words that want it.
let lshift = 0
let rshift = 0
let caps = 0
let linerest = ""
// The answers' laid row (Stone 38): the row the
// answer stream had reached when the terminal
// window's region was last repainted for it. The
// answers are kept in their block and laid by the
// terminal's painter, never drawn straight to the
// screen — a window standing over the terminal must
// never be painted over by an answer — so when the
// stream moves in a pass, the pass's end repaints
// the terminal's region in the standing order.
let anslaid = ansrow

while true {
    // The arrow comes off first (Stone 13): whatever
    // this pass draws, it draws on the desktop as it
    // stands without the arrow; the arrow is laid again
    // at the pass's end, wherever it then stands.
    if pix == 1 {
        if mshown == 1 {
            pixrestore(pixdesk, mx, my)
            mshown = 0
        }
    }
    let nowsec = ticks() / 100
    let utcnow = 0 - 1
    if rtcok == 1 {
        utcnow = rtcbase + (nowsec - bootsec)
    }
    if nowsec != lastsec {
        lastsec = nowsec
        drawclock(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk)
        if twoup == 1 {
            if utcnow >= 0 {
                if utcnow / 60 != twolastmin {
                    twolastmin = utcnow / 60
                    paintsecond(twodesk, fontblock, utcnow, offset)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    let k = readkey()
    if k == 0 {
        // The PS/2 road is quiet this pass; ask the USB road.
        k = usbpoll(usbdesk)
    }
    if k == 0 {
        // Both of those are quiet this pass; ask the
        // modern USB road (Stone 32). The asking order is
        // the roads' hearing order: when more than one
        // USB keyboard stands ready, the UHCI road — asked
        // first — is the one the loop hears first, and
        // this road is heard when it is quiet; the PS/2
        // road stands before both, as it always has.
        k = xhpoll(xdesk)
    }
    if k != 0 {
        // The shift keys and Caps Lock speak first (Stone
        // 12): their numbers are keys like any other on both
        // roads — a shift going down is 42 or 54, coming up
        // is 170 or 182, and Caps Lock's press is 58 — and
        // they turn the standing the next letter is resolved
        // under. None of them is itself typed into the line,
        // and no other key's release (a number of 128 or
        // more) is typed either, as it never was.
        let plain = 1
        if k == 42 {
            lshift = 1
            plain = 0
        }
        if k == 54 {
            rshift = 1
            plain = 0
        }
        if k == 170 {
            lshift = 0
            plain = 0
        }
        if k == 182 {
            rshift = 0
            plain = 0
        }
        if k == 58 {
            caps = 1 - caps
            plain = 0
        }
        if k >= 128 {
            plain = 0
        }
        if plain == 1 {
            // The arrow keys in the files window (Stone
            // 19): the up and down arrows arrive as the
            // numbers 72 and 80 (the second byte of their
            // two-byte make codes; the first byte, 224,
            // stands at 128 or more and is let go above,
            // as every such byte is). They name no letter
            // — charFor gives them nothing, so the typing
            // below lets them pass untyped, as it always
            // has — and while the files window stands
            // forward they move its pick among the kept
            // notes, and the window is painted again.
            if k == 72 {
                if pix == 1 {
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 3 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 300) + pget(pixdesk, 376) + pget(pixdesk, 496) > 0 {
                            // The files window's asking
                            // (Stone 27): the arrows move
                            // the asking's pick — up to
                            // "Yes, let it go" — and the
                            // note's pick cannot move while
                            // the asking stands. A naming
                            // standing in the window (Stone
                            // 40) swallows the arrows the
                            // same way: the pick it names
                            // cannot move under it. The
                            // Cardinal asking (Stone 50)
                            // stands in the same way: the
                            // arrows move its pick — up to
                            // "Yes, make it a Cardinal".
                            if pget(pixdesk, 496) == 1 {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 500) == 1 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 500, 0)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            } else {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 304) == 1 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 304, 0)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        } else {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 196) + pichas > 0 {
                                let sel = pget(pixdesk, 192)
                                if sel > 0 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 192, sel - 1)
                                    pset(pixdesk, 492, 0)
                                    pset(pixdesk, 504, 0)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    // The editor window's picking way
                    // (Stone 21): the same arrows move its
                    // pick — over the session's notes too,
                    // which the window honestly holds when
                    // no disk is keeping them.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 5 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 308) == 1 {
                            // The editor window's asking
                            // (Stone 27): the arrows move
                            // its pick the same way.
                            if pget(pixdesk, 312) == 1 {
                                pset(pixdesk, 312, 0)
                                paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                            }
                        } else {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 224) == 0 {
                                let sel = pget(pixdesk, 228)
                                if sel > 0 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 228, sel - 1)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    // The help window's pages (Stone 31):
                    // while it stands forward, the up
                    // arrow turns back a page, and stands
                    // still on the first page.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 9 {
                        let hp = pget(pixdesk, 364)
                        if hp > 0 {
                            pset(pixdesk, 364, hp - 1)
                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                        }
                    }
                    // The Bible window's reading (Stone
                    // 41): while it stands forward, the
                    // up arrow turns back a page — on the
                    // contents page it moves the chapter
                    // pick up instead, and stands still
                    // on the first chapter.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 11 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 428) == 0 {
                            let bp = pget(pixdesk, 440)
                            if bp > 1 {
                                pset(pixdesk, 440, bp - 1)
                                paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                            }
                        } else {
                            pset(pixdesk, 428, pget(pixdesk, 428) - 1)
                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if k == 80 {
                if pix == 1 {
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 3 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 300) + pget(pixdesk, 376) + pget(pixdesk, 496) > 0 {
                            // The files window's asking
                            // (Stone 27): down to "Keep it".
                            // A naming standing (Stone 40)
                            // swallows the arrow the same
                            // way, as the up arrow's telling
                            // above says. The Cardinal
                            // asking (Stone 50) likewise:
                            // down to "Not yet".
                            if pget(pixdesk, 496) == 1 {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 500) == 0 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 500, 1)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            } else {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 304) == 0 {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 304, 1)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        } else {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 196) + pichas > 0 {
                                let sel = pget(pixdesk, 192)
                                // The pick runs over the notes
                                // and then the kept picture
                                // (Stone 26): one more place
                                // stands at the list's end when
                                // a picture stands kept. Since
                                // Stone 27 it runs over a
                                // sitting's notes too, which
                                // the window now lists.
                                if sel + 1 < pget(pixdesk, 196) + pichas {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 192, sel + 1)
                                    pset(pixdesk, 492, 0)
                                    pset(pixdesk, 504, 0)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    // The editor window's picking way
                    // (Stone 21): the pick runs from the
                    // new-note entry to the last kept note.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 5 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 308) == 1 {
                            // The editor window's asking
                            // (Stone 27): down to "Keep it".
                            if pget(pixdesk, 312) == 0 {
                                pset(pixdesk, 312, 1)
                                paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                            }
                        } else {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 224) == 0 {
                                let sel = pget(pixdesk, 228)
                                if sel < pget(pixdesk, 196) {
                                    pset(pixdesk, 228, sel + 1)
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    // The help window's pages (Stone 31):
                    // while it stands forward, the down
                    // arrow turns a page forward — the
                    // foot's own turning (its More word,
                    // and First page on the last page):
                    // on the last page it returns to the
                    // first page.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 9 {
                        let hp = pget(pixdesk, 364) + 1
                        if hp >= helppages() {
                            hp = 0
                        }
                        pset(pixdesk, 364, hp)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    }
                    // The Bible window's reading (Stone
                    // 41): while it stands forward, the
                    // down arrow turns a page forward —
                    // the foot's own turning (its More
                    // word, and First page on the last
                    // page): on the last page it returns
                    // to the contents page. On the
                    // contents page it moves the chapter
                    // pick down instead, and stands
                    // still on the last chapter.
                    if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 11 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 428) == 0 {
                            let bp = pget(pixdesk, 440)
                            if bp < 4 {
                                pset(pixdesk, 440, bp + 1)
                                paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                            }
                        } else {
                            let bp2 = pget(pixdesk, 428) + 1
                            if bp2 >= bibpages(bibwidth(pixdesk)) {
                                bp2 = 0
                            }
                            pset(pixdesk, 428, bp2)
                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if k == 28 {
                // Enter: the line is finished; answer it.
                // The line's first word is its command word,
                // and the rest of the line is kept whole in
                // 'linerest' for the words that want it
                // (Stone 12) — a line of one word is answered
                // exactly as it always was, its word being
                // the line itself. The command library
                // (commands.nifla) decides what the word is
                // answered with; the doing — printing the
                // line, drawing the face again, receiving
                // the feather, keeping the clock's new offset —
                // happens here, where the state lives.
                // The editor window's Enter (Stone 21):
                // while the editor stands forward, Enter on
                // an EMPTY typing line is the window's own
                // key, not a command's — in its picking way
                // it begins writing the picked note (or a
                // new one), and in its writing way it keeps
                // the draft through the same store path the
                // word 'note' uses. A line with words on it
                // is a command, as it always is: the window
                // never takes a typed word's Enter from it.
                // 'edused' marks the line as the window's,
                // so no command kind fires for it below. The
                // setup gate's waiting answer is never the
                // window's: while the gate waits, Enter
                // answers the gate as it always has.
                // The files window's Enter (Stone 26):
                // while the files window stands forward
                // with the kept picture picked, Enter on
                // an EMPTY typing line opens the picture
                // in the paint window — the paint window
                // is opened and the feather's opening
                // visit asked for, exactly as the word
                // 'paint' asks, and the receiving below
                // lays the kept picture back into its
                // cells. 'fileused' marks the line as the
                // window's, so no command kind fires.
                let fileused = 0
                if awaiting == 0 {
                    if klen == 0 {
                        if pix == 1 {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 3 {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 188) == 1 {
                                    if pget(pixdesk, 376) > 0 {
                                        // The naming way's own Enter
                                        // (Stone 40): the name typed
                                        // into the block's tenth slot
                                        // is kept — or refused in
                                        // plain words in the window
                                        // (the desk's 380 carries the
                                        // word for the window to
                                        // show), with nothing
                                        // changed. A keeping closes
                                        // the naming, repaints the
                                        // files window by the region
                                        // road over that window
                                        // alone, and answers in the
                                        // terminal like the window's
                                        // other doings.
                                        fileused = 1
                                        let nmway = pget(pixdesk, 376)
                                        let nmsub = pget(pixdesk, 192)
                                        let nmdn = notesblock[576]
                                        if nmdn == 0 {
                                            pset(pixdesk, 380, 2)
                                            layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                            paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        } else {
                                            let nmfull = 0
                                            if nmway == 2 {
                                                if notecount >= 8 {
                                                    nmfull = 1
                                                }
                                            }
                                            if nmfull == 1 {
                                                pset(pixdesk, 380, 3)
                                                layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                                paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                            } else {
                                                let nmdup = 0
                                                let nmj = 0
                                                while nmj < notecount {
                                                    let nmskip = 0
                                                    if nmway == 1 {
                                                        if nmj == nmsub {
                                                            nmskip = 1
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                    if nmskip == 0 {
                                                        if draftisname(notesblock, nmj) == 1 {
                                                            nmdup = 1
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                    nmj = nmj + 1
                                                }
                                                if nmway != 3 {
                                                    if pichas == 1 {
                                                        if draftispicname(notesblock) == 1 {
                                                            nmdup = 1
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                }
                                                if nmdup == 1 {
                                                    pset(pixdesk, 380, 1)
                                                    layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                                    paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                                } else {
                                                    if nmway == 1 {
                                                        let nmt = 0
                                                        while nmt < 20 {
                                                            notesblock[nmsub * 64 + 44 + nmt] = 0
                                                            nmt = nmt + 1
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[nmsub * 64 + 44] = nmdn
                                                        let nmi = 0
                                                        while nmi < nmdn {
                                                            notesblock[nmsub * 64 + 45 + nmi] = notesblock[576 + 1 + nmi]
                                                            nmi = nmi + 1
                                                        }
                                                        let nmwrote = 0
                                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                                            if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                                                nmwrote = 1
                                                            }
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[576] = 0
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 380, 0)
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 376, 0)
                                                        layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                                        paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                                            if nmwrote == 1 {
                                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is renamed, and its new name is kept on the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                            } else {
                                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the new name, so it stands only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                            }
                                                        } else {
                                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is renamed.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                    if nmway == 2 {
                                                        let nmcj = 0
                                                        while nmcj < 64 {
                                                            notesblock[notecount * 64 + nmcj] = notesblock[nmsub * 64 + nmcj]
                                                            nmcj = nmcj + 1
                                                        }
                                                        let nmt2 = 0
                                                        while nmt2 < 20 {
                                                            notesblock[notecount * 64 + 44 + nmt2] = 0
                                                            nmt2 = nmt2 + 1
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[notecount * 64 + 44] = nmdn
                                                        let nmi2 = 0
                                                        while nmi2 < nmdn {
                                                            notesblock[notecount * 64 + 45 + nmi2] = notesblock[576 + 1 + nmi2]
                                                            nmi2 = nmi2 + 1
                                                        }
                                                        notecount = notecount + 1
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 192, notecount - 1)
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 492, 0)
                                                        let nmwrote2 = 0
                                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                                            if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                                                nmwrote2 = 1
                                                            }
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[576] = 0
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 380, 0)
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 376, 0)
                                                        layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                                        paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                                            if nmwrote2 == 1 {
                                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your copy is kept on the disk, under its new name.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                            } else {
                                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the copy, so it is kept only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                            }
                                                        } else {
                                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your copy is kept for this sitting, under its new name.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                    if nmway == 3 {
                                                        let nmp = 0
                                                        while nmp < 44 {
                                                            notesblock[640 + nmp] = 0
                                                            nmp = nmp + 1
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[640] = nmdn
                                                        let nmi3 = 0
                                                        while nmi3 < nmdn {
                                                            notesblock[640 + 1 + nmi3] = notesblock[576 + 1 + nmi3]
                                                            nmi3 = nmi3 + 1
                                                        }
                                                        let nmwrote3 = 0
                                                        if keepusb == 1 {
                                                            if upichead(xdesk, 1, picsel, notesblock) == 1 {
                                                                let nmbody = upicbody(xdesk, picblock)
                                                                nmwrote3 = 1
                                                            }
                                                        } else {
                                                            if picsave(picblock, 1, picsel, notesblock) == 1 {
                                                                nmwrote3 = 1
                                                            }
                                                        }
                                                        notesblock[576] = 0
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 380, 0)
                                                        pset(pixdesk, 376, 0)
                                                        layroadwins(pixdesk, 3, 0)
                                                        paintregion(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                                        if nmwrote3 == 1 {
                                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your picture is renamed, and its new name is kept on the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                        } else {
                                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the new name, so it stands only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                }
                                            }
                                        }
                                    } else {
                                    if pget(pixdesk, 496) == 1 {
                                        // The Cardinal asking's
                                        // own Enter (Stone 50):
                                        // the picked choice is
                                        // done — "Not yet" puts
                                        // the asking away and
                                        // nothing is made; "Yes,
                                        // make it a Cardinal"
                                        // runs the making, the
                                        // same doing the
                                        // pointer's yes runs, in
                                        // the telling at that
                                        // press. Enter alone
                                        // never makes a Cardinal:
                                        // the asking opens with
                                        // "Not yet" standing
                                        // picked, so the yes
                                        // must be chosen — by
                                        // the arrow keys and
                                        // Enter, or by the
                                        // pointer — as the
                                        // house's askings ask.
                                        fileused = 1
                                        if pget(pixdesk, 500) == 0 {
                                            let ceword = 0
                                            if diskhere() == 0 {
                                                ceword = 2
                                            } else {
                                                if diskcardinal(2048) == 1 {
                                                    ceword = 4
                                                } else {
                                                    if usectors(xdesk) == 0 {
                                                        ceword = 3
                                                    } else {
                                                        let ceimg = ustickimage(xdesk)
                                                        if ceimg == 0 {
                                                            ceword = 8
                                                        } else {
                                                            selectdrive()
                                                            if disksectors() < 2048 + ceimg {
                                                                ceword = 5
                                                            } else {
                                                                pset(pixdesk, 496, 0)
                                                                pset(pixdesk, 504, 7)
                                                                paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                                                let cecopy = ucopyimage(xdesk, 2048, ceimg, copyblock)
                                                                if cecopy < ceimg {
                                                                    ceword = 6
                                                                } else {
                                                                    if ucopyproof(xdesk, 2048, ceimg, copyblock) == 0 {
                                                                        ceword = 6
                                                                    } else {
                                                                        if ulayrecord(xdesk, 2048, copyblock) == 0 {
                                                                            ceword = 6
                                                                        } else {
                                                                            if diskset == 0 {
                                                                                let cechime = 2
                                                                                if chimeon == 1 {
                                                                                    cechime = 1
                                                                                }
                                                                                let cecard = rememberall(offset, cechime, wordsize + 1)
                                                                                let cenotes = notesave(notesblock, notecount)
                                                                                let cepic = picsave(picblock, pichas, picsel, notesblock)
                                                                                diskset = 1
                                                                                awaiting = 0
                                                                                pset(pixdesk, 200, 1)
                                                                            }
                                                                            ceword = 1
                                                                        }
                                                                    }
                                                                }
                                                            }
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                }
                                            }
                                            pset(pixdesk, 504, ceword)
                                            pset(pixdesk, 496, 0)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        } else {
                                            pset(pixdesk, 496, 0)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        }
                                    } else {
                                    if pget(pixdesk, 300) == 1 {
                                        // The asking's own Enter
                                        // (Stone 27): the picked
                                        // choice is done — the
                                        // yes lets the picked
                                        // note go through the
                                        // store's own path, the
                                        // keep puts the asking
                                        // away and leaves the
                                        // note exactly as it was.
                                        fileused = 1
                                        if pget(pixdesk, 304) == 0 {
                                            let ft = pget(pixdesk, 192)
                                            let fi = ft
                                            while fi < notecount - 1 {
                                                let fj = 0
                                                while fj < 64 {
                                                    notesblock[fi * 64 + fj] = notesblock[(fi + 1) * 64 + fj]
                                                    fj = fj + 1
                                                }
                                                fi = fi + 1
                                            }
                                            let fz = 0
                                            while fz < 64 {
                                                notesblock[(notecount - 1) * 64 + fz] = 0
                                                fz = fz + 1
                                            }
                                            if diskset == 1 {
                                                let fw1 = knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount)
                                            }
                                            notecount = notecount - 1
                                            let fwrote = 0
                                            if diskset == 1 {
                                                if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                                    fwrote = 1
                                                }
                                            }
                                            pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
                                            let fsel = ft
                                            if fsel >= notecount {
                                                fsel = notecount - 1
                                            }
                                            if fsel < 0 {
                                                fsel = 0
                                            }
                                            pset(pixdesk, 192, fsel)
                                            pset(pixdesk, 492, 0)
                                            pset(pixdesk, 300, 0)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                            if diskset == 1 {
                                                if fwrote == 1 {
                                                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is let go, and gone from the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                } else {
                                                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the change, so the note is let go only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                                }
                                            } else {
                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is let go.", pix, pixdesk)
                                            }
                                        } else {
                                            pset(pixdesk, 300, 0)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        }
                                    } else {
                                        if pget(pixdesk, 192) < notecount {
                                            // Enter on a picked
                                            // note (Stone 27):
                                            // the asking opens,
                                            // with "Keep it"
                                            // standing picked.
                                            fileused = 1
                                            pset(pixdesk, 304, 1)
                                            pset(pixdesk, 300, 1)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        } else {
                                            if pichas == 1 {
                                                if pget(pixdesk, 192) == notecount {
                                                    fileused = 1
                                                    pset(pixdesk, 272, 1)
                                                    pset(pixdesk, 180, 6)
                                                    paintask = 1
                                                }
                                            }
                                        }
                                    }
                                    }
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                // The Bible window's Enter (Stone 41):
                // while the Bible window stands forward
                // on its contents page, Enter on an EMPTY
                // typing line is the window's own key, not
                // a command's — it reads the picked
                // chapter, turning to that chapter's first
                // page. 'bibused' marks the line as the
                // window's, so no command kind fires for
                // it below. The turning itself is done
                // below, beside the kind's setting-aside
                // — after the typing line is drawn again,
                // as the kind blocks' own paintings come
                // after it: the window's foot stands on
                // the terminal's typing line's own row,
                // and a painting made here would be
                // overpainted by that drawing, as the
                // help window's kind-block painting is
                // not. The setup gate's waiting answer
                // is never the window's, as with the other
                // windows' Enters.
                let bibused = 0
                if awaiting == 0 {
                    if klen == 0 {
                        if pix == 1 {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 11 {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 424) == 1 {
                                    if pget(pixdesk, 428) == 0 {
                                        bibused = 1
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                let edused = 0
                if awaiting == 0 {
                    if klen == 0 {
                    if pix == 1 {
                        if pget(pixdesk, 180) == 5 {
                            if pget(pixdesk, 220) == 1 {
                                if pget(pixdesk, 308) == 1 {
                                    // The asking's own Enter
                                    // (Stone 27): the picked
                                    // choice is done — the
                                    // yes lets the note the
                                    // asking names (the desk's
                                    // 316) go through the
                                    // store's own path, and
                                    // the window returns to
                                    // its picking way; the
                                    // keep puts the asking
                                    // away and leaves the
                                    // note — and any draft
                                    // standing — exactly as
                                    // it was.
                                    edused = 1
                                    if pget(pixdesk, 312) == 0 {
                                        let et = pget(pixdesk, 316)
                                        let ei = et
                                        while ei < notecount - 1 {
                                            let ej = 0
                                            while ej < 64 {
                                                notesblock[ei * 64 + ej] = notesblock[(ei + 1) * 64 + ej]
                                                ej = ej + 1
                                            }
                                            ei = ei + 1
                                        }
                                        let ez = 0
                                        while ez < 64 {
                                            notesblock[(notecount - 1) * 64 + ez] = 0
                                            ez = ez + 1
                                        }
                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                            let ew1 = knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount)
                                        }
                                        notecount = notecount - 1
                                        let ewrote = 0
                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                            if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                                ewrote = 1
                                            }
                                        }
                                        pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
                                        notesblock[512] = 0
                                        pset(pixdesk, 224, 0)
                                        let epick = et + 1
                                        if epick > notecount {
                                            epick = notecount
                                        }
                                        pset(pixdesk, 228, epick)
                                        pset(pixdesk, 308, 0)
                                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                            if ewrote == 1 {
                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is let go, and gone from the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                            } else {
                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the change, so the note is let go only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                            }
                                        } else {
                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is let go.", pix, pixdesk)
                                        }
                                    } else {
                                        pset(pixdesk, 308, 0)
                                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                    }
                                } else {
                                let edmode = pget(pixdesk, 224)
                                if edmode == 1 {
                                    edused = 1
                                    if notesblock[512] == 0 {
                                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Write the words of your note first.", pix, pixdesk)
                                    } else {
                                        let slot = pget(pixdesk, 232)
                                        let isnew = 0
                                        if slot == 8 {
                                            slot = notecount
                                            isnew = 1
                                        }
                                        let dn = notesblock[512]
                                        let di = 0
                                        while di < dn {
                                            notesblock[slot * 64 + 1 + di] = notesblock[512 + 1 + di]
                                            di = di + 1
                                        }
                                        notesblock[slot * 64] = dn
                                        if isnew == 1 {
                                            notecount = notecount + 1
                                            pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
                                        }
                                        pset(pixdesk, 228, slot + 1)
                                        pset(pixdesk, 224, 0)
                                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        if diskset == 1 {
                                            if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is kept on the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                            } else {
                                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the note, so it is kept only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                            }
                                        } else {
                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is kept until the machine is turned off.", pix, pixdesk)
                                        }
                                    }
                                }
                                if edmode == 0 {
                                    edused = 1
                                    let pick = pget(pixdesk, 228)
                                    if pick > notecount {
                                        pick = 0
                                    }
                                    if pick == 0 {
                                        if notecount >= 8 {
                                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The notes page is full. Eight notes are kept there.", pix, pixdesk)
                                        } else {
                                            notesblock[512] = 0
                                            pset(pixdesk, 232, 8)
                                            pset(pixdesk, 224, 1)
                                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                        }
                                    } else {
                                        let slot = pick - 1
                                        let cn = notesblock[slot * 64]
                                        notesblock[512] = cn
                                        let ci = 0
                                        while ci < cn {
                                            notesblock[512 + 1 + ci] = notesblock[slot * 64 + 1 + ci]
                                            ci = ci + 1
                                        }
                                        pset(pixdesk, 232, slot)
                                        pset(pixdesk, 224, 1)
                                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                    }
                                }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    }
                }
                let line = buildline(keys, keymod, 0, klen)
                let wlen = klen
                let wi = 0
                while wi < klen {
                    if keys[wi] == 57 {
                        if wlen == klen {
                            wlen = wi
                        }
                    }
                    wi = wi + 1
                }
                let cmd = buildline(keys, keymod, 0, wlen)
                linerest = ""
                if wlen < klen {
                    linerest = buildline(keys, keymod, wlen + 1, klen - wlen - 1)
                }
                klen = 0
                drawtyping("", pix, pixdesk)
                let kind = wordkind(cmd)
                if edused == 1 {
                    // The line was the editor window's own
                    // (Stone 21): no kind's doing fires.
                    kind = 0
                }
                if fileused == 1 {
                    // The line was the files window's own
                    // (Stone 26): Enter on the picked
                    // picture opened it in the paint
                    // window; no kind's doing fires.
                    kind = 0
                }
                if bibused == 1 {
                    // The line was the Bible window's own
                    // (Stone 41): Enter on its contents
                    // page read the picked chapter; no
                    // kind's doing fires. The turning is
                    // done here, after the typing line's
                    // drawing above, the kind blocks'
                    // own order.
                    kind = 0
                    pset(pixdesk, 428, bibchapterpage(pget(pixdesk, 440), bibwidth(pixdesk)))
                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                }
                if awaiting == 1 {
                    // The setup gate's answer (Stone 8):
                    // this line answers the gate, not the
                    // command library — only the exact word
                    // 'yes', the whole line and nothing more,
                    // lets the clearing begin, and anything
                    // else leaves the disk exactly as it was.
                    // The kind is set aside so no kind's
                    // doing fires for this line. Since
                    // Stone 50 the gate's yes is refused on
                    // a disk that carries a Cardinal: the
                    // clearing would wipe the very record
                    // the installed disk boots by, so the
                    // gate answers that the disk carries
                    // the system and leaves it as it was —
                    // an installed disk is never unmade by
                    // the word that cards a fresh one.
                    awaiting = 0
                    kind = 0 - 1
                    if line == "yes" {
                        let chbyte = 2
                        if chimeon == 1 {
                            chbyte = 1
                        }
                        if diskcardinal(2048) == 1 {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This disk carries your Cardnull system, so it was left as it was.", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                        if kdisksetup(xdesk, keepusb, offset, chbyte, wordsize + 1) == 1 {
                            diskset = 1
                            if pix == 1 {
                                // The files window's copy of
                                // the keeping follows (Stone 19).
                                pset(pixdesk, 200, 1)
                            }
                            if notecount > 0 {
                                // Notes kept for the session
                                // are written to the disk now
                                // that it keeps things (Stone
                                // 15) — after the gate's yes,
                                // as the card was.
                                let keptnotes = knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount)
                            }
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk will remember your choices now.", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk could not be written, so nothing was changed.", pix, pixdesk)
                        }
                        }
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk was left as it was.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if awaitingrest == 1 {
                    // The text face's rest answer (Stone
                    // 25): this line answers the word
                    // 'rest', not the command library —
                    // the exact words 'shutdown' and
                    // 'restart' name its two choices and
                    // carry them to the resting doing
                    // below, and anything else rests
                    // nothing. The kind is set aside so
                    // no kind's doing fires for this line.
                    awaitingrest = 0
                    kind = 0 - 1
                    if line == "shutdown" {
                        restdo = 1
                    } else {
                        if line == "restart" {
                            restdo = 2
                        } else {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The machine stays on. Type rest when you want to rest.", pix, pixdesk)
                        }
                    }
                }
                if kind == 2 {
                    // 'clear': the face is drawn again, empty.
                    drawface(offset, utcnow, pix, pixdesk)
                    if pix == 1 {
                        // The bar's menu is put away by a
                        // clearing too (Stone 23): the face
                        // stands drawn whole, and no menu
                        // stands on the new face. The rest
                        // panel is put away the same way
                        // (Stone 25).
                        pset(pixdesk, 268, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 292, 0)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    }
                    ansrow = 13
                    if pix == 1 {
                        ansrow = pget(pixdesk, 128)
                    }
                    lastsec = 0 - 1
                }
                if kind == 3 {
                    // 'feather': the receiving stands below,
                    // where the pointer's asking meets it —
                    // the one doing serves both (Stone 13).
                    featherask = 1
                }
                if kind == 4 {
                    // 'usb': the USB roads report their own
                    // standings, from their ledgers — each
                    // road that carries a keyboard names
                    // itself in plain words and tells its
                    // own count of the keys it has brought
                    // (Stone 6 for the UHCI road, Stone 32
                    // for the modern road).
                    if usbup == 1 {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB keyboard is ready. It has brought " + usbcount(usbdesk) + " keys so far.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                    if xup == 1 {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB keyboard is ready on the modern USB road. It has brought " + xhcount(xdesk) + " keys so far.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                    if usbup == 0 {
                        if xup == 0 {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "No USB keyboard is plugged in.", pix, pixdesk)
                        }
                    }
                    // The stick (Stone 43) reports itself
                    // on the same word: raised at the
                    // rising on the modern road, holding
                    // its sectors for the system's
                    // keeping.
                    if ustick > 0 {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "A USB stick is here. It holds " + ustick + " sectors.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 5 {
                    // 'time': the world's time in words —
                    // the real date and hour (Stone 7), with
                    // the honest count of the machine's
                    // waking beneath it. When no true reading
                    // was had, the waking count alone answers.
                    if utcnow >= 0 {
                        let local = utcnow + offset * 3600
                        let days = local / 86400
                        let daysec = local - days * 86400
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The time is " + clocktext(daysec, 0) + " on " + datetext(days) + ".", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This machine has been awake for " + nowsec + " seconds.", pix, pixdesk)
                }
                if kind == 6 {
                    // 'setup': the disk is offered its
                    // keeping, behind the gate (Stone 8).
                    // The asking itself writes nothing.
                    if diskup == 0 {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "There is no hard disk here to set up.", pix, pixdesk)
                    } else {
                        if diskset == 1 {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This disk is already keeping your choices.", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This will clear everything on the disk. Type yes to go on.", pix, pixdesk)
                            awaiting = 1
                        }
                    }
                }
                if kind == 7 {
                    // A time-choice word: the library reckons
                    // the new offset, this body keeps it, and
                    // the bar's clock is drawn again at once.
                    // When a disk is keeping the choices, the
                    // new one is written to it and the answer
                    // says so; otherwise the library's line
                    // answers, which says the choice lasts
                    // until the machine is turned off — true,
                    // there.
                    offset = wordoffset(cmd, offset)
                    lastsec = 0 - 1
                    if pix == 1 {
                        // The settings window's copy of the
                        // offset follows (Stone 20), and the
                        // window is painted again if it
                        // stands open, so what it shows is
                        // what the clock reckons by.
                        pset(pixdesk, 216, offset)
                        if pget(pixdesk, 208) == 1 {
                            paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                        }
                    }
                    if diskset == 1 {
                        let kept = kremember(xdesk, keepusb, offset)
                        if cmd == "eastern" {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The clock now shows Eastern time. The disk will remember it.", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The clock now shows the world's standard time. The disk will remember it.", pix, pixdesk)
                        }
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, wordline(cmd), pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 8 {
                    // 'welcome': the welcome window is
                    // opened and brought forward (Stone
                    // 14). The text face has no windows —
                    // its welcome panel simply stands —
                    // and says so in plain words.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 172, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 1)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 9 {
                    // 'note': the rest of the line, kept
                    // whole since Stone 12, is kept as a
                    // note (Stone 15) — on the page here,
                    // and written through to the keeping
                    // disk when one stands ready, so the
                    // note stays; otherwise it lives for
                    // the session, and the answer says so.
                    if linerest == "" {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Type note, then the words to keep.", pix, pixdesk)
                    } else {
                        if notecount >= 8 {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The notes page is full. Eight notes are kept there.", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                            let n = length(linerest)
                            notesblock[notecount * 64] = n
                            let ni = 0
                            while ni < n {
                                notesblock[notecount * 64 + 1 + ni] = textbyte(linerest, ni)
                                ni = ni + 1
                            }
                            notecount = notecount + 1
                            if pix == 1 {
                                // The files window's copy of
                                // the count follows (Stone 19).
                                pset(pixdesk, 196, notecount)
                                if pget(pixdesk, 188) == 1 {
                                    paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                                }
                            }
                            if diskset == 1 {
                                if knotesave(xdesk, keepusb, notesblock, notecount) == 1 {
                                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is kept on the disk.", pix, pixdesk)
                                } else {
                                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The disk would not take the note, so it is kept only for now.", pix, pixdesk)
                                }
                            } else {
                                ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your note is kept until the machine is turned off.", pix, pixdesk)
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                if kind == 10 {
                    // 'notes': the kept page is shown —
                    // a heading that says where the notes
                    // live, then each note in its turn.
                    if notecount == 0 {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "No notes are kept yet. Type note, then the words to keep.", pix, pixdesk)
                    } else {
                        if diskset == 1 {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your notes, kept on the disk:", pix, pixdesk)
                        } else {
                            ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Your notes, kept until the machine is turned off:", pix, pixdesk)
                        }
                        let ni = 0
                        while ni < notecount {
                            ansrow = printnote(ansrow, notesblock, ni * 2 + pix, pixdesk)
                            ni = ni + 1
                        }
                    }
                }
                if kind == 11 {
                    // 'chime': the welcome sounded again
                    // (Stone 18), and a line to say so.
                    // Since Stone 20 the chime's standing
                    // governs it: when the chime stands off
                    // the word is answered in plain words
                    // and nothing sounds.
                    if chimeon == 1 {
                        welcomechime()
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The welcome chime, once more.", pix, pixdesk)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "The chime is off, so nothing sounded.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 12 {
                    // 'files': the files window is opened
                    // and brought forward (Stone 19). The
                    // text face has no windows, and says so
                    // in plain words, as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 188, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 300, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 376, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 380, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 3)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 13 {
                    // 'settings': the settings window is
                    // opened and brought forward (Stone 20).
                    // The text face has no windows, and says
                    // so in plain words, as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 208, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 4)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 14 {
                    // 'editor': the editor window is opened
                    // and brought forward (Stone 21), in its
                    // picking way with the new-note entry
                    // picked, whatever way it last stood in.
                    // The text face has no windows, and says
                    // so in plain words, as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 220, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 224, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 228, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 308, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 5)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 15 {
                    // 'paint': the paint window is opened
                    // and brought forward, and the paint
                    // feather is asked for — its opening
                    // visit, gift 1 — through the door
                    // below, where the pointer's carried
                    // presses meet the same receiving
                    // (Stone 24). The asking stands whether
                    // the visit then stands or is refused,
                    // as the feather's asking has always
                    // stood. The text face has no windows,
                    // and says so in plain words, as for
                    // 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 272, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 6)
                        paintask = 1
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 16 {
                    // 'rest': the resting is offered
                    // (Stone 25). On the face of light the
                    // rest panel opens at the desktop's
                    // middle — its two choices, Shut down
                    // and Restart, and Stay — and the bar's
                    // menu, if it stood open, is put away.
                    // On the text face, which has no
                    // pointer, the choices are offered in
                    // words and the next typed line names
                    // one (awaitingrest above).
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 268, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 292, 1)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "Rest. Type shutdown to shut down, or restart to start again.", pix, pixdesk)
                        awaitingrest = 1
                    }
                }
                if kind == 17 {
                    // 'shutdown': the choice is carried to
                    // the one resting doing at the loop's
                    // end (Stone 25), where the disk is
                    // flushed before the goodbye.
                    restdo = 1
                }
                if kind == 18 {
                    // 'restart': the same carrying, for the
                    // starting again (Stone 25).
                    restdo = 2
                }
                if kind == 19 {
                    // 'verse': the verse window is opened
                    // and brought forward (Stone 29), and
                    // the day's verse is reckoned as it
                    // opens — from the date the desktop
                    // itself shows, the true clock's day in
                    // the person's chosen time (verseday
                    // in verses.nifla), taken around the
                    // kept set. The text face has no
                    // windows, and says so in plain words,
                    // as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 336, verseday(utcnow, offset))
                        pset(pixdesk, 324, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 7)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 20 {
                    // 'calculator': the calculator window
                    // is opened and brought forward, and
                    // the calculator feather is asked for
                    // — its opening visit, gift 1 — through
                    // the door below, where the pointer's
                    // carried presses meet the same
                    // receiving (Stone 30). The text face
                    // has no windows, and says so in plain
                    // words, as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 340, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 8)
                        calcask = 1
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 21 {
                    // 'help': the help window is opened
                    // and brought forward (Stone 31), with
                    // its first page standing in it. The
                    // text face has no windows, and says
                    // so in plain words, as for 'welcome'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 364, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 360, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 9)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 23 {
                    // 'bible': the Bible reading window
                    // is opened and brought forward (Stone
                    // 41), with its contents page standing
                    // in it and the first chapter picked
                    // there. The text face has no windows,
                    // and says so in plain words, as for
                    // 'help'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 428, 0)
                        pset(pixdesk, 440, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 424, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 11)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 24 {
                    // 'shield': the Shield window is
                    // opened and brought forward (Stone
                    // 42) — the feather door's guard
                    // showing itself, with the door's
                    // reckoning since the rising standing
                    // in it. The text face has no windows,
                    // and says so in plain words, as for
                    // 'help'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 444, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 12)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 25 {
                    // 'picture': the picture window is
                    // opened and brought forward (Stone
                    // 48) — the kept picture standing big
                    // in it, or, with no picture kept, its
                    // plain words saying so. The text face
                    // has no windows, and says so in plain
                    // words, as for 'help'.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 476, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 13)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face has no windows to open.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 22 {
                    // 'terminal': the terminal window is
                    // opened and brought forward (Stone
                    // 38) — the console raising itself by
                    // its own word, with every kept answer
                    // still standing in it. The text face
                    // IS a console already, and says so in
                    // plain words.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        pset(pixdesk, 404, 1)
                        pset(pixdesk, 180, 10)
                        paintwindows(pixdesk, cellblock, notesblock)
                    } else {
                        ansrow = printline(ansrow, "This face is the terminal: every line is typed here.", pix, pixdesk)
                    }
                }
                if kind == 1 {
                    // A line of words answers it. A word
                    // the machine does not know is a
                    // refusal, and the refusal is sounded
                    // too (Stone 18) — one low note. The
                    // old word 'guest' is not a refusal: it
                    // is answered with the way to the new
                    // one, and no note sounds for it.
                    if cmd != "guest" {
                        refusenote()
                    }
                    ansrow = printline(ansrow, wordline(cmd), pix, pixdesk)
                }
            } else {
                if k == 15 {
                    // Tab: the next open window comes
                    // forward (Stone 14, grown at Stone 19
                    // to three windows, at Stone 20 to
                    // four, at Stone 21 to five, and at
                    // Stone 24 to six, and at Stone 29 to
                    // seven, and at Stone 30 to eight, and
                    // at Stone 31 to nine, and at Stone 38
                    // to ten, at Stone 41 to eleven, at
                    // Stone 42 to twelve, and at Stone 48
                    // to thirteen:
                    // welcome, feather, files, settings,
                    // editor, paint, verse, calculator,
                    // help, terminal, bible, shield,
                    // picture, and
                    // round again). The key types nothing,
                    // as it never has; on the text face,
                    // which has no windows, it simply passes.
                    if pix == 1 {
                        let cur = pget(pixdesk, 180)
                        let nxt = 0
                        let cand = cur
                        let tries = 0
                        while tries < 13 {
                            cand = cand + 1
                            if cand > 13 {
                                cand = 1
                            }
                            let open = 0
                            if cand == 1 {
                                open = pget(pixdesk, 172)
                            }
                            if cand == 2 {
                                open = pget(pixdesk, 176)
                            }
                            if cand == 3 {
                                open = pget(pixdesk, 188)
                            }
                            if cand == 4 {
                                open = pget(pixdesk, 208)
                            }
                            if cand == 5 {
